/*
 * Eduqas A-Level Geography (A110) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Keyed by the same topic ids as geography-eduqas-alevel.js (1.1 – 3.2).
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme }] } }
 * 40 questions per topic, matching the site's reference build,
 * Computer Science OCR A-Level (cs-ocr-alevel-practice.js).
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACED. Until 2026-09-28 this file held ten single MCQs in a
 * bare-array shape, which practice.html could not read at all — getPracticeData
 * returned the array, data.questions was undefined, and the page rendered
 * "0 questions available" with no mode cards. Those ten MCQs were moved into
 * geography-eduqas-alevel.js (the diagnostic bank) rather than discarded.
 *
 * TARIFFS AND BANDS. Every tariff and every band structure used here was taken
 * from the A110 mark schemes already held in papers-config.js (Components 1–3,
 * June 2022 and June 2023), not from memory:
 *     4 marks  — AO3, banded 4 / 2–3 / 1
 *     5 marks  — AO3, banded 4–5 / 2–3 / 1
 *     8 marks  — AO1, banded 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2
 *    10 marks  — AO1, banded 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3
 *    12 marks  — AO2, banded 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4
 *    20 marks  — AO2 (1c), banded 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5
 * Point-marked questions of 1–4 marks also appear on real papers and are used
 * here for definitional and process recall. 0 = not creditworthy in every grid.
 *
 * On the real papers the 4 and 5-mark AO3 questions are answered against a
 * printed figure. Since a self-marked practice bank has no resource booklet,
 * those questions carry their own data in the stem — figures, tables or survey
 * results — so the AO3 skill being tested (calculation, interpretation,
 * recognising what the data cannot show) is preserved.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Point-marked questions give one bullet per mark. Banded
 * questions give indicative content followed by the real band descriptors and
 * mark ranges for that tariff, marked on best fit across the whole response.
 * Indicative content is not exhaustive and not a checklist; credit other valid
 * approaches. Questions requiring a case study name one worked example and
 * state that any appropriate alternative is credited to the same standard.
 *
 * Student self-marks against the mark scheme: Full / Partial / Missed.
 */

const GEOGRAPHY_EDUQAS_ALEVEL_PRACTICE = {

  '1.1': {
    name: 'Coastal Landscapes',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Define the term sediment cell and explain why sediment cells are used as the basis for coastal management in England and Wales.', marks: 4, command: 'Define', markScheme: `• A sediment cell is a length of coastline, usually bounded by prominent headlands, within which the movement of sediment is largely self-contained (1)\n• Inputs, transfers and outputs of sediment operate largely independently of adjacent cells, so it functions as a closed system (1)\n• Management within one cell therefore has little effect on neighbouring cells, making the cell a logical planning unit (1)\n• England and Wales are divided into 11 sediment cells, each covered by a Shoreline Management Plan, so that decisions at one location account for knock-on effects further along the same cell (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between constructive and destructive waves.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Constructive waves have a low height, long wavelength and low frequency (typically 6–8 per minute) (1)\n• Their swash is stronger than their backwash, so net sediment movement is up the beach, building it and producing berms (1)\n• Destructive waves are high, steep, short in wavelength and more frequent (10–14 per minute) (1)\n• Their backwash is stronger than their swash, so sediment is removed from the beach, producing a steeper profile and offshore bars (1)` },

      { q: 'State what is meant by fetch and explain its influence on wave energy.', marks: 3, command: 'State', markScheme: `• Fetch is the uninterrupted distance of open water over which the wind blows (1)\n• The longer the fetch, the longer the wind acts on the water surface and the more energy is transferred to the waves (1)\n• Coasts with a long fetch therefore receive higher-energy waves — south-west England faces an Atlantic fetch of thousands of kilometres, while Holderness faces a North Sea fetch of only 500–800 km (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the process of longshore drift.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Waves approach the shore at an angle determined by the prevailing wind (1)\n• Swash carries sediment up the beach at that same oblique angle (1)\n• Backwash returns under gravity straight down the steepest gradient, perpendicular to the shoreline (1)\n• The repeated cycle moves sediment along the coast in a zig-zag path, in the direction of the dominant wave approach (1)` },

      { q: 'Describe the four processes of marine erosion.', marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Hydraulic action — the force of water compressing air into joints and cracks; the pressure released as the wave retreats widens them (1)\n• Abrasion (corrasion) — sediment carried by waves is thrown against the cliff face, wearing it away; the most effective process on most coasts (1)\n• Attrition — sediment particles collide with each other and are progressively reduced in size and rounded (1)\n• Solution (corrosion) — chemical dissolution of soluble rock, particularly limestone and chalk, by slightly acidic seawater (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between traction, saltation, suspension and solution as processes of sediment transport.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Traction — the largest particles are rolled or dragged along the sea bed by the current (1)\n• Saltation — smaller pebbles and coarse sand are bounced along the bed in a series of short hops (1)\n• Suspension — fine silt and clay are carried within the body of the water, producing turbid water (1)\n• Solution — dissolved material is carried in chemical form and is invisible, remaining in transport regardless of current velocity (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how a wave-cut platform is formed.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Erosion is concentrated between high and low tide, undercutting the cliff to form a wave-cut notch (1)\n• The notch deepens until the overhanging rock above is unsupported and collapses (1)\n• The cliff line retreats inland, and the process repeats (1)\n• The gently sloping bedrock left behind at the base of the retreating cliff is the wave-cut platform, which widens over time — eventually dissipating wave energy and slowing further erosion (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the sequence by which a crack in a headland develops into a stack and then a stump.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Hydraulic action and abrasion exploit a line of weakness such as a joint or fault, widening it into a cave (1)\n• Continued erosion from both sides of the headland enlarges the cave until it breaks through, forming an arch (1)\n• Weathering widens the arch from above and erosion undercuts its supports, until the roof collapses, leaving an isolated pillar — a stack (1)\n• The stack is undercut at its base and eventually collapses, leaving a stump visible only at low tide; Old Harry Rocks, Dorset, shows the full sequence (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain why spits typically develop a recurved (hooked) end.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A spit forms where longshore drift carries sediment past an abrupt change in coastline direction, such as a river mouth or bay, and deposits it in deeper, sheltered water (1)\n• The distal end is exposed to waves approaching from a secondary direction, different from the dominant one that built the spit (1)\n• These waves curve the end landwards, forming a recurved hook; a series of former hooks may be preserved behind the present one, recording past growth (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how a salt marsh develops in the sheltered water behind a spit.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The spit shelters the water behind it, reducing wave energy and current velocity (1)\n• Fine silt and clay carried in suspension are deposited, and flocculation occurs where fresh and salt water meet, causing clay particles to clump and settle faster (1)\n• Pioneer halophytes such as Salicornia and Spartina colonise the mudflats, their roots trapping and binding further sediment (1)\n• The marsh surface builds upwards, is flooded less often, and successive plant communities colonise as salinity falls — a halosere succession (1)` },

      { q: 'Distinguish between eustatic and isostatic sea-level change.', marks: 4, command: 'Distinguish', markScheme: `• Eustatic change is a change in the volume of water in the oceans, so it is global in effect (1)\n• Causes include the melting of ice sheets adding water, and thermal expansion of seawater as it warms (1)\n• Isostatic change is a local vertical movement of the land relative to the sea (1)\n• Its main cause is the loading and unloading of ice: northern Britain is still rebounding from the removal of the last ice sheet, while southern England is tilting downwards in compensation (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how a raised beach provides evidence of past sea-level change.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A raised beach is a former wave-cut platform and beach deposit now lying above the present high-tide line (1)\n• It is often backed by a degraded cliff line with relict caves, arches or notches, showing marine erosion at that level (1)\n• It indicates the land has risen relative to the sea — typically through isostatic rebound following deglaciation, as on the west coast of Scotland (1)` },

      { q: 'A stretch of coastline retreated 1.8 m per year between 1995 and 2005, and 2.7 m per year between 2005 and 2020. Calculate the total retreat over the full period and the percentage increase in the annual rate.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1995–2005 is 10 years: 10 × 1.8 = 18 m (1)\n• 2005–2020 is 15 years: 15 × 2.7 = 40.5 m (1)\n• Total retreat = 18 + 40.5 = 58.5 m (1)\n• Increase in rate = 2.7 − 1.8 = 0.9 m per year (1)\n• Percentage increase = 0.9 ÷ 1.8 × 100 = 50% (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both totals and the correct percentage with working shown; Band 2 partial calculation with a method error; Band 1 isolated correct figures.` },

      { q: 'A coastal parish records the following annual erosion rates: 2018 — 1.2 m; 2019 — 1.4 m; 2020 — 3.9 m; 2021 — 1.3 m; 2022 — 1.5 m. Calculate the mean and the median, and explain which better represents the typical rate.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mean = (1.2 + 1.4 + 3.9 + 1.3 + 1.5) ÷ 5 = 9.3 ÷ 5 = 1.86 m (1)\n• Ordered values: 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, 1.5, 3.9 (1)\n• Median = 1.4 m (1)\n• 2020 is an anomaly, most likely a major storm year, and it pulls the mean upwards (1)\n• The median better represents a typical year because it is resistant to that outlier; the mean is nevertheless the better figure for projecting total land loss over a long period, since exceptional years do occur (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both statistics correct and a reasoned choice referring to the anomaly.` },

      { q: 'The table shows the cost of coastal defence options per metre: rock armour £1,500; sea wall £5,000; beach nourishment £400; managed realignment £100. Calculate the cost of protecting a 2.5 km frontage with a sea wall, and state how many kilometres could be nourished for the same sum.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 2.5 km = 2500 m (1)\n• Sea wall: 2500 × £5,000 = £12,500,000 (1)\n• £12,500,000 ÷ £400 per metre = 31,250 m (1)\n• = 31.25 km of beach nourishment (1)\n• Comparison point: over 12 times the length, though nourishment must be repeated every few years while a sea wall lasts decades, so whole-life cost must be compared rather than capital cost alone (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations correct with the comparison made rather than the figures merely stated.` },

      { q: 'Beach profile measurements give the following gradients: upper beach 11°, mid beach 6°, lower beach 2°. Sediment size falls from 45 mm at the top to 4 mm at the low-water mark. Analyse what these data suggest about the processes operating.', marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `• The profile steepens landwards, and sediment size decreases seawards — the two patterns are linked (1)\n• Coarse material is thrown to the top of the beach by swash during high-energy conditions and is too large to be returned by backwash, producing a steep upper beach (1)\n• Percolation through coarse sediment reduces backwash volume, allowing the steep gradient to be maintained (1)\n• Finer material is more easily transported by backwash and is carried seawards, giving the gentle lower beach (1)\n• The overall pattern suggests a mixed sand-and-shingle beach dominated by constructive waves, since a destructive-wave regime would produce a flatter profile with an offshore bar (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 analyses the relationship between the two variables rather than describing each separately.` },

      { q: 'Sea level at a tide gauge rose 1.4 mm per year from 1900 to 1990, and 3.6 mm per year from 1990 to 2020. Calculate the total rise over the whole period and comment on what the change in rate suggests.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1900–1990 is 90 years: 90 × 1.4 = 126 mm (1)\n• 1990–2020 is 30 years: 30 × 3.6 = 108 mm (1)\n• Total = 234 mm, or 23.4 cm (1)\n• The rate has more than doubled, so the rise is accelerating rather than linear (1)\n• This is consistent with increased thermal expansion and accelerating ice-sheet loss; it also means linear extrapolation of the historic rate would substantially underestimate future rise, which matters for defence design life (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct totals plus interpretation of the acceleration, not description alone.` },

      { q: 'A survey finds that 62% of residents in a coastal village support a sea wall, 24% support managed realignment and 14% are undecided. The village has 850 residents. Calculate the number supporting each option and suggest one limitation of using this survey to decide policy.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Sea wall: 0.62 × 850 = 527 residents (1)\n• Managed realignment: 0.24 × 850 = 204 (1)\n• Undecided: 0.14 × 850 = 119 (accept 527 + 204 + 119 = 850) (1)\n• Limitation: residents are not a neutral party — those whose property is at risk have a direct financial interest, so the result measures self-interest as much as judgement (1)\n• Also accept: the survey excludes taxpayers elsewhere who fund the scheme, excludes those downdrift who would be affected, and offers no information on cost, so respondents are not choosing between realistic alternatives (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires accurate calculation and a developed limitation.` },

      { q: 'Explain how geology influences the rate at which a coastline retreats.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Lithology — rock strength and resistance. Unconsolidated boulder clay at Holderness retreats at 1–2 m per year, while the granite of Land’s End retreats at a few millimetres.\n• Solubility — limestone and chalk are vulnerable to solution as well as mechanical erosion.\n• Structure — the density and orientation of joints, faults and bedding planes provides lines of weakness that hydraulic action exploits; heavily jointed rock erodes far faster than massive rock of the same strength.\n• Dip — seaward-dipping strata produce unstable cliffs prone to rock slides; landward-dipping strata produce more stable, steeper cliffs.\n• Concordant coasts, where strata run parallel to the coast, give relatively straight coastlines; discordant coasts, where strata run at right angles, give alternating headlands and bays through differential erosion — Swanage Bay and the Purbeck coast illustrate both.\n• Permeability — permeable rock allows water to percolate and raises pore-water pressure, promoting mass movement rather than marine erosion alone.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy). Indicative content is not exhaustive.\n• Band 3 (6–8): thorough, accurate explanation covering several distinct geological controls, with correct terminology and supporting examples, showing how each affects rate rather than simply listing rock types.\n• Band 2 (3–5): sound knowledge of some controls with partial explanation; a series of valid but loosely connected points, or examples asserted without development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): limited, generalised statements such as "hard rock erodes slowly", with little accurate terminology.` },

      { q: 'Explain how sub-aerial processes contribute to cliff retreat.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Sub-aerial processes operate on the cliff face from above and behind, in contrast to marine processes attacking the base.\n• Freeze-thaw weathering — water in joints expands by around 9% on freezing, widening cracks and loosening blocks; significant where temperatures oscillate around 0°C.\n• Salt crystallisation — evaporating spray leaves salt crystals that grow in pores, exerting pressure that disintegrates rock.\n• Chemical weathering — carbonation of limestone by weakly acidic rainwater; hydrolysis and oxidation weakening mineral structure.\n• Biological weathering — root action prising joints apart, burrowing organisms, and boring molluscs on the platform.\n• Mass movement — rockfall from undercut, weathered faces; slumping (rotational slip) where permeable rock overlies impermeable clay and pore-water pressure rises after heavy rain, as along the Holderness and Isle of Wight coasts; soil creep and mudflows on weaker material.\n• Interaction: weathering weakens material so marine erosion removes it more readily, and marine removal of the debris at the cliff foot re-exposes the face, so the two sets of processes reinforce each other rather than acting independently.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): accurate coverage of several distinct processes with correct terminology, and explicit linkage to cliff retreat including the interaction with marine processes.\n• Band 2 (3–5): describes some processes accurately but with limited explanation of their contribution to retreat.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated, generalised comments about weather or rain damaging cliffs.` },

      { q: 'Explain how coastal landforms of deposition develop along a drift-aligned coastline.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• On a drift-aligned coast, waves approach consistently obliquely, so longshore drift dominates and sediment moves along the shore rather than on and offshore.\n• Spits — form where the coastline changes direction; drift carries sediment into deeper, sheltered water where velocity falls and deposition occurs, extending the feature; secondary wave directions recurve the distal end; salt marsh develops in the sheltered water behind.\n• Bars — where a spit extends across a bay and joins the far side, sealing a lagoon behind it (Slapton Ley).\n• Tombolos — where a spit connects the mainland to an island (Chesil Beach to Portland).\n• Cuspate forelands — where drift operates from two directions, sediment accumulates in a triangular projection (Dungeness).\n• Offshore bars and barrier islands — sediment deposited where waves break offshore.\n• Requirements common to all: a sediment supply, an energy gradient so that transport capacity falls, and time; removal of the supply (for example by updrift defences) halts growth and leads to erosion of the landform.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): explains formation sequences accurately for more than one landform, uses correct terminology, and supports with located examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): describes landforms with partial explanation of processes, or explains one landform well with little breadth.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names landforms with little or no process explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain how human activity can increase the rate of coastal erosion.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Terminal groyne syndrome — groynes trap drift sediment updrift, starving the beach downdrift so it thins and no longer dissipates wave energy; erosion accelerates immediately beyond the defended section. Mappleton on the Holderness coast is the standard example.\n• Hard defences reflect rather than absorb wave energy, causing scour at the toe of the wall and lowering the beach in front of it, which eventually undermines the structure itself.\n• Dredging of offshore sediment and aggregate extraction remove material from the cell, reducing supply to beaches.\n• River management — dams and channelisation trap sediment inland, cutting the fluvial supply that nourishes deltas and beaches.\n• Building on cliff tops adds weight and, with poor drainage or leaking services, raises pore-water pressure, promoting slumping.\n• Removal of vegetation and trampling of dunes destroys the root systems that bind sediment, allowing blowouts.\n• Global contribution — greenhouse gas emissions drive sea-level rise and increased storminess, raising erosion rates everywhere.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several distinct mechanisms explained accurately with the causal chain made explicit and located support.\n• Band 2 (3–5): valid mechanisms identified with partial explanation, or reliance on a single example.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised assertions that people cause erosion, with no mechanism.` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics and formation of a psammosere (sand dune succession).', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Requirements: a large tidal range exposing sand at low tide, a prevailing onshore wind, an obstacle to trap sand, and an abundant sediment supply.\n• Embryo dunes — sand accumulates around debris; colonised by pioneers such as sea rocket and sea couch grass tolerant of salt and burial.\n• Fore dunes — marram grass colonises, its deep roots binding sand and its growth responding to burial, so dunes build rapidly; humus begins to accumulate.\n• Yellow dunes — still mobile with much bare sand visible; increasing organic matter and falling salinity.\n• Grey dunes — fixed by a continuous vegetation mat; humus gives a grey colour, soil has developed, greater species diversity, less exposure.\n• Dune slacks — hollows where the water table reaches the surface, supporting moisture-loving species such as creeping willow.\n• Climax woodland where succession is undisturbed, typically birch and pine.\n• Trend across the sere: increasing soil depth, humus, moisture retention and species diversity; decreasing salinity, wind speed and pH.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): accurate sequence with named species and the environmental gradients explained, not just listed.\n• Band 2 (3–5): correct sequence with partial explanation, limited use of species names or gradients.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about sand dunes and plants.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and coastal consequences of rising sea level.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Thermal expansion — warming seawater expands, historically the largest single contributor.\n• Melting land ice — mountain glaciers, and the Greenland and West Antarctic ice sheets, add water to the ocean; melting sea ice does not raise sea level because it already displaces its own weight.\n• Local isostatic subsidence adds to the relative rise in some places, such as south-east England, while rebound offsets it in Scotland — so relative sea-level change varies regionally even where the eustatic signal is global.\n• Consequences: permanent inundation of low-lying land, including deltas and small island states; increased frequency of storm-surge flooding as a given surge starts from a higher base.\n• Accelerated erosion as waves reach further up the profile and attack cliffs previously above wave reach.\n• Saltwater intrusion into aquifers and soils, damaging agriculture and water supply.\n• Coastal squeeze — intertidal habitats such as salt marsh cannot migrate landward where fixed defences stand behind them, so they are lost along with the natural protection they provide.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): accurate treatment of both causes and consequences, correct distinction between eustatic and isostatic, supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes or consequences covered adequately but the other thinly, or the eustatic/isostatic distinction confused.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised statements about ice melting and flooding.` },

      { q: 'Explain the operation of the coastal system in terms of inputs, stores, transfers and outputs.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The coast is treated as an open system exchanging energy and matter with its surroundings.\n• Inputs: energy from waves, wind, tides and currents; sediment from cliff erosion, rivers, offshore sources and longshore drift from adjacent cells.\n• Stores (components): beaches, dunes, spits, bars, offshore bars, salt marsh, and the nearshore sediment body.\n• Transfers (flows): longshore drift, swash and backwash, mass movement delivering material to the cliff foot, aeolian transport landward into dunes, and tidal currents.\n• Outputs: sediment lost offshore beyond the closure depth, transported out of the cell, or deposited above the reach of waves; energy dissipated by friction and breaking.\n• Sediment budget: where inputs exceed outputs there is a positive budget and accretion; where outputs exceed inputs the budget is negative and erosion results.\n• Dynamic equilibrium — the system adjusts toward a balance, so an intervention such as a groyne or a dredging operation triggers compensating change elsewhere.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): systems terminology used accurately throughout, with the sediment budget and feedback explained rather than a list of examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): correct categories with limited explanation of how the system responds to change.\n• Band 1 (1–2): a few correct examples placed in categories, with no systems reasoning.` },

      { q: 'Explain the differences between hard and soft engineering approaches to coastal management.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Hard engineering uses built structures to resist or control natural processes: sea walls, groynes, rock armour (rip-rap), gabions, revetments, breakwaters.\n• Characteristics: high capital cost, immediate and visible protection, long design life, but they interrupt natural processes and frequently transfer the problem elsewhere (terminal groyne syndrome); ongoing maintenance costs are substantial and failure can be sudden.\n• Soft engineering works with natural processes: beach nourishment, dune stabilisation and replanting, managed realignment, cliff regrading and drainage, do-nothing.\n• Characteristics: lower capital cost, more sustainable and better for habitat and landscape, but protection is less certain, benefits accrue slowly, and repeated intervention (renourishment every few years) is often required.\n• Managed realignment deliberately allows flooding of low-value land to recreate salt marsh, which dissipates wave energy and protects the new defence line behind — Medmerry in West Sussex is the standard UK example.\n• Choice depends on the value of what is protected, the cost-benefit ratio required for funding, and local political acceptability, so the two approaches are increasingly combined.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): accurate contrast across cost, effectiveness, sustainability and side-effects, with named techniques and examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): lists techniques under each heading with limited comparison.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names one or two techniques with no clear distinction drawn.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the concept of the sediment cell helps explain the consequences of coastal defence schemes.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• A sediment cell is largely closed, so sediment held back at one point is sediment unavailable further along the same cell — the total budget is conserved.\n• Groynes trap drift sediment updrift, building the beach there; the downdrift beach receives less, thins, and provides less wave-energy dissipation, so erosion accelerates.\n• Hence protection at one settlement imposes costs on another within the same cell — Mappleton’s rock groynes protected the village while erosion downdrift increased sharply.\n• Cliff protection removes an input: if cliffs feed beaches, fixing them starves the cell of sediment, so defending one place reduces the natural defence of others.\n• Consequences are therefore predictable in principle if the cell is understood, which is why Shoreline Management Plans are drawn at cell and sub-cell scale rather than by local authority boundary.\n• It also explains the equity problem: those bearing the cost are usually not those receiving the benefit, and are often not consulted — a governance as much as a physical issue.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): applies the closed-system logic explicitly to defence consequences, with an accurate located example.\n• Band 2 (3–5): defines the sediment cell and identifies some consequences without fully connecting the two.\n• Band 1 (1–2): describes the sediment cell or a defence with no link between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics of a concordant and a discordant coastline, and the landforms associated with each.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Concordant (Pacific) coasts — strata run parallel to the coastline. Resistant rock forms a continuous barrier, so the coast is relatively straight and erosion is slow while that barrier remains intact.\n• Where the sea breaches the resistant outer band, it erodes the softer rock behind more rapidly, producing a cove with a narrow entrance — Lulworth Cove, Dorset, where the sea breached Portland limestone to reach softer clays and sands behind.\n• Continued erosion can remove the outer barrier entirely, leaving a wide bay such as Worbarrow Bay.\n• Dalmatian coasts — a drowned concordant coastline producing long offshore islands parallel to the shore, as along the Croatian coast.\n• Discordant (Atlantic) coasts — strata run at right angles to the coastline, so bands of differing resistance are exposed alternately to the sea.\n• Differential erosion produces headlands of resistant rock and bays of less resistant rock — the Purbeck coast, with chalk at Ballard Point and clay in Swanage Bay.\n• Wave refraction concentrates energy on headlands and disperses it in bays, so headlands are attacked (caves, arches, stacks, stumps at Old Harry) while sediment accumulates in bays, forming beaches.\n• Over time refraction tends to straighten the coast, as headlands erode faster than bays fill — the system moves toward equilibrium.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (7–10): thorough, accurate explanation of both coastline types with associated landforms, correct terminology, well-chosen located examples, and reference to wave refraction and differential erosion.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound knowledge of both types with partial explanation of landform development, or one type covered well and the other superficially.\n• Band 1 (1–3): limited statements distinguishing the two, few or no examples and little process detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain the physical and human factors that determine the level of coastal flood risk in a given location.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical factors:\n• Elevation and gradient — low-lying, gently sloping land such as the Somerset Levels or the Netherlands floods extensively for a small rise in water level.\n• Subsidence, whether isostatic or caused by groundwater and hydrocarbon extraction, increases relative sea level (Jakarta, the Mississippi delta).\n• Storm surge potential — funnel-shaped coastlines and shallow seas amplify surges, as in the southern North Sea and the Bay of Bengal.\n• Tidal range and the coincidence of surge with high spring tide.\n• Presence of natural buffers — mangroves, salt marsh, dunes and coral reefs dissipate wave energy.\n• Local climate — exposure to tropical cyclones or mid-latitude depressions.\n\nIndicative content — human factors:\n• Population density and the value of assets on the floodplain — risk combines hazard with exposure, so an identical surge has very different consequences in Bangladesh and the Netherlands.\n• Wealth and governance — capacity to build and maintain defences, to forecast and to evacuate.\n• Removal of natural buffers through mangrove clearance or dune development.\n• Building on the floodplain and reliance on defences, which encourages further development behind them — the levee effect.\n• Preparedness: warning systems, building codes, insurance, and emergency planning.\n• Risk is best expressed as the product of hazard, vulnerability and exposure, so the physical and human factors are multiplicative rather than simply additive.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers both physical and human factors thoroughly and accurately, with examples, and explains how they interact rather than listing them.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound coverage but unbalanced between physical and human, or limited explanation of interaction.\n• Band 1 (1–3): isolated factors, largely descriptive.` },

      { q: 'Explain the formation of the landforms found along a coastline of cliff retreat, from wave-cut notch to offshore stump.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Marine erosion concentrates between high and low water, where wave energy is applied most frequently.\n• Hydraulic action compresses air in joints; abrasion uses sediment as tools; solution acts on soluble rock. A wave-cut notch develops at the cliff foot.\n• The notch deepens, leaving an unsupported overhang that fails under gravity; the cliff retreats parallel to itself.\n• Repeated retreat leaves a gently seaward-sloping wave-cut platform, typically under 0.5 km wide because a wider platform dissipates wave energy before it reaches the cliff — a negative feedback that limits further retreat.\n• On headlands, lines of weakness are exploited: crack → cave → arch (where caves from both sides meet) → stack (roof collapse) → stump (undercutting of the stack).\n• Sub-aerial processes act simultaneously: freeze-thaw, salt weathering and mass movement weaken and lower the cliff face, so cliff profile reflects the balance between basal erosion and sub-aerial retreat — rapid basal erosion gives steep cliffs, dominant sub-aerial processes give lower-angle ones.\n• Located support: Old Harry Rocks (Dorset) for the stack sequence; Flamborough Head for caves and arches in chalk.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): accurate sequential explanation with correct process terminology, recognition of the interaction of marine and sub-aerial processes, and located examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): correct sequence with partial process explanation, or marine processes only.\n• Band 1 (1–3): names landforms with little explanation of formation.` },

      { q: 'Explain how coastal landscapes in the UK have been affected by both physical processes and human management over the past century.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical:\n• Continued marine erosion on soft-rock coasts — Holderness retreating 1–2 m per year, the fastest in Europe, having lost some 29 villages since Roman times.\n• Relative sea-level rise, faster in the south and east because of isostatic tilting, increasing both inundation and erosion.\n• Increased storminess and notable surge events, particularly the 1953 North Sea flood and the 2013–14 winter storms.\n• Ongoing deposition building spits and marshes elsewhere — Spurn Head, Blakeney Point.\n\nIndicative content — human:\n• Extensive hard engineering after 1953 — sea walls and groynes along much of the east and south coast, fixing the position of the shoreline.\n• Consequential downdrift starvation and accelerated erosion beyond defended frontages.\n• Shift in policy from "hold the line" everywhere toward Shoreline Management Plans with four options, including managed realignment and no active intervention, on cost-benefit grounds.\n• Managed realignment schemes — Medmerry (2013), the largest coastal realignment in Europe, creating 183 ha of intertidal habitat while reducing flood risk to the village.\n• Coastal squeeze — fixed defences prevent landward migration of salt marsh as sea level rises, so intertidal habitat has been lost.\n• Designation and protection — SSSIs, Heritage Coasts and the Jurassic Coast World Heritage Site constraining development.\n• Synthesis: the present landscape is a product of both, and the two now interact — management decisions determine where physical processes are allowed to operate.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): balanced, accurate treatment of physical and human influences with specific UK examples and dates, and explicit recognition of their interaction.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound coverage of one side with the other thinner, or examples asserted rather than developed.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generalised statements about erosion and sea defences.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and effects of coastal squeeze.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Intertidal habitats such as salt marsh and mudflat naturally migrate landward as sea level rises, maintaining their width.\n• Where a fixed defence — sea wall, embankment or road — stands immediately behind them, that migration is blocked.\n• The seaward edge continues to erode while the landward edge cannot advance, so the habitat is progressively narrowed and eventually eliminated.\n• Accelerating sea-level rise increases the rate; sediment starvation from upstream damming or updrift defences removes the accretion that might otherwise keep pace.\n\nIndicative content — effects:\n• Loss of internationally designated habitat and the species dependent on it — overwintering wading birds in UK estuaries protected under the Ramsar Convention.\n• Loss of the natural wave-energy dissipation the marsh provided, so wave energy reaching the defence increases and the defence must be raised or strengthened — a rising maintenance cost.\n• Loss of ecosystem services: nursery grounds for fisheries, carbon sequestration in marsh sediment, water filtration.\n• Legal obligation to compensate for designated habitat lost, which is a principal driver of managed realignment schemes such as Medmerry and Wallasea Island.\n• Synthesis: coastal squeeze is the clearest illustration that holding the line has ecological as well as financial costs, and that they accumulate rather than being one-off.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): explains the mechanism accurately and covers ecological, economic and management effects with examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): mechanism understood but effects limited, or effects listed with the cause only partly explained.\n• Band 1 (1–3): isolated statements about habitat loss.` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between swash-aligned and drift-aligned beaches.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `\u2022 On a swash-aligned beach, waves approach parallel to the shore, so swash and backwash move sediment directly up and down the beach (1)\n\u2022 There is little net longshore movement, and the beach plan form is in equilibrium with wave approach \u2014 typically a smooth, curved bay-head beach (1)\n\u2022 On a drift-aligned beach, waves approach consistently obliquely, so longshore drift moves sediment along the coast (1)\n\u2022 Sediment is transported away from the updrift end and accumulates downdrift, producing elongated features such as spits; drift-aligned beaches are therefore far more sensitive to interruption of the sediment supply (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how wave refraction affects the distribution of energy along an indented coastline.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n\u2022 As waves approach shallowing water they slow through friction with the sea bed; the shallower the water, the greater the retardation.\n\u2022 Water is shallower off headlands than in adjacent bays, so the section of the wave crest over the headland slows first while the section in the bay continues at speed.\n\u2022 The wave crest bends (refracts) to become more parallel to the contours of the coastline.\n\u2022 Orthogonals (lines drawn perpendicular to the wave crest) converge on headlands, concentrating energy there, and diverge in bays, dispersing it.\n\u2022 Consequence at headlands: high energy, erosional landforms \u2014 caves, arches, stacks \u2014 and little sediment retained.\n\u2022 Consequence in bays: low energy, deposition, and beach accumulation; longshore drift within the bay redistributes sediment toward a swash-aligned equilibrium form.\n\u2022 Longer-term effect: headlands erode faster than bays, so refraction tends to straighten the coastline over time \u2014 a negative feedback moving the system toward equilibrium.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6\u20138 / 3\u20135 / 1\u20132).\n\u2022 Band 3 (6\u20138): accurate explanation of the mechanism using correct terminology (orthogonals, convergence, divergence) and its consequences for landforms in both settings.\n\u2022 Band 2 (3\u20135): states that energy concentrates on headlands with partial explanation of why.\n\u2022 Band 1 (1\u20132): isolated statements about waves hitting headlands.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which hard engineering is an effective response to coastal erosion.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — effective:\n• Provides immediate, reliable protection where asset values are high; a sea wall at a town or port can be justified many times over on cost-benefit grounds.\n• Long design life (30–80 years for a sea wall), giving certainty that supports investment and insurance behind the line.\n• Rock armour is comparatively cheap, absorbs rather than reflects energy, and can be added incrementally.\n• Groynes demonstrably widen beaches updrift, and a wider beach dissipates energy, so the benefit is partly self-reinforcing.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Very high capital cost (£5,000+ per metre for a sea wall), so it cannot be applied everywhere; funding rules mean low-value coasts are not defended, which is itself contentious.\n• Transfers the problem: terminal groyne syndrome starves downdrift frontages — Mappleton protected while erosion downdrift accelerated.\n• Reflective structures cause toe scour and beach lowering, undermining the defence and requiring further expenditure.\n• Visually intrusive and can damage habitat and amenity, reducing the tourism value the defence was partly intended to protect.\n• Sea-level rise means structures designed for past conditions become undersized, so whole-life costs rise and the design assumption is now a moving target.\n• Locks in future spending — once a community is defended it is politically very difficult to withdraw.\n\nJudgement: effectiveness depends on what is being protected and over what timescale; hard engineering is defensible at high-value, high-density frontages but not as a general strategy, and is increasingly used in combination with soft approaches.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained, balanced assessment using accurate evidence and examples, with criteria for effectiveness made explicit and a supported judgement reached.\n• Band 2 (5–8): some assessment with evidence on both sides, but the judgement asserted rather than argued, or the criteria left implicit.\n• Band 1 (1–4): descriptive account of techniques with little or no assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that managed realignment is the most sustainable approach to coastal management in the UK.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Works with natural processes rather than resisting them, so it does not require ever-increasing intervention as sea level rises.\n• Creates intertidal habitat that dissipates wave energy, reducing the height and cost of the defence behind — protection that improves over time rather than degrading.\n• Delivers the habitat compensation required by law when designated sites are lost to coastal squeeze.\n• Carbon sequestration in marsh sediment, and nursery grounds supporting fisheries.\n• Lower whole-life cost: Medmerry cost around £28m but protects 348 properties and the wastewater treatment works, avoiding repeated emergency repairs.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Requires land to give up, which means agricultural land lost and, in some schemes, property and community displacement — socially and politically very difficult.\n• Not viable where high-value assets sit immediately at the shoreline; a city or port cannot be realigned.\n• Outcomes are uncertain and slow: the habitat may take decades to establish and may not develop as modelled.\n• Compensation and land purchase costs can be high, and the process is lengthy.\n• "Sustainable" is contested — environmentally sustainable but not necessarily socially sustainable for the community that loses land.\n\nJudgement: sustainable in the environmental and long-run economic sense and clearly preferable on undeveloped or low-value coasts, but it cannot be a universal strategy; a defensible answer distinguishes the dimensions of sustainability rather than treating the term as a single measure.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained evaluation engaging directly with "most sustainable", distinguishing environmental, economic and social sustainability, supported by accurate located evidence and reaching a clear judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): assesses advantages and disadvantages with some evidence; engagement with sustainability partial.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes managed realignment with little or no evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Assess the relative importance of physical and human factors in explaining rates of coastal erosion.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical factors:\n• Geology is the dominant control on the range of rates observed: boulder clay at Holderness retreats 1–2 m per year against millimetres for granite — three orders of magnitude, far exceeding any human effect.\n• Wave energy and fetch, storm frequency, tidal range and the presence of a protective beach.\n• Sub-aerial processes and mass movement, particularly where permeable rock overlies impermeable clay.\n\nIndicative content — human factors:\n• Sediment starvation from groynes and updrift defences, which can double local rates — the downdrift consequence of Mappleton.\n• Dredging, aggregate extraction and river damming reducing sediment supply to the cell.\n• Cliff-top development and drainage raising pore-water pressure and triggering slumping.\n• Vegetation removal and dune trampling.\n• Global emissions driving sea-level rise and storminess — a human factor operating everywhere, which blurs the distinction.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Physical factors set the potential range of rates; human factors modify them within that range, so the two operate at different scales rather than competing.\n• Human factors are decisive in explaining variation over short distances and short timescales — why erosion is rapid here and not 200 m away.\n• Scale and timescale determine the answer: over geological time and at regional scale, geology dominates; over decades at a single site, management usually explains the change.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained comparative assessment that resolves the question by scale or timescale rather than simply listing both sets of factors, with accurate supporting evidence.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sets covered with some comparison; judgement present but asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists factors with little or no assessment of relative importance.` },

      { q: 'Assess the usefulness of the sediment cell concept to those responsible for managing the coast.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — useful:\n• Provides a physically meaningful planning unit, so decisions are taken at the scale at which processes actually operate rather than by administrative boundary — the basis of the 11 English and Welsh Shoreline Management Plans.\n• Makes downdrift consequences predictable, so the cost of a scheme to neighbouring frontages can be anticipated rather than discovered.\n• Supports a sediment budget approach, identifying whether a frontage is in surplus or deficit and therefore what kind of intervention could work.\n• Forces coordination between authorities that would otherwise act independently.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Cells are not perfectly closed — sediment leaks between them, particularly fine material, so the assumption is an approximation.\n• Boundaries are not fixed: they shift with changing wave direction and sea level, so a cell defined today may not hold in 50 years.\n• Cells are large and internally varied, so sub-cells and management units are needed in practice, adding complexity.\n• The concept describes the physical system but says nothing about who pays, who decides, or how to weigh a village against a nature reserve — the hardest problems in coastal management are political and economic.\n• Data on sediment fluxes is often poor, so the budget is estimated rather than measured, limiting confidence in predictions.\n\nJudgement: a necessary but not sufficient tool — indispensable for understanding physical consequences and for setting the scale of planning, but it does not by itself determine decisions.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): evaluates usefulness against clear criteria, recognises both the physical value and the limits of a physical concept in a policy context, and reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): identifies strengths and weaknesses with partial evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): explains what a sediment cell is with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that coastal landscapes are shaped more by marine processes than by any other factor. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — the case for marine processes:\n• Erosional landforms — notches, wave-cut platforms, caves, arches, stacks, stumps — are produced directly by hydraulic action, abrasion and solution, and cannot form without wave energy.\n• Depositional landforms — spits, bars, tombolos, cuspate forelands — are produced by longshore drift and deposition, again driven by waves.\n• Wave refraction redistributes energy along the coast, explaining why headlands erode and bays accumulate sediment, and therefore the plan form of the coastline.\n• Marine processes operate constantly, twice daily with the tide, so their cumulative effect is large even where individual events are modest.\n\nIndicative content — the case for other factors:\n• Geology determines the rate and the form: identical wave energy produces 1–2 m per year of retreat in boulder clay and millimetres in granite; structure and dip control cliff profile; concordant and discordant arrangement controls whether headlands and bays form at all.\n• Sub-aerial processes — freeze-thaw, salt crystallisation, mass movement — supply much of the material removed and shape the cliff profile above the wave zone; slumping at Holderness delivers more material than wave erosion alone could remove.\n• Sea-level change determines where marine processes operate: raised beaches and rias are landforms of changing base level, not of wave action at present levels.\n• Human management fixes the shoreline over much of the developed coast, so in those places the landscape is a product of engineering decisions.\n• Ecological processes build landforms directly — salt marsh and dune systems depend on vegetation trapping and binding sediment, and coral and mangrove coasts are biologically constructed.\n• Climate and weather control the frequency of high-energy events; a single storm surge can achieve more change than years of ordinary conditions.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The factors are not independent: marine processes are the agent, but geology sets the resistance, sub-aerial processes prepare the material, and sea level positions the attack. Asking which matters "more" requires specifying scale and timescale.\n• At the scale of an individual landform over years, marine processes usually dominate; at the scale of a coastline over millennia, geology and sea-level change dominate; on developed coasts over recent decades, human management often dominates.\n• A strong answer establishes criteria and sustains a line of reasoning rather than alternating between factors.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5; 0 = not creditworthy). Indicative content is not prescriptive; credit other valid approaches.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent line of reasoning that engages directly with "more than any other factor"; a wide range of accurate, well-selected evidence and located examples; the interdependence of factors recognised and resolved by scale or timescale; a convincing, fully supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation covering several factors with accurate support and a clear judgement, though the reasoning may be less sustained or the resolution less developed.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes several factors with some attempt at evaluation; examples present but generalised; the judgement asserted rather than argued.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of coastal processes or landforms, with little evaluation and few or no examples.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the success of coastal management at one named location you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named location. Medmerry, West Sussex, is used below; credit any appropriate case study (Holderness, Lyme Regis, the Netherlands Delta Works, Sandbanks) assessed to the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — the scheme:\n• Medmerry, completed 2013, the largest open-coast managed realignment in Europe; a 2 km shingle bank that had required annual emergency repair was deliberately breached and 7 km of new embankment built inland.\n• Cost approximately £28m, funded by the Environment Agency.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Flood risk to 348 properties, the B2145 road and the Sidlesham wastewater treatment works substantially reduced; the area had flooded repeatedly, most seriously in 2008.\n• 183 ha of intertidal habitat created, meeting the legal obligation to compensate for habitat lost to coastal squeeze elsewhere in the Solent.\n• Ongoing maintenance cost sharply reduced — the annual shingle recycling is no longer required, so the scheme improves rather than degrades over time.\n• New public access, cycle routes and an RSPB reserve delivering recreational and tourism benefit.\n• The marsh dissipates wave energy, so the new defence line faces lower loading than the old one.\n\nIndicative content — limitations and counter-arguments:\n• Around 300 ha of agricultural land deliberately given up, a permanent loss to the farming businesses concerned.\n• High capital cost, and a lengthy consultation and compensation process.\n• Local opposition during planning: loss of land and perceived loss of a familiar landscape; realignment is often read as abandonment.\n• Long-term performance under accelerating sea-level rise is not yet proven; the habitat is still developing.\n• Not transferable: it succeeded because the land behind was low-value farmland, which is not the case at most threatened frontages.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Success must be judged against stated criteria — flood risk reduction, cost-effectiveness, habitat creation, social acceptability, sustainability — and the scheme performs differently against each.\n• On flood protection, cost and habitat it is a clear success; on social acceptability it was contested; on long-term sustainability the evidence is promising but incomplete.\n• Also relevant: success for whom, and over what period — the farmers who lost land and the residents who gained protection would answer differently.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria, detailed and accurate place-specific evidence including figures, genuine consideration of limitations and of whose interests are served, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate case-study detail and a clear judgement, though criteria may be implicit or one dimension underdeveloped.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the scheme with some evaluative comment; place detail generalised, judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of coastal management with little or no specific locational detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.2': {
    name: 'Glaciated Landscapes',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain the difference between accumulation and ablation, and state what is meant by the glacial budget.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Accumulation is the addition of mass to a glacier, mainly by snowfall, but also by avalanche and windblown snow (1)\n• Ablation is the loss of mass, by melting, sublimation, evaporation and calving (1)\n• The glacial budget (mass balance) is the difference between the two over a year (1)\n• A positive budget means the glacier advances, a negative budget means it retreats, and a balanced budget means the snout is stationary although ice continues to move through it (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between the zone of accumulation and the zone of ablation, and state where the equilibrium line lies.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The zone of accumulation is the upper part of the glacier where annual gains exceed losses, so ice thickens (1)\n• The zone of ablation is the lower part where losses exceed gains, so ice thins (1)\n• The equilibrium line separates them, where accumulation exactly equals ablation; it migrates up-glacier in warmer years, which is why its position is monitored as an indicator of glacier health (1)` },

      { q: 'Describe the process by which snow is transformed into glacial ice.', marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Fresh snow falls as low-density crystals with a high proportion of trapped air (around 0.05 g/cm³) (1)\n• Successive falls compress the snow beneath, and partial melting and refreezing round the crystals, forming névé or firn (1)\n• Continued burial expels air and increases density; individual crystals grow larger (1)\n• At a density of about 0.83–0.9 g/cm³ the air passages seal and the mass becomes impermeable glacial ice, a process taking decades in temperate glaciers and centuries in polar ones (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a warm-based (temperate) and a cold-based (polar) glacier.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A warm-based glacier has ice at or close to pressure melting point at its base, so meltwater is present (1)\n• That meltwater lubricates the bed, allowing basal sliding, so the glacier moves relatively quickly and erodes actively (1)\n• A cold-based glacier is frozen to its bed, with temperatures well below pressure melting point and no basal water (1)\n• Movement is almost entirely by internal deformation, so it is very slow and erosion is minimal — which is why much of central Antarctica preserves ancient landscapes beneath the ice (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the processes of basal sliding and internal deformation.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Basal sliding is the movement of the whole ice mass over its bed, lubricated by a thin film of meltwater (1)\n• It includes regelation — pressure melting on the upstream side of an obstacle and refreezing on the downstream side — and enhanced creep around larger obstacles (1)\n• Internal deformation is movement within the ice itself, as ice crystals slip past one another under the weight of overlying ice (1)\n• It is the dominant mechanism in cold-based glaciers and in the upper, brittle zone the ice fractures instead, forming crevasses (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the processes of plucking and abrasion.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Plucking occurs where meltwater at the base penetrates joints in the bedrock and refreezes, bonding the ice to blocks of rock (1)\n• As the glacier moves forward these blocks are torn away, producing the steep, jagged lee slopes typical of roches moutonnées and corrie backwalls (1)\n• Abrasion occurs where rock debris embedded in the base of the ice is dragged across the bedrock (1)\n• It scours and polishes the surface, producing striations whose orientation records the direction of ice movement; its effectiveness depends on debris hardness, concentration and ice velocity (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how a corrie (cirque) is formed.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Snow accumulates in a north- or north-east-facing hollow, sheltered from sun and prevailing wind, and compacts into ice (1)\n• Freeze-thaw weathering and plucking steepen the backwall, while abrasion deepens the floor (1)\n• Rotational movement of the ice deepens the hollow at its centre, leaving a rock lip at the front where the ice thins and erosion is weaker (1)\n• After deglaciation the hollow may hold a tarn dammed behind the lip — Red Tarn beneath Helvellyn is the standard UK example (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how arêtes and pyramidal peaks form.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Where two corries develop back to back on either side of a ridge, their backwalls retreat toward each other through plucking and freeze-thaw (1)\n• The ridge between them is narrowed to a steep, knife-edged arête, such as Striding Edge (1)\n• Where three or more corries erode back around a single summit, the peak is reduced to a steep-sided pyramidal peak or horn, such as the Matterhorn (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain why a glacial trough has a characteristic U-shaped cross profile.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A glacier occupies a pre-existing V-shaped river valley (1)\n• Unlike a river, ice occupies the full width of the valley and erodes the sides as well as the floor (1)\n• Abrasion and plucking widen, deepen and straighten the valley, truncating the interlocking spurs of the former river valley (1)\n• The result is a steep-sided, flat-floored trough; tributary valleys, eroded by smaller glaciers, are left hanging above the main valley floor with waterfalls descending into it (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a ribbon lake and a tarn.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A tarn occupies a corrie basin, dammed behind the rock lip at the front of the hollow — small and roughly circular (1)\n• A ribbon lake occupies a long, narrow rock basin on the floor of a glacial trough (1)\n• The basin forms where the ice eroded a band of weaker rock more deeply, or where a tributary glacier added mass and increased erosive power; it may also be dammed by a terminal moraine — Windermere is the standard example (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between till and outwash as glacial deposits.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Till is deposited directly by ice, so it is unsorted — particles of every size from clay to boulders are mixed together (1)\n• It is angular, because particles have not been rounded by prolonged transport in water, and is unstratified (1)\n• Outwash (fluvioglacial material) is deposited by meltwater streams beyond the ice margin (1)\n• It is sorted, with particle size decreasing away from the ice as stream velocity falls, stratified in layers, and more rounded through attrition in water (1)` },

      { q: 'Describe the formation of a drumlin and state what its shape indicates.', marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Drumlins are elongated, streamlined hills of till, typically 30–50 m high and up to 1 km long, usually occurring in swarms (a "basket of eggs" topography) (1)\n• They form beneath moving ice where the glacier is overloaded with debris and deposits it, or where existing till is remoulded (1)\n• The blunt, steeper stoss end faces up-glacier and the tapering lee end points down-glacier (1)\n• The long axis therefore records the direction of ice movement, which is how former ice-flow directions are reconstructed (1)` },

      { q: 'A glacier snout retreated 340 m between 1980 and 2020. Calculate the mean annual rate of retreat, and the projected year of disappearance if the glacier is currently 2.1 km long and retreat continues at that rate.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Period = 2020 − 1980 = 40 years (1)\n• Mean rate = 340 ÷ 40 = 8.5 m per year (1)\n• 2.1 km = 2100 m (1)\n• 2100 ÷ 8.5 = 247 years (accept 247.06) (1)\n• 2020 + 247 = year 2267; note the projection assumes a constant rate, but retreat is accelerating and the glacier thins as well as shortens, so this is very likely an overestimate of its lifespan (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct arithmetic and a stated limitation of the linear assumption.` },

      { q: 'A glacier gains 2.8 m water equivalent in its accumulation zone and loses 3.5 m in its ablation zone in one year. The accumulation zone covers 60% of its area. Calculate the net mass balance per unit area and state what it implies.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Accumulation contribution = 0.60 × 2.8 = +1.68 m (1)\n• Ablation zone covers 40%: 0.40 × 3.5 = 1.40 m (1)\n• Net balance = 1.68 − 1.40 = +0.28 m water equivalent (1)\n• The budget is positive, so the glacier is gaining mass overall (1)\n• Implication: the glacier would be expected to advance, though with a lag of years to decades while the mass is transferred down-glacier to the snout — so snout position is not an instantaneous indicator of mass balance (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct area weighting and interpretation including the lag.` },

      { q: 'Clast measurements from a deposit give a mean roundness index of 0.18 and a standard deviation of particle size of 84 mm; a second deposit gives 0.62 and 11 mm. Analyse what these data suggest about the origin of each deposit.', marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `• The first deposit has low roundness (angular clasts) and a very high spread of particle sizes — poorly sorted (1)\n• Angularity indicates little transport in water, and poor sorting indicates deposition by a medium with no capacity to separate by size (1)\n• This is characteristic of till, deposited directly by ice (1)\n• The second has high roundness and a low spread — well sorted and rounded, indicating attrition and selective deposition by flowing water (1)\n• This is characteristic of fluvioglacial outwash; the contrast in both variables together is stronger evidence than either alone (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 uses both variables and reaches a supported conclusion for each deposit.` },

      { q: 'Ice cores show that global sea level was approximately 120 m lower at the Last Glacial Maximum, 20,000 years ago. Calculate the mean annual rate of sea-level rise since then, and comment on how this compares with the present rate of 3.6 mm per year.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 120 m = 120,000 mm (1)\n• 120,000 ÷ 20,000 years = 6 mm per year (1)\n• The long-term post-glacial mean is therefore higher than the present rate of 3.6 mm per year (1)\n• However the rise was not uniform: most occurred in rapid meltwater pulses between about 15,000 and 7,000 years ago, at rates exceeding 40 mm per year, with near-stability for the last 6,000 years (1)\n• So the comparison with a mean is misleading — the present rate is exceptional against the recent baseline, which is the relevant comparison for assessing current change (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation plus recognition that a mean conceals variation.` },

      { q: 'A corrie has a backwall height of 280 m and a floor-to-lip distance of 700 m. Calculate the mean gradient of the corrie floor as a ratio and as a percentage, given that the lip is 40 m higher than the deepest point.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• The rise from the deepest point to the lip is 40 m over a horizontal distance of 700 m (1)\n• Ratio = 40 : 700 (1)\n• Simplified = 1 : 17.5 (1)\n• Percentage gradient = 40 ÷ 700 × 100 = 5.7% (1)\n• The reverse (up-glacier to down-glacier) slope of the floor is evidence of rotational movement, since normal downslope flow could not produce an overdeepened basin behind a lip (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both forms of the answer and the interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Striation orientations at a site give the following readings: 040°, 038°, 042°, 039°, 041°, 315°. Calculate the mean of the dominant set and explain how the anomalous reading should be treated.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• The dominant set is 040, 038, 042, 039, 041; 315° is clearly anomalous (1)\n• Sum = 040 + 038 + 042 + 039 + 041 = 200 (1)\n• Mean = 200 ÷ 5 = 040° (1)\n• The anomaly should not simply be discarded: it may record an earlier or later phase of ice movement from a different source, or a local deflection around an obstacle (1)\n• It should be reported separately and investigated, since in glacial reconstruction cross-cutting striations are important evidence of changing flow direction rather than measurement error (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires the correct mean and a reasoned treatment of the outlier.` },

      { q: 'Explain the formation of the main landforms of glacial erosion in an upland area.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Corries — snow accumulation in a sheltered hollow; freeze-thaw and plucking steepen the backwall, abrasion and rotational flow overdeepen the floor, leaving a rock lip and often a tarn.\n• Arêtes — knife-edged ridges where two corries erode back to back.\n• Pyramidal peaks — three or more corries eroding around a single summit.\n• Glacial troughs — pre-existing river valleys widened, deepened and straightened; truncated spurs where interlocking spurs are cut off.\n• Hanging valleys — tributary glaciers with less erosive power leave their valleys above the main trough floor, with waterfalls descending.\n• Ribbon lakes — overdeepened rock basins on the trough floor, where weaker rock or increased ice discharge concentrated erosion.\n• Roches moutonnées — smoothed, striated stoss slope from abrasion and steep, jagged lee slope from plucking.\n• Processes throughout: abrasion, plucking, freeze-thaw weathering, and rotational or channelled ice flow; located support from the Lake District or Snowdonia.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several landforms explained accurately with the responsible processes identified and correct terminology, supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): landforms described with partial process explanation, or narrow coverage.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names landforms with little or no explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the formation of landforms produced by glacial deposition.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Till is deposited directly by ice, unsorted and angular; deposition occurs where the glacier can no longer transport its load, principally at the margins and at the snout.\n• Lateral moraine — debris from freeze-thaw on valley sides accumulating along the glacier edge.\n• Medial moraine — formed where two glaciers merge and their adjacent lateral moraines combine mid-glacier.\n• Terminal moraine — a ridge marking the furthest advance of the ice, deposited where accumulation and ablation balance for a sustained period; recessional moraines mark stillstands during retreat.\n• Ground moraine — an uneven sheet of till released as the ice melts, forming till plains.\n• Drumlins — streamlined till mounds shaped beneath moving ice, blunt stoss end up-glacier.\n• Erratics — individual boulders of rock foreign to the area, transported and dropped by ice; used to trace ice-movement paths, e.g. Shap granite found across northern England.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several depositional landforms explained accurately with their formative conditions and correct terminology.\n• Band 2 (3–5): landforms named and partly explained, or till and outwash confused in places.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about ice dropping rocks.` },

      { q: 'Explain the formation of fluvioglacial landforms.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Fluvioglacial landforms are produced by meltwater, so the material is sorted, stratified and more rounded than till.\n• Outwash plains (sandur) — braided meltwater streams beyond the snout deposit sediment, with particle size decreasing away from the ice as velocity falls; Skeiðarársandur in Iceland is the standard example.\n• Eskers — sinuous ridges of sorted sand and gravel deposited in subglacial or englacial meltwater tunnels, left standing as the ice melts; they run roughly parallel to ice flow.\n• Kames — mounds of stratified sediment deposited in hollows or crevasses on or against the ice surface, collapsing into irregular mounds on melting; kame terraces form along valley sides from meltwater streams between ice and valley wall.\n• Kettle holes — depressions formed where blocks of detached ice buried in outwash melt, causing the surface to subside; often water-filled.\n• Meltwater channels — including overflow channels cut by proglacial lakes breaching a divide.\n• Key contrast to emphasise: sorted and stratified (fluvioglacial) versus unsorted and unstratified (glacial), which is how the two are distinguished in the field.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several landforms explained accurately, with the distinctive sedimentary characteristics explained rather than asserted.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some landforms described with partial explanation, or fluvioglacial and glacial deposits confused.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names one or two landforms with no process detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics of periglacial environments and the processes operating within them.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Periglacial environments are cold but not glaciated, characterised by permafrost — ground remaining below 0°C for at least two consecutive years.\n• The active layer thaws each summer and refreezes each winter, and is the zone in which most processes operate.\n• Frost heave — differential expansion on freezing pushes larger stones upward, producing patterned ground: stone circles on flat sites and stone stripes on slopes.\n• Solifluction — the saturated active layer, unable to drain through the frozen ground beneath, flows slowly downslope even on gentle gradients, producing solifluction lobes and terracettes.\n• Nivation — freeze-thaw and meltwater removal beneath a snow patch, enlarging a hollow.\n• Ice wedges — thermal contraction cracks fill with water which freezes and expands, widening the crack each year.\n• Pingos — ice-cored mounds formed by groundwater freezing beneath the surface.\n• Wind action is significant because vegetation cover is sparse, producing loess deposits.\n• Human significance: construction must avoid thawing the permafrost, hence buildings and pipelines on piles.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): accurate characteristics and several processes explained with correct terminology and their resulting landforms.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some processes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised statements about cold, frozen environments.` },

      { q: 'Explain how glaciers move and why rates of movement vary between glaciers and within a single glacier.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — mechanisms:\n• Internal deformation — ice crystals slipping past one another under the weight of overlying ice; dominant in cold-based glaciers and in the deeper, plastic zone.\n• Basal sliding — movement over the bed lubricated by meltwater; includes regelation around small obstacles and enhanced creep around large ones; dominant in warm-based glaciers.\n• Extending and compressing flow — acceleration and thinning over steeper sections, deceleration and thickening where the gradient eases, producing crevasse patterns.\n\nIndicative content — variation:\n• Between glaciers: thermal regime (warm-based glaciers move an order of magnitude faster than cold-based), gradient, ice thickness and therefore basal pressure, mass balance, and bedrock permeability and roughness.\n• Within a glacier: fastest at the surface in the centre, where friction with the bed and valley sides is least; slowest at the bed and margins.\n• Faster in summer when meltwater is abundant, and variable over shorter periods with rainfall.\n• Surging glaciers can accelerate by an order of magnitude for short periods where basal water pressure rises suddenly.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): both mechanisms explained accurately and variation addressed at both scales with reasons.\n• Band 2 (3–5): mechanisms described but variation only partly explained, or one scale only.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that glaciers move downhill, with little explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain how glacial deposits are used to reconstruct the extent and behaviour of former ice sheets.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Terminal moraines mark the furthest extent of an advance; recessional moraines behind them record stillstands during retreat, so their spacing indicates the pace of deglaciation.\n• Drumlin long axes and striation orientations record ice-flow direction; cross-cutting striations record successive flow phases.\n• Erratics can be traced to their source outcrop, giving transport paths and distances — Shap granite and Ailsa Craig microgranite are classic UK tracers.\n• Till composition and provenance indicate which source areas contributed, and therefore ice divides.\n• Till fabric analysis — the preferred orientation of elongated clasts — gives flow direction at a site.\n• Outwash and meltwater channels indicate drainage routes and the position of ice margins.\n• Dating: radiocarbon on organic material above and below till, and cosmogenic nuclide exposure dating of erratic surfaces, provide a chronology.\n• Limitations worth credit: later advances erode earlier evidence, so the record is incomplete and biased toward the last glaciation.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several distinct lines of evidence explained with what each reveals, plus recognition of limitations.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some evidence identified with partial explanation of its use.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that moraines show where ice was, with no development.` },

      { q: 'Explain the value of glaciated upland landscapes to people, and the pressures this creates.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — value:\n• Tourism and recreation — dramatic scenery, walking, climbing and skiing; the Lake District receives some 18 million visitors a year and is a World Heritage Site.\n• Water supply — ribbon lakes and high precipitation make natural reservoirs; Thirlmere and Haweswater supply Manchester.\n• Hydroelectric power from steep gradients and reliable flow, significant in Norway and the Alps.\n• Farming — hill sheep farming on marginal land, shaping the cultural landscape.\n• Forestry and quarrying of slate and aggregate.\n• Scientific and cultural value — SSSIs, and landscapes central to national identity and to Romantic literature.\n\nIndicative content — pressures:\n• Footpath erosion on popular routes, requiring expensive repair; honeypot congestion and parking pressure.\n• Second-home ownership raising house prices beyond local incomes and hollowing out communities.\n• Conflict between conservation designation and development or farming intensification.\n• Traffic, air quality and seasonal employment that is low-paid and insecure.\n• Climate change reducing snow reliability, threatening ski economies in the Alps.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): a range of uses and pressures explained with located support and the link between value and pressure made explicit.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several uses listed with limited explanation of pressures.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised statements about tourism.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of glacial retreat in high mountain environments.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Rising air temperatures increase ablation and raise the equilibrium line altitude, shrinking the accumulation zone.\n• Reduced snowfall, and more precipitation falling as rain, reduces accumulation and darkens the surface.\n• Albedo feedback — exposed ice and deposited dust or black carbon absorb more radiation, accelerating melt.\n• Debris cover complicates the picture: thin cover accelerates melt, thick cover insulates.\n• The response is lagged — present retreat partly reflects warming decades ago.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Water supply — glaciers act as seasonal reservoirs, releasing meltwater in the dry season; some 800 million people depend partly on Himalayan and Andean glacier melt, so retreat brings an initial increase in flow followed by long-term decline.\n• Glacial lake outburst floods as proglacial lakes grow behind unstable moraine dams, a major hazard in Nepal and Peru.\n• Slope instability as debuttressing removes support from valley sides, increasing rockfall and landslide risk.\n• Hydroelectric generation affected by changing flow regime.\n• Ecosystem change as new terrain is colonised and cold-adapted species lose habitat.\n• Economic impact on tourism and skiing; loss of cultural and spiritual significance in some communities.\n• Contribution to global sea-level rise.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): causes and consequences both covered thoroughly and accurately, with examples and recognition of feedbacks or lags.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound coverage but unbalanced, or consequences listed without explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generalised statements about ice melting because of global warming.` },

      { q: 'Explain how landforms in a formerly glaciated upland provide evidence of past ice movement and extent.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Direction — striations and drumlin long axes give flow orientation; roches moutonnées give flow sense, with the smooth stoss slope facing up-glacier and the plucked lee slope down-glacier; erratic provenance gives transport paths.\n• Extent — terminal moraines mark maximum advance; the trimline, above which slopes are weathered rather than ice-scoured, marks the upper ice surface; the limit of till and erratic distribution marks the outer boundary.\n• Style and duration — recessional moraines record stillstands; overdeepened basins and ribbon lakes indicate sustained warm-based erosion; corrie orientation records the aspect favouring accumulation, typically north and north-east in the UK.\n• Deglaciation — meltwater channels, eskers and kettle holes record the pattern of ice wastage and the position of the margin through time.\n• Chronology — dating of organic material and cosmogenic exposure ages of erratics fix the sequence in time.\n• Limitations — later advances destroy earlier evidence; some landforms are polygenetic; and periglacial modification since deglaciation has altered slopes, so interpretation requires several converging lines of evidence.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): a range of landforms linked explicitly to what each reveals about direction, extent or timing, with terminology accurate and limitations acknowledged.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several landforms described with partial linkage to the evidence they provide.\n• Band 1 (1–3): names landforms with little explanation of their evidential value.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ways in which glacial and periglacial processes have shaped the landscape of upland Britain.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — glacial:\n• Corries, arêtes and pyramidal peaks in Snowdonia, the Lake District and the Cairngorms; Red Tarn, Striding Edge, Helvellyn.\n• Glacial troughs — Nant Ffrancon, Great Langdale — with truncated spurs and hanging valleys.\n• Ribbon lakes — Windermere, Wastwater — in overdeepened rock basins.\n• Depositional features — drumlin swarms in the Ribble and Eden valleys, terminal moraines, erratics such as Shap granite carried across the Pennines.\n• Ice sheets extended to approximately the Bristol Channel–Thames line at the maximum, so southern England was periglacial rather than glaciated.\n\nIndicative content — periglacial:\n• Solifluction deposits (head) on slopes across southern England.\n• Dry valleys on chalk, cut by surface flow over frozen ground when the permafrost prevented percolation.\n• Tors, e.g. on Dartmoor, from differential weathering along joints with the debris removed by solifluction.\n• Patterned ground and relict ice-wedge casts recorded in aerial photography and sections.\n• Loess deposits (brickearth) in south-east England.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• The present landscape is a palimpsest — glacial forms modified by subsequent periglacial and then temperate processes, and by human activity, so few landforms are purely one thing.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): both glacial and periglacial influences covered accurately with specific British examples and recognition that the landscape is relict and modified.\n• Band 2 (4–6): mainly glacial with periglacial thin, or examples generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–3): isolated statements about ice carving valleys.` },

      { q: 'Explain the concept of the glacial system and how changes to its inputs and outputs affect the landscape.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The glacier is an open system exchanging mass and energy with its surroundings.\n• Inputs: snowfall, avalanche, windblown snow, and rock debris from valley sides; energy from solar radiation and geothermal heat.\n• Stores: the ice mass itself, meltwater, and the debris held within and beneath the ice.\n• Transfers: ice movement by internal deformation and basal sliding, meltwater flow, debris entrainment and transport.\n• Outputs: ablation by melting, sublimation and calving; deposition of sediment; meltwater discharge.\n• A positive mass balance causes advance, increasing erosive power, extending troughs and pushing moraine forward; a negative balance causes retreat, exposing fresh terrain and releasing large volumes of sediment and meltwater, so fluvioglacial deposition dominates.\n• Dynamic equilibrium: the snout position adjusts toward balance, but with a lag of years to decades, so snout position reflects past rather than present climate.\n• Feedbacks: albedo feedback accelerates melt once ice is exposed; debuttressing after retreat destabilises slopes, increasing debris supply.\n• Human influence on inputs, principally through greenhouse gas emissions altering the energy balance.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): systems terminology used accurately throughout, with mass balance, lags and feedbacks explained and linked to landscape change.\n• Band 2 (4–6): correct system components listed with limited explanation of response to change.\n• Band 1 (1–3): a few correct examples with no systems reasoning.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which glacial landscapes should be protected from economic development.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — for protection:\n• Scientific value — relict landforms are irreplaceable records of past climate; once quarried or built over, the evidence is gone permanently.\n• Biodiversity — fragile montane and alpine habitats with slow recovery rates, and species with nowhere to migrate as climate warms.\n• Aesthetic and cultural value, underpinning tourism economies that depend on the landscape remaining unspoilt — so protection and economy align rather than conflict.\n• Water supply and carbon storage in upland peat depend on catchment integrity.\n• Designations — National Parks, SSSIs, World Heritage status — already reflect a settled judgement that these landscapes warrant protection.\n\nIndicative content — against absolute protection:\n• Upland communities are economically fragile, with limited employment, ageing populations and out-migration; restrictions on development can accelerate decline.\n• Renewable energy — hydroelectric and wind — is well suited to these areas and serves a wider environmental good, so protection can conflict with decarbonisation.\n• Quarrying and forestry provide employment and materials.\n• Landscapes are already cultural products of farming and management, not wilderness, so "protection" means choosing which human landscape to preserve.\n• Tourism itself is development, and causes footpath erosion, congestion and housing pressure — so the choice is not between development and none.\n\nJudgement: the question is not whether to protect but what and for whom; a defensible answer distinguishes irreversible damage to scientific and ecological value, which warrants strong protection, from reversible or low-impact development that can be accommodated through planning — and notes that excluding development entirely can hollow out the communities that maintain the landscape.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment engaging with "extent", weighing conflicting legitimate interests with accurate located evidence and reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides presented with some evaluation; judgement asserted rather than argued.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes uses or protections with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the relative importance of glacial erosion and glacial deposition in shaping upland landscapes.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — erosion:\n• Produces the large-scale, defining features of upland glaciated landscapes — troughs, corries, arêtes, pyramidal peaks — which determine the overall relief and are what makes the landscape recognisable.\n• Operates on bedrock, so the forms are durable and persist for tens of thousands of years after deglaciation.\n• Vertical scale is large: troughs hundreds of metres deep, overdeepened basins below sea level in fjords.\n\nIndicative content — deposition:\n• Produces the detail of the valley floor and lowland fringe — moraines, drumlins, till sheets, outwash plains — which determine soils, drainage and land use.\n• Dominates the landscape beyond the ice limit and in lowland areas; in eastern England the landscape is almost entirely depositional.\n• Depositional forms are more fragile and more readily modified or removed, so they are under-represented relative to their original extent.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The two are not independent: material deposited was first eroded, so deposition is the second half of the same process and the mass balance of sediment links them.\n• Relative importance depends on location — erosion dominates the uplands, deposition the lowlands and valley floors — and on scale: erosion sets the macro-relief, deposition the micro-relief that people actually farm and build on.\n• It also depends on the criterion: visual impact and relief favour erosion; influence on soils, agriculture and settlement favours deposition.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained comparison that resolves relative importance by location, scale or criterion, with accurate supporting evidence.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both covered with some comparison; judgement present but underdeveloped.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes erosional and depositional landforms with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the value of glaciers as an indicator of climate change.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — valuable as indicators:\n• Mass balance responds directly to temperature and precipitation, integrating both into a single measurable quantity.\n• The signal is visible and easily communicated — photographic comparison of snout position over a century is immediately legible to non-specialists.\n• Global coverage: glaciers exist on every continent except Australia, giving a worldwide network.\n• The response is largely independent of the instrumental temperature record, so it provides corroborating evidence from a different kind of measurement.\n• Ice cores from the same bodies give a long palaeoclimate record for context.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Lagged response — snout position reflects climate of past decades, so it is not a current indicator; mass balance responds faster and is the better measure.\n• Non-climatic controls — debris cover, topography, calving into water, and surging behaviour can cause advance or retreat independent of climate.\n• Precipitation and temperature can act in opposite directions: a warmer, wetter climate may increase accumulation at high altitude even as ablation increases.\n• Monitoring is uneven — long records exist mainly for accessible European and North American glaciers, biasing the global picture.\n• Glaciers only exist in cold regions, so they say nothing directly about change elsewhere.\n\nJudgement: valuable as one line of evidence, particularly for communication and for corroboration, but unreliable in isolation and best used alongside instrumental records, satellite gravimetry and ocean heat content.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): evaluates against clear criteria, distinguishes snout position from mass balance, and reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): strengths and limitations identified with partial evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that melting glaciers show warming, with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a nunatak and state what its presence indicates.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A nunatak is an isolated peak or ridge of bedrock projecting above the surface of an ice sheet or ice field (1)\n• It stands above the ice, so it is subject to freeze-thaw weathering rather than glacial erosion, and typically has an angular, frost-shattered appearance with a blockfield summit (1)\n• Its presence indicates the former ice surface level: the trimline separating frost-shattered rock above from ice-scoured rock below marks the maximum ice thickness (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a terminal moraine and a recessional moraine.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Both are ridges of till deposited at the snout of a glacier where accumulation and ablation are in balance for a sustained period (1)\n• A terminal moraine marks the maximum extent reached by the ice, and is therefore the outermost ridge (1)\n• Recessional moraines lie behind it and mark stillstands during an overall retreat; a series of them records the stages of deglaciation, and their spacing indicates how quickly retreat proceeded between stillstands (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how glacial troughs are modified after deglaciation.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Paraglacial adjustment — removal of the ice removes lateral support from oversteepened valley sides (debuttressing), triggering rockfall, rock avalanche and landslide in the centuries following retreat.\n• Scree (talus) accumulates at the foot of the trough walls from freeze-thaw weathering, progressively burying the sharp glacial break of slope.\n• Alluvial fans build where tributary streams enter the main trough, and deltas prograde into ribbon lakes, infilling them — some former lakes are now flat valley floors.\n• Rivers misfit to the trough incise into the floor sediments and rework the till.\n• Peat accumulates on poorly drained flat floors and plateaux.\n• Soil formation and vegetation colonisation stabilise slopes, until human deforestation and grazing reverse this and raise erosion rates again.\n• Net effect: the characteristic U-shape is softened — the floor is infilled and the walls buried at their base — so a present-day cross profile understates the depth of glacial erosion.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several post-glacial processes explained accurately with their effect on the trough form made explicit.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some processes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about weathering or rivers.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the characteristics of glacial meltwater streams differ from those of ordinary rivers.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — discharge regime:\n• Strongly seasonal, with very low winter flow and a large summer peak driven by melt rather than by rainfall.\n• Marked diurnal variation in summer, peaking in the afternoon a few hours after maximum insolation — a pattern no rainfall-fed river shows.\n• Highly variable and occasionally catastrophic: jökulhlaup outburst floods from subglacial or proglacial lakes can raise discharge by orders of magnitude within hours.\n\nIndicative content — sediment:\n• Very high suspended sediment load, giving the characteristic milky appearance from rock flour produced by abrasion.\n• Sediment supply is effectively unlimited, unlike most temperate rivers where supply limits transport.\n\nIndicative content — channel form:\n• Braided channels across outwash plains, because high and variable discharge combined with abundant coarse sediment and non-cohesive, unvegetated banks prevents a single stable channel.\n• Channels shift position frequently between and within melt seasons.\n• Steep gradients close to the ice margin, with rapid downstream fining of sediment.\n\nIndicative content — other:\n• Water temperature close to 0°C, limiting aquatic ecology.\n• Flow may be subglacial, englacial or supraglacial before emerging at a portal, so part of the drainage network is inside the ice.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): differences explained across regime, sediment and channel form, with reasons given rather than characteristics listed.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several differences identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): isolated statements, e.g. that meltwater streams are cold.` },

      { q: 'Explain the opportunities and challenges that glaciated environments present for human activity.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — opportunities:\n• Hydroelectric power — steep gradients, reliable meltwater flow and natural reservoir sites; Norway generates the great majority of its electricity this way.\n• Water supply — glaciers act as seasonal reservoirs releasing water in the dry season, critical in the Indus and Andean basins.\n• Tourism — skiing, mountaineering and scenery; the Alps receive over 100 million visitor nights a year.\n• Mineral extraction, where glacial erosion has exposed ore bodies, and aggregate from outwash deposits.\n• Fertile soils on lowland till and outwash, and transhumance pastoralism on upland pasture.\n\nIndicative content — challenges:\n• Hazards — avalanche, rockfall, glacial lake outburst floods, and slope instability following debuttressing.\n• Accessibility — steep relief and snow closure make transport expensive to build and maintain.\n• Short growing season, thin soils and steep slopes limiting agriculture.\n• Construction on permafrost requires piling and insulation to avoid thaw settlement.\n• Economic fragility — dependence on seasonal tourism, with climate change reducing snow reliability at lower altitudes.\n• Conflict between development and conservation designation.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): balanced treatment of both with specific located examples and explanation of why the environment produces each.\n• Band 2 (4–6): both covered but unbalanced, or examples generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–3): isolated points about skiing or cold weather.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which glacial retreat presents a greater threat to water security than to any other aspect of human wellbeing.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Around 800 million people depend partly on glacier melt for dry-season water, particularly in the Indus, Ganges, Brahmaputra and Andean basins.\n• Glaciers buffer seasonal variability, releasing water precisely when rainfall is least; no infrastructure replaces this cheaply.\n• The trajectory is a temporary increase in flow followed by long-term decline past "peak water", so the problem worsens after an apparent improvement — which makes adaptation harder to time.\n• Consequences cascade into food security, since much of the water is used for irrigation, and into transboundary tension over shared basins.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• Hazard risk may be more immediate: glacial lake outburst floods and slope failures kill people directly and with little warning.\n• Sea-level rise from ice loss threatens far larger populations globally than mountain water supply does.\n• Economic impacts on tourism and hydroelectricity affect regional economies substantially.\n• In many basins, monsoon rainfall contributes far more of the annual flow than glacier melt, so the water-security threat is often overstated — the glacial contribution is significant seasonally rather than in total.\n• Cultural and spiritual loss matters to communities in ways not captured by resource accounting.\n\nJudgement: the answer depends on scale and location — water security dominates in specific high-mountain-dependent basins during the dry season, while globally sea-level rise affects more people; a defensible response distinguishes severity, immediacy and the number affected rather than treating "greater threat" as a single measure.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment weighing water security against alternatives using explicit criteria and accurate evidence, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): identifies several impacts with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes impacts of glacial retreat with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the landscape of glaciated uplands owes more to processes operating since deglaciation than to glaciation itself. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — the case for glaciation being dominant:\n• Macro-relief is glacial: troughs hundreds of metres deep, corries, arêtes and pyramidal peaks are cut in bedrock and set the entire framework of the landscape.\n• These forms are durable; 10,000–20,000 years of temperate processes have modified but not removed them.\n• Drainage patterns, lake basins and valley long profiles are inherited directly from glacial erosion, and control where rivers, roads and settlements go today.\n• Depositional forms — drumlin swarms, moraines, till sheets — determine soil type and depth, and therefore land use, over large areas.\n\nIndicative content — the case for post-glacial processes:\n• Paraglacial adjustment: debuttressing after ice retreat destabilised slopes, generating rockfall, landslides and scree that have substantially modified valley sides; much of the present slope form is post-glacial.\n• Periglacial modification during the Loch Lomond Stadial and after — solifluction deposits, blockfields, patterned ground, tors.\n• Fluvial action — rivers have incised, built alluvial fans where tributaries enter troughs, and infilled ribbon lakes with deltas; some lakes have been entirely infilled.\n• Weathering and soil formation, peat accumulation across upland plateaux — blanket bog is a post-glacial deposit covering much of the British uplands.\n• Vegetation succession and then deforestation by people from the Neolithic onwards, which changed slope hydrology and erosion rates.\n• Human activity — grazing preventing regeneration, quarrying, reservoir construction, afforestation, footpath erosion; the "natural" Lake District is substantially a farmed landscape.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Scale is decisive: glaciation determines macro-relief, post-glacial processes the meso- and micro-scale detail, including almost everything a visitor actually walks on.\n• Timescale matters: glaciation acted for a comparatively short period with enormous energy; post-glacial processes have acted for longer with less energy.\n• The landscape is best read as a palimpsest — glacial forms inherited and then reworked — so the question is partly false, but a judgement can still be reached by specifying which elements are being discussed.\n• A strong answer commits to a position, most defensibly that glaciation provides the template and post-glacial processes the modification, while conceding that on slopes and valley floors the post-glacial contribution may dominate.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning engaging directly with the comparison; wide range of accurate evidence and located examples; resolves the question by scale or landscape element; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation of both with accurate support and a clear judgement, less sustained or less fully resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes glacial and post-glacial processes with some evaluation; examples generalised; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of glacial landforms with little evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the success of management strategies used in one glaciated upland area you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a named area. The Lake District is used below; credit any appropriate case study (Snowdonia, the Cairngorms, the Alps) assessed to the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — the pressures and strategies:\n• Context: National Park since 1951, UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2017, around 18 million visitors a year against a resident population of about 40,000.\n• Footpath erosion — Fix the Fells, a partnership repairing and maintaining upland paths using stone pitching and drainage; over 300 paths maintained.\n• Traffic and congestion — park-and-ride, bus services, cycle routes, parking restrictions at honeypots.\n• Housing — local occupancy planning conditions and affordable housing requirements to counter second-home ownership.\n• Farming — agri-environment payments supporting traditional hill farming, which maintains the cultural landscape the designation protects.\n• Zoning and visitor management concentrating pressure at sites able to absorb it.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Repaired paths demonstrably stabilise and revegetate; erosion scars on treated routes have narrowed.\n• The landscape remains the basis of a tourism economy worth over £1.4bn a year supporting some 18,000 jobs.\n• Designations have prevented large-scale inappropriate development.\n• Partnership working pools funding from the National Trust, National Park Authority and charities.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Funding is insufficient and insecure — path repair costs hundreds of pounds per metre and relies partly on donations.\n• Visitor numbers continue to rise, so management is reactive; post-2020 pressure produced severe parking and litter problems.\n• Housing measures have not prevented prices rising far beyond local incomes; the resident population continues to age and school rolls fall.\n• Success for tourism may conflict with success for residents or for ecology — overgrazing maintained by farm support suppresses woodland regeneration, so one policy undermines another.\n• Climate change presents pressures no local strategy can address.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — landscape conservation, economic viability, community sustainability, ecological health — and note the strategies perform differently against each.\n• Success for whom, and over what timescale, is the key evaluative question.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed place-specific evidence including figures, genuine treatment of conflicts between objectives, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate case-study detail and a clear judgement, criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes strategies with some evaluative comment; place detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of upland management with little locational detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.3': {
    name: 'Changing Places',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain the difference between space and place.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Space is a location defined objectively — a set of coordinates or an area on a map, without meaning attached (1)\n• Place is space to which people have attached meaning, value and emotional significance through experience (1)\n• The same physical space is therefore many different places to different people, and a space becomes a place through lived experience — "space is place without meaning" (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by endogenous and exogenous factors in shaping the character of a place.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Endogenous factors originate within the place itself (1)\n• They include site, geology, topography, land use, built environment, infrastructure and the demographic and economic characteristics of the resident population (1)\n• Exogenous factors originate outside the place and reach it through its relationships with elsewhere (1)\n• They include flows of people, money, investment, ideas and resources — migration, foreign direct investment, national government policy and media representation (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between an insider and an outsider perspective on a place.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An insider perspective belongs to someone who lives in or strongly identifies with a place, giving a sense of belonging and familiarity (1)\n• It is based on lived experience, personal memory and daily routine, so it registers detail and meaning invisible to others (1)\n• An outsider perspective belongs to someone experiencing the place from outside — a visitor, a newcomer, or someone who knows it only through media (1)\n• It tends to rely on visible characteristics and stereotypes; the two are not fixed categories, since a newcomer may gradually become an insider, and some residents feel like outsiders in their own neighbourhood after it changes (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by placelessness and give one example of a landscape that illustrates it.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Placelessness describes landscapes that lack distinctive character and could be almost anywhere (1)\n• It arises from standardised design and global branding, so the built environment carries no reference to local history, materials or culture (1)\n• Example: a retail park, airport terminal, or a "clone town" high street dominated by the same national chains found in every other town (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a clone town and state one reason the phenomenon has been criticised.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A clone town is a settlement whose high street is dominated by national and international chain stores, with few independent retailers remaining (1)\n• The New Economics Foundation classified towns as clone, border or home towns based on the proportion of independent shops (1)\n• Criticism: money spent in chains largely leaves the local economy, whereas independents recirculate a greater share locally; the loss of distinctiveness also weakens place identity and tourism appeal (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by place identity.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Place identity is the distinctive character of a place — what makes it recognisably itself and different from anywhere else (1)\n• It arises from the combination of physical features, built environment, economic function, history, culture and the people who live there (1)\n• It is contested and dynamic rather than fixed: different groups hold different views of what a place "really" is, and it changes as the place changes (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between place attachment and sense of place.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Sense of place is the set of feelings, meanings and perceptions an individual or group associates with a place (1)\n• Place attachment is the strength of the emotional bond between a person and a place, which can be strongly positive or negative (1)\n• Sense of place describes the content of the relationship; attachment describes its intensity — so a person can have a vivid sense of a place they feel no attachment to (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain three ways in which a place can be represented.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three of (1 mark each):\n• Formal/statistical representation — census data, indices of deprivation, maps and official statistics, appearing objective but shaped by the categories chosen\n• Media representation — news coverage, television, film and advertising, which select and frame\n• Artistic representation — painting, photography, literature, poetry and music\n• Place marketing — tourist board and council branding, logos and slogans\n• Informal representation — social media, graffiti, local conversation and oral history` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between qualitative and quantitative sources in the study of place, giving one strength of each.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Quantitative sources give numerical data — census statistics, house prices, deprivation indices (1)\n• Strength: they allow objective comparison between places and over time, and can cover a whole population (1)\n• Qualitative sources give non-numerical evidence — interviews, photographs, literature, oral history, field sketches (1)\n• Strength: they capture meaning, emotion and lived experience, which is precisely what makes a space a place and what statistics cannot record (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by placemaking.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Placemaking is the deliberate shaping of the public realm to strengthen the character of a place and the connection people have with it (1)\n• It involves design of streets, squares, landmarks, public art and green space, often with community consultation (1)\n• It is carried out by local authorities, developers and community groups, and is intended to attract investment and residents as well as to improve quality of life — which is why its benefits are contested (1)` },

      { q: 'The population of a town fell from 84,000 in 2001 to 71,400 in 2021. Calculate the absolute and percentage change, and the mean annual rate of decline.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Absolute change = 84,000 − 71,400 = −12,600 (1)\n• Percentage change = 12,600 ÷ 84,000 × 100 (1)\n• = −15% (1)\n• Period = 20 years, so mean annual loss = 12,600 ÷ 20 = 630 people per year (1)\n• Equivalent to a mean annual rate of about −0.8%; note that a mean conceals whether decline was steady or concentrated in a particular period, such as after a major employer closed (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires all calculations correct plus the limitation of the mean.` },

      { q: 'A ward records: 34% of residents in managerial occupations (national mean 22%); median house price £412,000 (national £285,000); 8% social housing (national 17%). Analyse what these data suggest about the character of the place.', marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `• All three indicators point the same way: the ward is substantially more affluent than the national picture (1)\n• Managerial occupations are 12 percentage points above the mean — over 1.5 times the national rate (1)\n• House prices are 45% above the national median, which both reflects and reinforces the occupational profile by excluding lower-income households (1)\n• Social housing at less than half the national share indicates limited affordable provision, restricting who can move in (1)\n• Together the indicators suggest a gentrified or long-established affluent suburb; the data describe the population composition but say nothing about residents’ own sense of the place, for which qualitative sources would be needed (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 connects the indicators rather than describing each in turn, and recognises what the data cannot show.` },

      { q: 'In a survey of 240 residents, 38% described the town centre as "declining", 29% as "improving" and the remainder as "unchanged". Calculate the number in each category and suggest one limitation of the survey.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Declining: 0.38 × 240 = 91 residents (accept 91.2) (1)\n• Improving: 0.29 × 240 = 70 (accept 69.6) (1)\n• Unchanged: 100 − 38 − 29 = 33%, so 0.33 × 240 = 79 (1)\n• Limitation: the sample may not be representative — those who use the town centre most, or who feel strongly, are more likely to respond (1)\n• Also accept: perceptions vary systematically by age, length of residence and whether the respondent is an insider or outsider, so a single aggregate figure conceals the disagreement that matters most (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires accurate calculation and a developed limitation.` },

      { q: 'Employment in a place shifts from 42% secondary and 38% tertiary in 1981 to 9% secondary and 74% tertiary in 2021. Analyse what this suggests about the changing character of the place.', marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `• Secondary employment has fallen by 33 percentage points, to less than a quarter of its former share — substantial deindustrialisation (1)\n• Tertiary employment has nearly doubled its share, rising 36 percentage points (1)\n• This suggests the closure or relocation of manufacturing and its replacement by services, a pattern typical of UK industrial towns since 1980 (1)\n• Likely consequences for place character: loss of the industrial identity around which community and workplace culture were organised, changed skill demands, and possible mismatch for former manufacturing workers (1)\n• Caution: proportions can shift because one sector shrinks rather than another growing, so the absolute number of tertiary jobs may not have risen; total employment data would be needed to tell (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 interprets the shift and recognises the limitation of percentage data.` },

      { q: 'A regeneration scheme cost £48 million and created 1,150 jobs. A second scheme cost £12 million and created 260 jobs. Calculate the cost per job for each and comment on what this does and does not tell you.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Scheme 1: £48,000,000 ÷ 1,150 = £41,739 per job (1)\n• Scheme 2: £12,000,000 ÷ 260 = £46,154 per job (1)\n• Scheme 1 is therefore more cost-effective on this measure, by about £4,400 per job (1)\n• Limitation: the figure says nothing about job quality, permanence, or whether the jobs went to local residents rather than commuters (1)\n• It also ignores displacement — jobs may have moved from elsewhere rather than being created — and excludes wider benefits such as improved environment or housing (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and a developed critique of the measure.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the characteristics of a place are shaped by its past.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Physical legacy — the built environment records past economic function: mill architecture, terraced housing built for a workforce, dock infrastructure, chapels and institutes.\n• Street pattern and plot layout often survive long after the buildings change, constraining what can be built now.\n• Contamination and dereliction from former industry constrain redevelopment and shape land values.\n• Demographic legacy — past migration produces the present population composition; nineteenth-century Irish migration and post-war Commonwealth migration continue to shape cultural geography.\n• Economic path dependency — skills, institutions and transport links built for a former industry influence what succeeds it, for good and ill.\n• Cultural and symbolic legacy — street and pub names, monuments, sporting clubs, dialect and collective memory of events such as pit closures.\n• Contested heritage — statues and street names connected to slavery or empire, where the past is actively re-evaluated, showing that the meaning of the legacy changes even when the fabric does not.\n• Located support expected, e.g. Liverpool’s docks, Manchester’s cotton warehouses converted to apartments.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several distinct legacies explained with the mechanism made clear and supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): valid points with partial explanation, or reliance on the built environment alone.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised statements that history affects places.` },

      { q: 'Explain how exogenous factors can change the character of a place.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Migration — in-migration changes demographic structure, language, retail provision and religious infrastructure; out-migration of the young leaves an ageing population and falling school rolls.\n• Investment and disinvestment — foreign direct investment can create an entirely new economic base, as with Japanese car manufacturing in Sunderland; withdrawal can remove it just as quickly.\n• Government policy — enterprise zones, transport investment such as HS2 or Crossrail, austerity in local government funding, planning designations.\n• Global economic shifts — the global shift of manufacturing to lower-cost economies drove deindustrialisation in northern England and south Wales.\n• Media representation — how a place is portrayed shapes investment and visitor decisions, so representation is itself an exogenous force with material effects.\n• Technology — online retail reducing high-street footfall; remote working changing commuter and rural housing demand.\n• Tourism and second-home ownership altering housing markets and seasonal population.\n• Interaction with endogenous factors: exogenous forces act on what is already there, so identical national policy produces different outcomes in different places.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): a range of exogenous factors explained with mechanisms and located examples, and the interaction with endogenous factors recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several factors identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about outside influences.` },

      { q: 'Explain how places are represented in the media and why these representations matter.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — how:\n• Selection and framing — what is shown and what is left out; news coverage disproportionately reports crime and deprivation in some areas.\n• Repetition of a narrow set of images creates a shorthand: post-industrial decline, rural idyll, inner-city danger.\n• Film and television locations create associations that persist for decades.\n• Advertising and place marketing construct deliberately selective images to attract visitors or investors.\n• Social media allows residents to produce counter-representations, so the picture is contested rather than dictated.\n\nIndicative content — why it matters:\n• Material consequences — representations influence where people choose to live, visit, study and invest, so they affect house prices, employment and regeneration funding.\n• Stigmatisation — negative representation of an estate or town can affect insurance costs, job applications and residents’ self-perception, becoming self-fulfilling.\n• Identity — residents may not recognise themselves in outsider representations, producing resentment and disengagement.\n• Power — those who control representation are usually not those being represented, so the question of who speaks for a place is a question about power.\n• Examples: the portrayal of Glasgow’s East End before and after the 2014 Commonwealth Games; ‘Benefits Street’ and James Turner Street.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): covers both how representations are constructed and their material consequences, with examples and reference to power.\n• Band 2 (3–5): describes types of representation with limited explanation of consequences.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated points about the media showing places.` },

      { q: 'Explain the process of gentrification and its effects on a neighbourhood.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — process:\n• Relatively low property prices in an accessible inner-city area attract higher-income individuals, often initially artists and students — the "rent gap" between current and potential value.\n• Renovation of individual properties raises values; new retail and services follow the changed clientele.\n• Media attention and estate-agent framing accelerate the process; developers then enter at scale.\n• Rising prices and rents progressively exclude lower-income households.\n\nIndicative content — effects:\n• Positive: improved housing stock, reduced dereliction, increased local spending and tax base, lower recorded crime, new services.\n• Negative: displacement of long-standing residents through rising rents and the loss of private rented stock; loss of affordable shops and services replaced by ones serving the incomers.\n• Loss of community networks and of the place identity that attracted gentrifiers in the first place — a self-undermining process.\n• Social polarisation and tension between established residents and newcomers, and the displacement of deprivation rather than its reduction, since statistics improve because poorer residents leave.\n• Examples: Shoreditch and Hackney in London; Digbeth in Birmingham.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): explains the process sequentially and covers both positive and negative effects with examples, including the displacement point.\n• Band 2 (3–5): describes gentrification with partial explanation of process or one-sided effects.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that an area becomes richer, with no development.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ways in which places can be rebranded.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Rebranding is the deliberate creation of a new identity for a place to change how it is perceived by residents, visitors and investors.\n• Flagship development — a landmark building or cultural venue intended to signal change; the Guggenheim in Bilbao, the Baltic and Sage in Gateshead.\n• Cultural and sporting events — City of Culture, Commonwealth Games, Olympics, used to attract investment and media coverage.\n• Heritage-led regeneration — conserving and reusing industrial buildings to make the past an asset rather than a liability, as in Liverpool’s Albert Dock.\n• Marketing and design — new logos, slogans and campaigns; renaming areas, as with estate agents’ coinages.\n• Public realm improvement — pedestrianisation, public art, lighting and green space.\n• Sectoral repositioning — targeting a new economic base such as digital, higher education or tourism.\n• Requirements for success: substance behind the image, local involvement, and sustained investment — marketing alone produces a gap between image and experience that damages credibility.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several distinct strategies explained with located examples and the conditions for success considered.\n• Band 2 (3–5): strategies listed with limited explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated examples with no explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the concept of "lived experience" is used in the study of place, and the methods used to investigate it.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the concept:\n• Lived experience refers to how people actually encounter and understand a place through daily life, rather than how it appears in statistics or official descriptions.\n• It emphasises that a place is constituted by the practices and meanings of those who use it, and that these differ systematically by age, gender, ethnicity, class, disability and length of residence.\n• It exposes the limits of quantitative data: two wards with identical deprivation scores can be experienced entirely differently.\n\nIndicative content — methods:\n• Semi-structured interviews and oral histories, allowing respondents to set the terms.\n• Participant observation and time-space diaries recording actual routine use.\n• Mental mapping, in which participants draw their own map, revealing what they notice and avoid.\n• Photo elicitation and participant photography.\n• Analysis of local literature, poetry, art and social media as expressions of experience.\n• Issues: small samples limit generalisation; the researcher’s presence and assumptions influence responses; and interpretation is itself a representation, so reflexivity is required. Triangulation with quantitative sources strengthens conclusions.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): explains the concept accurately and describes several appropriate methods with awareness of their limitations.\n• Band 2 (3–5): concept understood with methods listed but little critique.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about asking people how they feel.` },

      { q: 'Explain why different groups may have contrasting perceptions of the same place.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — sources of difference:\n• Age — young people and older residents use different spaces at different times; a skate park or night-time economy is an amenity to one group and a nuisance to another.\n• Length of residence — long-standing residents judge a place against its past; newcomers against the alternatives they chose between.\n• Insider and outsider status — visitors respond to visible character, residents to daily function such as parking, schools and services.\n• Ethnicity and culture — whether a place feels welcoming or hostile, and the significance of particular sites.\n• Gender — differential perceptions of safety, particularly after dark, which shape which routes and spaces are used at all.\n• Disability — accessibility determines whether a place is usable, so the same street is a different place.\n• Socio-economic position — gentrification is opportunity to some and displacement to others; regeneration benefits are unevenly distributed.\n• Direct versus mediated experience — those who know a place only through media hold representations at odds with residents’ experience.\n\nIndicative content — why it matters:\n• Consultation that hears only one group produces policy that serves that group; whose perception counts is a question of power.\n• Conflict over land use, licensing and development is often conflict between perceptions rather than over facts.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several bases of difference explained with reasons and examples, plus recognition of the consequences for decision-making.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several groups identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that people see places differently, with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain how economic change has altered the character of places in the UK since 1980.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — deindustrialisation:\n• Global shift of manufacturing to lower-cost economies, plus mechanisation, closed coal, steel, shipbuilding and textile industries.\n• Consequences: mass unemployment, out-migration of the young, dereliction, falling property values, and loss of the workplace-centred community and identity that had defined such places — south Wales valleys, Teesside, east Lancashire.\n\nIndicative content — the service and knowledge economy:\n• Growth of finance, business services and the digital sector, concentrated in London and a few regional cities, widening regional inequality.\n• University expansion transforming towns through studentification, changing housing markets and the retail mix — Loughborough, Lancaster.\n• Growth of logistics and distribution around motorway junctions, creating large-employment sites with little settlement character.\n\nIndicative content — retail and the high street:\n• Out-of-town retail parks and then online shopping reducing town-centre footfall; vacancy rates rising, anchor department stores closing.\n• Replacement by cafes, takeaways, charity shops and betting shops, and increasingly conversion of retail to residential.\n\nIndicative content — counter-urbanisation and rural change:\n• Rural in-migration of commuters and retirees raising house prices beyond local incomes; second homes hollowing out coastal and national-park communities.\n• Agricultural employment falling, diversification into tourism.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• The net effect is greater divergence between places; economic change has not affected all places similarly, which is why "levelling up" became a policy frame.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers several dimensions of economic change with accurate located examples and explains effects on place character rather than listing economic facts.\n• Band 2 (4–6): some changes covered with partial explanation of their effect on places.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generalised statements about factories closing.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of different players in shaping and changing places.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• National government — sets planning law, funding formulas, transport and industrial policy; decisions such as HS2 or enterprise zone designation reshape places directly.\n• Local authorities — planning permission, licensing, public realm, housing and regeneration strategy; constrained by funding.\n• Developers and investors — decide what is actually built, and where; motivated by return, so viability assessments determine how much affordable housing is delivered.\n• Businesses — location decisions create and remove employment; a single large employer can define a place’s identity and its vulnerability.\n• Residents and community groups — consultation responses, campaigns, neighbourhood plans, and everyday use that gives a place meaning; their influence varies with organisation and resources.\n• Media — representations that shape perception and therefore investment.\n• Landowners — control of land supply, including land banking that constrains development.\n• NGOs and heritage bodies — designation and objection.\n• Key analytical point: players have unequal power and conflicting objectives, so outcomes reflect negotiation and the distribution of power rather than any single plan — and communities with more time, money and expertise are consistently more effective in consultation.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): a range of players with their motivations and means of influence explained, and the inequality of power recognised, supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several players identified with partial explanation of their role.\n• Band 1 (1–3): lists players with little explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain how a place’s connections to other places affect its character.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — types of connection:\n• Flows of people — migration, commuting, tourism and student movement, each changing population composition seasonally or permanently.\n• Flows of money — investment, remittances, trade, tourism spending, and the extraction of profit to distant headquarters.\n• Flows of goods — supply chains locating warehouses and ports, and determining what is available locally.\n• Flows of ideas and culture — media, diaspora networks, food, music and religious practice.\n• Infrastructure — transport links determine which places are connected to which; a new station or motorway junction can transform accessibility and land value.\n\nIndicative content — effects:\n• Connected places tend to be more dynamic and diverse, but also more exposed to external shocks — a port city thrives and suffers with world trade.\n• Poorly connected places may retain distinctiveness but face decline through lack of investment and out-migration.\n• Relative connectivity matters as much as absolute: a place bypassed by a new route can decline although its own links are unchanged.\n• Connections are asymmetric — a place may be strongly shaped by decisions taken elsewhere while having no influence in return, which is a relation of dependency.\n• Examples: Sunderland and Nissan; Boston, Lincolnshire, and EU migration; Cornish coastal towns and second-home ownership.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several types of flow explained with effects on character and recognition of asymmetry or vulnerability, supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): flows identified with partial explanation of effect.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that places are connected, with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a near place and a far place, and why the distinction is not purely about distance.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Near places are those felt to be close, familiar and connected; far places are felt to be distant, unfamiliar or different (1)\n• The distinction is subjective rather than metric: a city 200 miles away may feel nearer than a village 10 miles away if it is better connected and more familiar (1)\n• Transport links, media exposure, family ties and shared culture all reduce perceived distance — time-space compression has made many physically distant places feel near (1)\n• Conversely, a neighbouring area with a different social or ethnic composition may be perceived as far despite adjacency, so the distinction reflects social relationships as much as geography (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by studentification and state two effects on a neighbourhood.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Studentification is the concentration of students in a residential area near a university, transforming its character (1)\n• Family housing is converted to houses in multiple occupation, raising rents and reducing the supply of family homes (1)\n• The retail and service mix shifts toward takeaways, bars and letting agencies, and the population becomes highly transient with strong seasonal variation (1)\n• Effects are contested: increased spending and reduced dereliction against noise, waste, falling school rolls and loss of established community (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by counter-urbanisation and one effect it has on rural places.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Counter-urbanisation is the movement of population from urban areas to smaller settlements and rural areas (1)\n• It is driven by the search for a perceived better quality of life, made possible by car ownership, remote working and rising urban house prices (1)\n• Effect: house prices rise beyond local incomes, so younger local people cannot afford to stay; incomers often commute out, so local services still decline despite population growth (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a perceived place and a formal region.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A formal region is defined by measurable criteria or administrative boundaries — a local authority area, a census ward, a river basin (1)\n• A perceived (vernacular) place is defined by how people think about it, and its boundaries are fuzzy and contested — "the North", "the Midlands", "inner London" (1)\n• The two frequently do not coincide, which matters because data is collected for formal regions while people live in perceived ones (1)` },

      { q: 'A neighbourhood records 46% of households privately renting in 2021, up from 19% in 2001, while owner-occupation fell from 62% to 38%. Calculate the percentage point change in each and analyse what this suggests.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Private renting: 46 − 19 = +27 percentage points (1)\n• Owner-occupation: 38 − 62 = −24 percentage points (1)\n• Private renting has more than doubled its share while owner-occupation has fallen by well over a third (1)\n• This suggests a shift to a more transient population, since private tenancies are typically short — consistent with studentification, buy-to-let investment, or affordability pushing households out of ownership (1)\n• Consequence for place character: shorter residence weakens place attachment and community networks; the data cannot distinguish between the possible causes, so qualitative evidence would be needed (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation, interpretation and recognition of what the data cannot settle.` },

      { q: 'Explain how place attachment can influence responses to proposed change.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Strong attachment produces investment of effort in a place — participation in community groups, campaigns and neighbourhood plans.\n• It also produces resistance to change perceived as threatening valued features, whether physical (a green space, a landmark) or social (the character of the community).\n• Opposition is often framed in terms of loss rather than of cost or benefit, which is why economic arguments frequently fail to persuade.\n• Attachment varies systematically: longer-standing residents and owner-occupiers typically show stronger attachment than recent arrivals or private renters, so consultation over-represents those groups.\n• Where attachment is weak — highly transient populations, areas with poor reputation — there may be little organised response at all, so change proceeds with less scrutiny; this can compound disadvantage.\n• Attachment can also be negative: residents may want change urgently where a place is experienced as failing them.\n• Consequence for planners: consultation measures the views of those with the attachment, time and resources to respond, so it is not a neutral measure of local opinion — which affects whose place is protected.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): explains the mechanism and its variation between groups, with consequences for decision-making made explicit.\n• Band 2 (3–5): identifies that attachment produces resistance with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that people do not like change.` },

      { q: 'Explain how demographic change alters the character of a place.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Age structure — an ageing population changes service demand toward health and care and away from schools; school closures then accelerate the loss of younger families, a reinforcing cycle. Coastal retirement towns illustrate this.\n• Youthful populations — studentification or areas of high in-migration produce demand for different retail, night-time economy and housing type.\n• Ethnic and cultural composition — migration brings new places of worship, food retail, festivals and languages, visibly changing the streetscape and creating new place identities.\n• Household composition — more single-person households changes housing demand toward smaller units.\n• Socio-economic composition — gentrification or decline alters the retail mix, since shops follow spending power.\n• Transience — a high turnover population weakens social networks and participation regardless of who the residents are.\n• Interaction: demographic change is usually a consequence of economic change and housing market change rather than an independent cause, so it is best read as part of a wider process.\n• Located support expected, e.g. Boston (Lincolnshire) after 2004 EU accession, or Leicester’s Belgrave Road.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several dimensions of demographic change explained with their effects on place character and supporting examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some changes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that the population changes.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ways in which literature, art and music contribute to the identity of places.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• They record and transmit the meaning of a place, preserving experience that statistics cannot capture — mining communities in the work of local writers, or Lowry’s industrial Salford.\n• They create associations for outsiders who have never visited: the Lake District as Romantic landscape after Wordsworth, Brönte country, Hardy’s Wessex.\n• These associations have material consequences, driving literary tourism and shaping conservation priorities — so representation feeds back into the physical place.\n• Music scenes become part of civic identity and are used in place marketing — Liverpool and the Beatles, Manchester and the Haçienda, Sheffield and its electronic music heritage.\n• Public art and murals assert identity in the built environment and can be a form of claim over contested space.\n• They can also fix a place in an outdated or partial image, which residents may resent — a romanticised rural idyll obscures rural poverty and poor transport.\n• Whose art represents the place is a question of power: official commissions and community murals may say very different things.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several forms covered with examples and the feedback into material outcomes recognised, including contestation.\n• Band 2 (3–5): examples given with limited explanation of their effect on identity.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names a book or band linked to a place.` },

      { q: 'Explain why regeneration schemes often generate conflict between different groups.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — sources of conflict:\n• Distribution of benefits and costs — those who gain (developers, incomers, the wider city) are often not those who bear the costs (displaced residents, small businesses facing higher rents).\n• Affordable housing — viability assessments routinely reduce the affordable component below policy targets, which residents experience as a broken commitment.\n• Displacement — rising rents force out existing residents and the businesses serving them; statistics improve because poorer people leave, not because they are better off.\n• Identity and heritage — demolition of buildings with community significance, or renaming of areas, is experienced as erasure.\n• Consultation — residents frequently feel consulted after decisions are effectively made; those with time, confidence and expertise dominate the process.\n• Competing legitimate objectives — a council needs to raise its tax base and attract investment, while residents want security of tenure; both positions are reasonable.\n• Environmental impacts during construction, and changes to traffic, parking and daylight.\n\nIndicative content — why conflict persists:\n• The players have unequal power and different time horizons: developers need returns within years, communities think in generations.\n• There is rarely an agreed measure of success, so each group evaluates the scheme against different criteria.\n• Examples: the Heygate and Aylesbury estates in Southwark; Elephant and Castle shopping centre traders.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several distinct sources of conflict explained with the underlying inequality of power recognised and supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): identifies conflicts with partial explanation of why they arise.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that people disagree about regeneration.` },

      { q: 'Explain how places can be studied using a combination of sources, and the problems of relying on any single source.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the range of sources:\n• Census and official statistics — population, occupation, housing tenure, deprivation indices.\n• Historical sources — old maps, photographs, trade directories, parish records, showing change over time.\n• Media and literary sources — newspapers, film, novels, showing how the place has been represented.\n• Fieldwork — land use mapping, environmental quality surveys, pedestrian counts.\n• Qualitative primary research — interviews, oral histories, mental maps, photo elicitation.\n• Digital sources — social media geotags, Street View, house price data.\n\nIndicative content — problems with single sources:\n• Statistics lack meaning and use boundaries that may not match lived places; they also age.\n• Interviews use small, often self-selecting samples, and responses are shaped by the interviewer.\n• Media representations are selective and made by outsiders for particular audiences.\n• Historical sources are partial and record the concerns of those who produced them.\n• Fieldwork is a snapshot, sensitive to the day, season and weather.\n\nIndicative content — why combination matters:\n• Triangulation — where independent sources agree, confidence rises; where they conflict, the conflict is itself informative, e.g. official statistics showing improvement while residents report decline points to displacement.\n• Quantitative sources establish structure and allow comparison; qualitative sources explain meaning; together they answer questions neither can alone.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): a range of sources covered with specific limitations of each and a clear explanation of why triangulation matters.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sources listed with some limitations identified.\n• Band 1 (1–3): names one or two sources with little critique.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which rebranding successfully changes the character of a place.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — evidence of success:\n• Bilbao: the Guggenheim (1997) drew over a million visitors a year and repositioned a declining industrial city; the "Bilbao effect" became a template.\n• Gateshead and Newcastle: the Angel of the North, Baltic and Sage changed external perception and supported a cultural economy.\n• Liverpool: 2008 European Capital of Culture, with measurable increases in visitor numbers and investment.\n• Rebranding can shift media representation, which in turn affects real investment decisions — so image has material effects.\n\nIndicative content — limits:\n• Image can change faster than substance, producing a gap between marketing and experience that visitors quickly detect.\n• Benefits are frequently concentrated in a small flagship area while surrounding neighbourhoods are unchanged — regeneration by adjacency does not reliably occur.\n• Employment created is often low-paid and seasonal in hospitality, and may not go to the residents whose area was rebranded.\n• Displacement — rising values can push out the existing community, so the place changes by changing its people rather than improving their circumstances.\n• Homogenisation — the same formula applied everywhere (waterfront, gallery, apartments) can erode rather than strengthen distinctiveness.\n• Cost and opportunity cost: flagship spending may exceed what the same sum would deliver in housing or services.\n\nJudgement: rebranding reliably changes external perception and can change the economic base; whether it changes the place for existing residents depends on whether it is accompanied by investment in housing, skills and services — so "success" depends on whose experience is used as the measure.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with criteria made explicit, accurate located evidence on both sides, and a supported judgement that distinguishes image from substance and identifies beneficiaries.\n• Band 2 (5–8): examples of rebranding with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes rebranding schemes with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that quantitative data is of limited value in understanding the character of a place.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Statistics describe population composition, not meaning; they cannot capture belonging, memory or attachment, which is what distinguishes place from space.\n• Administrative boundaries rarely match the areas people recognise as their place, so ward-level data may describe no real community.\n• Averages conceal internal variation — a ward with a median income at the national mean may contain two sharply divided populations.\n• The categories are chosen by the state, so apparently neutral data embeds particular assumptions about what matters.\n• Census data ages quickly, and places can change substantially within a decade.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• Quantitative data allows systematic comparison between places and over time in a way qualitative evidence cannot.\n• It covers whole populations rather than the small, self-selecting samples typical of interviews.\n• It reveals patterns invisible to residents — no one experiences a deprivation gradient directly, but it is real and consequential.\n• It is required for resource allocation: funding formulas must rest on comparable measures, and qualitative evidence cannot perform that function.\n• It provides the framework within which qualitative work can be targeted and its representativeness assessed.\n\nJudgement: the value depends on the question. For understanding meaning and lived experience, quantitative data is genuinely limited; for establishing structure, comparison and change, it is indispensable. The defensible position is that the two are complementary and that triangulation is the proper method, rather than that either is superior.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained evaluation that distinguishes the purposes data is put to, uses examples of both kinds of evidence, and reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): strengths and weaknesses of quantitative data identified with partial evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): asserts that statistics are useful or useless with little support.` },

      { q: 'Assess the relative importance of endogenous and exogenous factors in shaping the character of places.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — endogenous:\n• Site and physical setting are fixed and continue to constrain what a place can be — a valley town cannot expand outward, a coastal town is shaped by its relationship to the sea.\n• Built environment and street pattern persist over centuries, outlasting the economies that created them.\n• Local culture, institutions and social networks are internally generated and give places their distinctiveness.\n\nIndicative content — exogenous:\n• In an interconnected economy, the decisions that most change places — to invest, to close a plant, to route a railway — are usually taken elsewhere.\n• Deindustrialisation was driven by global competition, not local failure; the same is true of high-street decline under online retail.\n• Migration, tourism and second-home ownership transform demography and housing markets from outside.\n• National policy determines local authority funding and planning powers.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The two are not separable: exogenous forces act on endogenous conditions, so the same national trend produces different outcomes in different places — which is itself evidence that endogenous factors matter.\n• Timescale: endogenous factors dominate long-run continuity of character; exogenous factors dominate episodes of rapid change.\n• Scale and type of place: remote rural places may be shaped more by endogenous factors, globally connected cities more by exogenous ones.\n• A defensible conclusion is that exogenous factors increasingly drive change while endogenous factors determine how that change is absorbed and what the place remains.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained comparison resolved by timescale, place type or the change/continuity distinction, with accurate located evidence.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both covered with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): defines the two categories and lists examples with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that places are increasingly losing their distinctive identities. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Clone towns — national and international chains dominating high streets, so town centres across the country look alike; the New Economics Foundation found a substantial majority of UK towns were clone or border towns.\n• Placelessness in new build: retail parks, business parks, airports and volume housebuilder estates use standardised designs with no reference to local materials or vernacular.\n• Globalisation of consumption — the same brands, food, music and media available everywhere, weakening locally distinct culture.\n• Decline of place-defining industries removes the shared work, institutions and identity that once distinguished mining or mill towns.\n• Online retail and remote working reduce the significance of physical location altogether.\n• Gentrification applies a recognisable formula — exposed brick, independent coffee, converted warehouses — so even "distinctive" areas converge on one another.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• Physical setting, topography and building stock are immovable and remain strongly differentiating; no amount of chain retail makes Whitby resemble Slough.\n• Glocalisation — global brands adapt to local markets, and global influences are absorbed into local practice rather than replacing it, producing new hybrid identities rather than none.\n• Migration creates new distinctiveness: Leicester, Bradford and east London have identities that did not exist fifty years ago and are not replicated elsewhere.\n• Active resistance — conservation areas, Portas Pilots, local food and brewing, neighbourhood plans, and campaigns against chain proliferation.\n• Place marketing itself depends on distinctiveness, so there is a strong economic incentive to cultivate it; rebranding often works by recovering local heritage.\n• Digital connection allows dispersed local cultures to sustain themselves rather than being overwhelmed.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The question conflates the commercial landscape with place identity: retail may homogenise while lived identity does not.\n• Distinctiveness may be changing in kind rather than declining in quantity — identity based on industry replaced by identity based on culture, ethnicity or landscape.\n• Scale matters: convergence at the level of the high street can coexist with divergence at the level of neighbourhood and region.\n• Who is judging also matters: an outsider sees the chains, an insider sees the differences — so the claim is partly an outsider’s perception.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning engaging directly with "increasingly"; wide range of accurate, well-selected evidence and located examples; distinguishes dimensions or scales of identity; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation of both sides with accurate support and a clear judgement, less sustained or less well resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes homogenisation and some counter-examples with limited evaluation; examples generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of clone towns or globalisation with little evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the success of attempts to change the character of one place you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named place. Salford Quays is used below; credit any appropriate case study (Liverpool Albert Dock, Bilbao, Stratford and the Olympic Park, Margate) assessed to the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — context and strategy:\n• Manchester Docks closed in 1982 with the loss of some 3,000 jobs, leaving 150 ha of derelict, contaminated land and severe local deprivation.\n• Salford City Council purchased the site in 1983 using a derelict land grant; strategy combined land reclamation, water quality improvement, flagship culture (The Lowry, 2000), commercial development and, from 2011, MediaCityUK anchored by the BBC and ITV.\n• Transport investment: Metrolink extension improving accessibility.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Physical transformation of derelict docks into a functioning mixed-use waterfront; contamination remediated and water quality restored.\n• Employment created — MediaCityUK supports several thousand jobs in a high-value sector; substantial private investment leveraged by public funding.\n• External perception decisively changed; the area is now a visitor destination and a recognised media cluster.\n• Population growth reversing decades of decline.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Benefits unevenly distributed: adjacent Ordsall and Langworthy remain among the most deprived wards in England, so proximity did not deliver trickle-down.\n• Skills mismatch — media and digital jobs largely went to incomers and commuters rather than former dock workers or their descendants.\n• Housing built is largely unaffordable to existing residents; social housing provision limited.\n• Loss of the previous industrial and working-class identity, which some residents experience as erasure rather than improvement.\n• Dependence on a small number of anchor tenants creates vulnerability, as the docks’ reliance on a single function once did.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — physical regeneration, economic output, local employment, social equity, identity — and note performance differs sharply between them.\n• Success for whom is the decisive question: outstanding for the city’s image and economy, partial at best for the original community.\n• Timescale: forty years of sustained commitment was required, which is itself a finding about what regeneration demands.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed, accurate place-specific evidence including figures and dates, genuine engagement with who benefited, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate case-study detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit or the equity dimension underdeveloped.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the scheme with some evaluative comment; place detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of regeneration with little locational detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.1': {
    name: 'Water Cycle & Water Insecurity',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain the difference between a global water cycle and a drainage basin system.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The global water cycle is a closed system — the total quantity of water on Earth is fixed and water is neither added nor lost, only transferred between stores (1)\n• Its stores include oceans, ice caps, groundwater, atmosphere and surface water, linked by evaporation, condensation, precipitation and runoff (1)\n• A drainage basin is an open subsystem — the area drained by a river and its tributaries, bounded by a watershed (1)\n• It has external inputs (precipitation) and outputs (river discharge, evaporation, deep groundwater loss), so water enters and leaves the system (1)` },

      { q: 'Define the terms interception, infiltration and percolation.', marks: 3, command: 'Define', markScheme: `• Interception — the capture of precipitation by vegetation before it reaches the ground surface, from where it may evaporate or drip through (1)\n• Infiltration — the movement of water from the ground surface into the soil (1)\n• Percolation — the deeper movement of water from the soil down into the underlying permeable rock (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between throughflow and groundwater flow.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Throughflow is the lateral movement of water downslope through the soil layer, toward the river channel (1)\n• Groundwater flow is the much slower lateral movement of water through permeable rock below the water table (1)\n• Throughflow typically reaches the channel within hours or days and contributes to the storm hydrograph peak, while groundwater flow takes weeks to years and sustains baseflow between storms (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a water budget and state what a soil moisture deficit indicates.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A water budget is the annual balance between precipitation and evapotranspiration within a drainage basin (1)\n• It is usually plotted through the year to show periods of surplus and deficit (1)\n• A soil moisture deficit occurs when potential evapotranspiration exceeds precipitation and the soil store has already been drawn down (1)\n• It indicates that plants and crops are under water stress and irrigation may be required; in the UK it typically develops through late spring and summer and is recharged in autumn (1)` },

      { q: 'Define the terms lag time, peak discharge and baseflow as used of a storm hydrograph.', marks: 3, command: 'Define', markScheme: `• Lag time — the interval between peak rainfall and peak discharge (1)\n• Peak discharge — the maximum volume of water passing the gauging station per second, measured in cumecs (1)\n• Baseflow — the component of discharge supplied by groundwater, which sustains the river between storm events (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain three physical factors that produce a flashy storm hydrograph.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three of (1 mark each):\n• Impermeable geology or already saturated soil — infiltration is limited so water reaches the channel rapidly as overland flow\n• Steep relief — water moves downslope quickly under gravity, shortening lag time\n• Sparse vegetation — little interception and low evapotranspiration, so more precipitation reaches the surface and reaches it sooner\n• Small, circular basin with high drainage density — water from all parts arrives at the gauging station at a similar time\n• High-intensity rainfall or snowmelt exceeding the infiltration capacity of the soil` },

      { q: 'Explain how urbanisation affects a storm hydrograph.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Impermeable surfaces — tarmac, concrete and roofs — prevent infiltration, so a far greater proportion of rainfall becomes overland flow (1)\n• Drains, gutters and sewers deliver that water to the channel far faster than natural throughflow would (1)\n• Lag time therefore shortens and the rising limb steepens (1)\n• Peak discharge rises while baseflow falls, since less water percolates to groundwater — producing a flashy hydrograph and increased flood risk downstream (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between physical and economic water scarcity.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Physical water scarcity exists where demand exceeds the renewable supply available in the environment (1)\n• It is characteristic of arid regions such as the Arabian Peninsula, North Africa and parts of Australia (1)\n• Economic water scarcity exists where water is physically available but people lack access to it (1)\n• The constraint is a lack of infrastructure, investment or governance rather than a lack of water — much of sub-Saharan Africa, including basins with abundant rainfall, falls into this category (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by water stress and water scarcity, giving the thresholds used.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Water stress occurs where renewable supply falls below 1,700 m³ per person per year (1)\n• Water scarcity occurs below 1,000 m³ per person per year, and absolute scarcity below 500 m³ (1)\n• These are per-capita averages over a whole country and year, so they conceal seasonal shortage and regional and social inequality in access — a country above the threshold can still contain severely deprived populations (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a water footprint and distinguish between blue, green and grey water.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A water footprint is the total volume of freshwater used to produce the goods and services consumed by an individual, business or nation (1)\n• Blue water — surface and groundwater abstracted and consumed, for example in irrigation or manufacturing (1)\n• Green water — rainwater stored in soil and used by crops through evapotranspiration, without abstraction (1)\n• Grey water — the volume of freshwater required to dilute pollutants to acceptable standards; the concept matters because it reveals that consumption in one country draws on water resources in another through trade (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by virtual water and why it matters for water-scarce countries.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Virtual water is the water embedded in the production of a traded good, transferred between countries when that good is exported (1)\n• Producing 1 kg of beef requires around 15,000 litres and 1 kg of wheat around 1,300 litres, so trade in food is effectively trade in water (1)\n• Water-scarce countries can import water-intensive goods rather than producing them, easing domestic pressure — but this creates dependence on imports and exposure to world food prices (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between an aquifer and an artesian basin.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An aquifer is a body of permeable rock, such as chalk or sandstone, capable of storing and transmitting significant quantities of groundwater (1)\n• An artesian basin is a confined aquifer, sandwiched between impermeable layers, in which the water is under hydrostatic pressure (1)\n• Where a well penetrates the confining layer, pressure forces water to the surface without pumping — the Great Artesian Basin in Australia is the classic example (1)` },

      { q: 'A river has a mean discharge of 42 cumecs. During a storm it peaks at 315 cumecs. Calculate the percentage increase, and the total volume passing the gauging station in the six hours the peak is sustained.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Increase = 315 − 42 = 273 cumecs (1)\n• Percentage increase = 273 ÷ 42 × 100 = 650% (1)\n• Six hours = 6 × 60 × 60 = 21,600 seconds (1)\n• Volume = 315 × 21,600 = 6,804,000 m³ (1)\n• Equivalent to about 6.8 million tonnes of water — the figure assumes discharge is constant at the peak for the full six hours, which overstates the true volume since discharge rises to and falls from the peak (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations correct and the stated limitation of the assumption.` },

      { q: 'A country has renewable freshwater resources of 38 km³ per year and a population of 47 million. Calculate the water available per person per year and classify the country against the standard thresholds.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 38 km³ = 38 × 10⁹ m³ = 38,000,000,000 m³ (1)\n• 38,000,000,000 ÷ 47,000,000 (1)\n• = 809 m³ per person per year (accept 808–810) (1)\n• This is below the 1,000 m³ threshold, so the country experiences water scarcity (1)\n• It is above the 500 m³ absolute scarcity threshold; note the national average conceals regional and seasonal variation, and says nothing about whether the water is accessible to the population (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation, correct classification and a stated limitation.` },

      { q: 'Abstraction from an aquifer is 340 million m³ per year against a recharge rate of 215 million m³. The aquifer holds 6.2 billion m³. Calculate the annual deficit and the number of years until depletion at this rate.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Annual deficit = 340 − 215 = 125 million m³ per year (1)\n• 6.2 billion m³ = 6,200 million m³ (1)\n• 6,200 ÷ 125 (1)\n• = 49.6 years, so approximately 50 years to depletion (1)\n• The calculation assumes abstraction and recharge stay constant; in practice abstraction usually rises with population while recharge may fall with climate change, and water quality deteriorates and pumping costs rise long before the aquifer is empty — so the usable life is shorter than 50 years (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct arithmetic and a developed critique of the assumptions.` },

      { q: 'The Aral Sea covered 68,000 km² in 1960 and 7,000 km² in 2020. Calculate the percentage loss of surface area and the mean annual rate of loss.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Loss = 68,000 − 7,000 = 61,000 km² (1)\n• Percentage loss = 61,000 ÷ 68,000 × 100 (1)\n• = 89.7%, approximately 90% (1)\n• Period = 60 years, so mean annual loss = 61,000 ÷ 60 = 1,017 km² per year (1)\n• The mean conceals the pattern: loss accelerated through the 1980s as irrigation abstraction from the Amu Darya and Syr Darya peaked, and the sea separated into distinct basins, so a single rate misrepresents the process (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both figures and recognition that the mean conceals variation.` },

      { q: 'Rainfall at a station is: DJF 310 mm; MAM 95 mm; JJA 20 mm; SON 140 mm. Calculate the annual total and the percentage falling in the wettest season, and analyse the implications for water security.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Annual total = 310 + 95 + 20 + 140 = 565 mm (1)\n• Wettest season is DJF: 310 ÷ 565 × 100 (1)\n• = 54.9%, approximately 55% (1)\n• Over half the annual rainfall arrives in one three-month period, and the summer quarter receives under 4% — a strongly seasonal regime (1)\n• Implication: the annual total alone suggests adequate supply, but storage is essential to carry water through the dry season, so water security depends on reservoir capacity and groundwater rather than on rainfall totals (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires accurate calculation and an implication drawn beyond description.` },

      { q: 'Household water use is: toilet 30%, bathing 21%, washing machine 13%, taps 12%, outdoor 7%, other 17%. Mean use is 142 litres per person per day. Calculate the litres used by the three largest categories combined.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Three largest are toilet 30%, bathing 21%, washing machine 13% (1)\n• Combined = 30 + 21 + 13 = 64% (1)\n• 0.64 × 142 (1)\n• = 90.9 litres per person per day (accept 90–91) (1)\n• Interpretation point: nearly two-thirds of household use is accounted for by three activities, so demand management targeting efficient toilets, showers and appliances addresses most of the potential saving — whereas outdoor use, though visible during hosepipe bans, is only 7% (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and a supported inference about demand management.` },

      { q: 'Explain the factors affecting the water balance within a drainage basin.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The water balance is expressed as precipitation = evapotranspiration + runoff ± change in storage.\n• Climate — precipitation total, intensity and seasonality; temperature controlling potential evapotranspiration, so warm regions lose far more to the atmosphere.\n• Geology — permeable rock allows percolation and groundwater storage, sustaining baseflow; impermeable rock forces water into rapid surface runoff.\n• Soil type and depth — sandy soils have high infiltration capacity, clay soils low; deep soils store more.\n• Vegetation — interception reduces water reaching the surface, transpiration returns it to the atmosphere, and root systems improve infiltration; deciduous cover makes this seasonal.\n• Relief — steep slopes reduce the time available for infiltration, favouring overland flow.\n• Basin size and shape — affecting the timing rather than the total of runoff.\n• Human activity — abstraction, reservoirs, drainage, deforestation and urbanisation all alter the partition between the terms.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several factors explained accurately in terms of how they alter the balance, with correct terminology and examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): factors identified with partial explanation of their effect.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about rain and rivers.` },

      { q: 'Explain how deforestation affects the hydrological cycle within a drainage basin.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Interception is reduced, so a greater proportion of rainfall reaches the ground surface, and reaches it faster and with greater erosive energy.\n• Transpiration falls sharply, reducing the return of water to the atmosphere; in the Amazon this reduces recycled precipitation downwind, so deforestation can reduce regional rainfall.\n• Infiltration capacity falls as root channels are lost and soil is compacted by machinery and grazing, so overland flow increases.\n• Storm hydrographs become flashier — shorter lag time, higher peak discharge — increasing flood risk downstream.\n• Soil erosion increases without root binding and canopy protection; sediment loads rise, silting reservoirs and channels and raising flood risk further.\n• Groundwater recharge falls because less water percolates, reducing baseflow and increasing the severity of dry-season low flows — so deforestation produces both more flooding and more drought.\n• Soil moisture and nutrient stores are depleted, and the microclimate becomes hotter and drier.\n• Located support expected, e.g. Amazonia, Borneo, or Himalayan headwaters.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several flows and stores addressed with causal chains made explicit, including the apparently paradoxical flood-and-drought outcome.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some effects identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that cutting trees causes floods.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes of water insecurity.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical causes:\n• Low or highly seasonal precipitation, and high evapotranspiration in hot climates.\n• Climate variability — ENSO cycles, multi-year droughts.\n• Salinisation of coastal aquifers through over-abstraction drawing in seawater.\n• Geology lacking productive aquifers.\n\nIndicative content — human causes:\n• Population growth and urbanisation concentrating demand.\n• Rising affluence increasing per-capita use and demand for water-intensive foods, particularly meat.\n• Agricultural abstraction — around 70% of global freshwater withdrawal — often using inefficient flood irrigation.\n• Industrial demand and pollution rendering supplies unusable; untreated sewage and agricultural runoff.\n• Infrastructure failure — leakage from distribution networks, and absence of treatment and piped supply, producing economic scarcity.\n• Governance — weak regulation of abstraction, subsidised water encouraging waste, and conflict preventing investment.\n• Transboundary issues — upstream abstraction reducing downstream availability.\n• Climate change intensifying both drought and flood, and reducing glacial storage.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): both physical and human causes covered accurately with examples and the distinction between physical and economic scarcity used.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several causes identified, unbalanced or with limited explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about not enough rain.` },

      { q: 'Explain the consequences of water insecurity for people and the environment.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — for people:\n• Health — waterborne disease from contaminated supply; diarrhoeal disease remains a leading cause of child mortality.\n• Time poverty — hours spent collecting water, borne disproportionately by women and girls, reducing school attendance and earnings.\n• Food insecurity — irrigation failure reduces yields, raising prices and malnutrition.\n• Economic constraint — industry and energy generation require water; shortage limits development and deters investment.\n• Migration and displacement where livelihoods fail.\n• Conflict — between users, sectors and states, and within cities over rationing.\n\nIndicative content — for the environment:\n• Over-abstraction lowers water tables, drying wetlands and reducing river flows below ecological minima.\n• Salinisation of soils under irrigation, taking land out of production.\n• Ecosystem collapse — the Aral Sea lost around 90% of its area, destroying a fishery employing 60,000 people and leaving a salt desert whose dust damages health and crops.\n• Land subsidence from groundwater depletion, as in Jakarta and Mexico City.\n• Saltwater intrusion into coastal aquifers, permanently degrading the resource.\n• Loss of biodiversity in freshwater habitats, which have declined faster than terrestrial or marine ones.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): consequences covered for both people and environment with specific examples and causal explanation.\n• Band 2 (3–5): consequences listed with partial explanation or one dimension only.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised statements that people need water.` },

      { q: 'Explain the strategies available for managing water supply.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — supply-side:\n• Reservoirs and dams — store seasonal surplus for dry-period use and generate power, but flood land, displace people, trap sediment and lose water to evaporation.\n• Water transfer schemes — moving water between basins, as in China’s South–North Water Transfer Project; expensive, with ecological effects in the donor basin.\n• Groundwater abstraction — immediate and cheap but readily unsustainable where recharge is slow.\n• Desalination — reliable and independent of climate, used extensively in the Gulf; very energy-intensive and costly, with brine disposal problems.\n• Wastewater recycling — Singapore’s NEWater supplies a substantial share of demand; faces public acceptance issues.\n• Rainwater harvesting and small-scale storage, appropriate where capital is limited.\n\nIndicative content — demand-side:\n• Metering and pricing to signal scarcity; leakage reduction, since a large share of treated water is lost in distribution.\n• Efficient appliances, building regulations, and public education campaigns.\n• Agricultural efficiency — drip irrigation in place of flood irrigation, and crop choice matched to water availability.\n• Integrated water resource management coordinating across the whole basin and between sectors.\n• Demand management is usually far cheaper per cubic metre than new supply, but is politically harder and slower.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): covers both supply and demand strategies with examples and the trade-offs of each.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several strategies listed with limited evaluation of their drawbacks.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names one or two strategies with no development.` },

      { q: 'Explain how climate change is affecting the hydrological cycle.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• A warmer atmosphere holds more water vapour — roughly 7% more per degree of warming (Clausius–Clapeyron) — intensifying the cycle overall.\n• Precipitation becomes more intense when it occurs, raising flood risk even where annual totals are unchanged.\n• Spatial redistribution — wet regions generally becoming wetter and dry subtropical regions drier, as circulation cells shift poleward.\n• Higher potential evapotranspiration increases soil moisture deficits and drought severity for a given rainfall.\n• Loss of cryospheric storage — glaciers and snowpack act as seasonal reservoirs; their loss shifts runoff earlier in the year and reduces dry-season flow.\n• Reduced snowfall fraction, so precipitation runs off immediately rather than being stored until spring.\n• Sea-level rise driving saltwater intrusion into coastal aquifers and deltas.\n• Greater variability overall, so infrastructure designed for historic conditions is increasingly mismatched — stationarity can no longer be assumed in water planning.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several mechanisms explained accurately, with the intensification concept and the implications for storage and planning made clear.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some effects identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that climate change causes droughts and floods.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and effects of the over-abstraction of groundwater.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Agricultural irrigation, particularly where electricity or water is subsidised and abstraction unmetered — the Indian Punjab and the High Plains (Ogallala) aquifer.\n• Urban and industrial demand growth outpacing surface supply.\n• Drilling technology making deep abstraction cheap and individually rational, while collectively unsustainable — a tragedy of the commons, since the resource is invisible and shared.\n• Weak regulation and the difficulty of monitoring millions of private wells.\n\nIndicative content — effects:\n• Falling water tables requiring deeper wells and more energy, so costs rise and poorer farmers are excluded first.\n• Depletion of fossil aquifers, which receive negligible recharge and are effectively being mined.\n• Land subsidence and permanent loss of aquifer storage capacity as pore spaces compact — Jakarta subsiding up to 25 cm a year, Mexico City and the San Joaquin Valley similarly affected.\n• Saltwater intrusion in coastal aquifers, permanently degrading quality.\n• Drying of springs, wetlands and baseflow-dependent rivers, damaging ecosystems.\n• Increased flood risk where subsidence lowers land relative to sea level.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): causes and effects both covered accurately with examples and recognition of the common-pool nature of the resource.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes or effects covered adequately with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that taking too much water is bad.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of different players in the management of water resources.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• National governments — set abstraction law, water pricing, investment priorities and environmental standards; negotiate transboundary treaties.\n• Regulators — in England and Wales, the Environment Agency licenses abstraction and Ofwat regulates prices and company performance.\n• Water companies and utilities — supply, treatment and network maintenance; private companies balance investment against returns to shareholders, which shapes leakage reduction decisions.\n• Farmers — the largest abstractors globally; their crop and irrigation choices dominate demand.\n• Industry — large users, and a principal source of pollution.\n• Consumers — household demand responds to metering, price and information.\n• International organisations — the UN, World Bank and NGOs funding infrastructure and setting targets such as SDG 6.\n• Intergovernmental basin bodies — the Mekong River Commission, the Nile Basin Initiative — attempting to coordinate where rivers cross borders.\n• Key point: players have conflicting objectives and unequal power, and water crosses the boundaries within which each operates, which is why integrated basin-scale management is advocated and rarely fully achieved.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): a range of players with their roles, motivations and conflicts explained, supported by examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): players identified with partial explanation of their roles.\n• Band 1 (1–2): lists players with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes of conflict over transboundary water resources.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Around 260 river basins cross international borders, so upstream action has downstream consequences outside the acting state’s jurisdiction.\n• Upstream dam construction reduces or alters the timing of downstream flow — the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam and Egypt’s dependence on the Nile for over 90% of its freshwater.\n• Upstream abstraction for irrigation, as with Turkey’s GAP project on the Tigris and Euphrates affecting Syria and Iraq.\n• Pollution transferred downstream.\n• Absence of enforceable international law — the 1997 UN Watercourses Convention is weakly ratified, and principles of "equitable use" and "no significant harm" conflict with each other.\n• Historic agreements made under colonial arrangements are disputed by states that were not parties to them.\n• Asymmetric power — the upstream or militarily stronger state can act unilaterally.\n• Growing demand from population growth and climate change reducing supply, raising the stakes.\n• Counterpoint worth credit: cooperation is historically far more common than armed conflict over water, with hundreds of treaties signed — "water wars" are frequently predicted and rarely observed.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several causes explained with named basins and recognition that cooperation is the more common outcome.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes identified with limited exemplification.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that countries argue about rivers.` },

      { q: 'Explain how drainage basin stores and flows are altered by human activity.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Urbanisation — impermeable surfaces reduce infiltration and soil storage; drains increase and accelerate channel flow; groundwater recharge falls.\n• Deforestation — reduces interception and transpiration, increases overland flow, reduces soil and groundwater storage.\n• Agriculture — field drainage accelerates throughflow; ploughing alters infiltration; irrigation transfers groundwater to soil and atmosphere stores; compaction by machinery reduces infiltration.\n• Reservoirs — create a large new surface store, increase evaporation losses, and regulate downstream discharge, flattening the hydrograph.\n• Abstraction — removes water from groundwater and surface stores, reducing baseflow.\n• Water transfers — move water between basins, breaching the basin as a closed unit.\n• Channel engineering — straightening and dredging increase velocity and transfer flood risk downstream.\n• Afforestation and wetland restoration work in the opposite direction, increasing interception and storage — the basis of natural flood management.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several activities explained in terms of specific stores and flows using correct systems terminology.\n• Band 2 (3–5): activities identified with partial linkage to stores and flows.\n• Band 1 (1–2): generalised statements about people affecting rivers.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of drought.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — types and causes:\n• Meteorological drought — a prolonged period of below-average precipitation.\n• Hydrological drought — reduced river flow, reservoir and groundwater levels, lagging behind the meteorological deficit.\n• Agricultural drought — soil moisture insufficient for crops, which can occur after a short dry spell at a critical growth stage.\n• Socio-economic (famine) drought — where water shortage causes failure to meet human needs.\n• Causes: persistent high pressure and blocking anticyclones; ENSO, with El Niño bringing drought to Australia and Indonesia; shifts in the ITCZ affecting the Sahel; land-use change reducing recycled precipitation; over-abstraction converting a modest meteorological deficit into severe hydrological drought.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Environmental — reduced river flow concentrating pollutants; wetland loss; vegetation die-back; increased wildfire risk; soil erosion and desertification.\n• Economic — crop failure and livestock loss; reduced hydroelectric output; restrictions on industrial abstraction; rising food prices.\n• Social — malnutrition and famine where coping capacity is low; displacement; competition and conflict between users; health effects from poor water quality.\n• Key analytical point: drought is a hazard only where there is vulnerability — comparable rainfall deficits produce restrictions on hosepipe use in the UK and famine elsewhere, so consequences depend on wealth, storage and governance rather than on the physical deficit alone.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): distinguishes types of drought, covers causes and consequences thoroughly with examples, and recognises the role of vulnerability.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound coverage of causes or consequences with the other thinner; types partly distinguished.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that drought is lack of rain causing problems.` },

      { q: 'Explain how water supply is managed in a country you have studied.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `This question requires specific national detail. Singapore is used below; credit any appropriate example (Israel, Australia, the UK, Egypt) at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Context: a city-state of 5.9 million with almost no natural freshwater, historically dependent on imports from Malaysia under agreements expiring in 2061 — so water security is treated as an existential national issue.\n• The "Four National Taps" strategy diversifies sources deliberately to avoid dependence on any one.\n• Tap 1 — local catchment: two-thirds of the land surface is protected catchment, with 17 reservoirs; the Marina Barrage dams an estuary to create a freshwater reservoir and control flooding.\n• Tap 2 — imported water from Johor under treaty.\n• Tap 3 — NEWater: treated wastewater purified by microfiltration, reverse osmosis and UV, meeting a substantial share of demand, mostly for industry; public acceptance was built through a visitor centre and political leadership.\n• Tap 4 — desalination, with several plants supplying up to around 25% of demand; energy-intensive and the most expensive source.\n• Demand management: universal metering, rising block tariffs and a water conservation tax, mandatory efficiency labelling, and public education; domestic consumption reduced from about 165 to 141 litres per person per day.\n• Leakage reduction to around 5%, among the lowest rates in the world.\n• Governance: a single agency, PUB, manages the whole cycle, allowing integrated planning — a structural advantage over fragmented systems.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): accurate, detailed, place-specific account covering both supply and demand strategies with figures, and explaining why the approach suits the context.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound outline with some specific detail; generalised in places.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generic strategies with little locational detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain the relationship between water security and economic development.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — water security enabling development:\n• Reliable supply underpins agriculture, industry and energy generation; thermal and hydroelectric power both require water.\n• Reduced disease burden from safe water and sanitation raises productivity and school attendance; every dollar invested in water and sanitation is estimated to return several in economic benefit.\n• Time released from water collection, disproportionately for women and girls, enters education and paid work.\n• Predictable supply reduces investment risk and attracts industry.\n\nIndicative content — development enabling water security:\n• Capital is required for reservoirs, treatment works, distribution networks and desalination.\n• Institutional capacity for regulation, metering and enforcement develops with state capacity.\n• Wealthier countries can import virtual water rather than producing water-intensive goods domestically.\n• This is why economic water scarcity dominates in low-income countries even where water is physically abundant.\n\nIndicative content — complications:\n• The relationship is circular and mutually reinforcing, producing a poverty trap: insecurity constrains development, which prevents the investment that would deliver security.\n• Development can worsen insecurity — industrialisation raises demand and pollution, and rising affluence increases per-capita and dietary water footprints.\n• Wealth does not guarantee security: Gulf states are wealthy but physically water-scarce, meeting demand only through energy-intensive desalination.\n• Distribution matters within countries as well as between them — urban elites may be secure while rural populations are not.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): explains causation in both directions with examples, and recognises the circularity and the exceptions.\n• Band 2 (4–6): explains one direction well with the other thinner.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that rich countries have more water.` },

      { q: 'Explain how a river’s regime differs from a storm hydrograph, and the factors that determine each.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the distinction:\n• A regime is the pattern of a river’s discharge over a year, showing seasonal variation; a storm hydrograph shows discharge over hours or days following a single rainfall event.\n• The two operate at different timescales, so different factors dominate each.\n\nIndicative content — factors controlling the regime:\n• Climate — seasonal distribution of precipitation, and the timing of snowmelt, which produces a spring peak in nival regimes.\n• Seasonal evapotranspiration, reducing summer flow in temperate basins.\n• Glacier melt producing a summer maximum in glacial regimes.\n• Basin geology — permeable catchments with large groundwater storage have a damped, even regime; impermeable catchments are more variable.\n• Human regulation — reservoirs flatten the regime; abstraction reduces summer flow.\n\nIndicative content — factors controlling the storm hydrograph:\n• Rainfall intensity and duration relative to infiltration capacity.\n• Antecedent conditions — an already saturated soil produces a much flashier response to identical rainfall.\n• Basin size, shape and drainage density determining travel time.\n• Relief, soil, vegetation cover and land use.\n• Urbanisation and field drainage shortening lag time.\n• Key point: some factors (geology, land use) affect both, but antecedent conditions and rainfall intensity matter only for the hydrograph, while seasonal climate matters only for the regime.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): clear distinction maintained throughout, with factors correctly assigned and the overlap recognised.\n• Band 2 (4–6): both described with some confusion between the controlling factors.\n• Band 1 (1–3): describes discharge with little distinction between the two concepts.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by an aquifer recharge zone and why such zones are protected.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A recharge zone is the area of the surface where water infiltrates and percolates into an aquifer, replenishing it (1)\n• It is usually where the permeable rock outcrops at the surface rather than being confined beneath impermeable layers (1)\n• Protection matters because pollutants applied at the surface — nitrates, pesticides, industrial spills — travel directly into the groundwater, and because sealing the surface with development prevents recharge altogether; groundwater contamination is extremely slow and costly to reverse (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how sustainable drainage systems reduce flood risk in urban areas.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Conventional urban drainage removes water as fast as possible, shortening lag time and raising peak discharge downstream; SuDS reverse this by slowing and storing water close to where it falls.\n• Permeable paving allows infiltration where conventional tarmac would generate overland flow, restoring groundwater recharge.\n• Green roofs intercept and store rainfall, releasing it slowly through evaporation and delayed drainage.\n• Swales and filter strips convey water slowly overland rather than in pipes, allowing infiltration en route.\n• Detention basins and balancing ponds store storm water temporarily and release it at a controlled rate, flattening the hydrograph peak.\n• Rainwater harvesting removes roof runoff from the system entirely and substitutes for mains supply.\n• Additional benefits: water quality improves as sediment and pollutants settle out; urban cooling and biodiversity gains; amenity value.\n• Limitations worth credit: they require space and long-term maintenance, are less effective on clay soils or once saturated in a prolonged event, and must be designed into development rather than retrofitted cheaply.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several techniques explained with their effect on specific parts of the hydrograph, plus recognition of limitations.\n• Band 2 (3–5): techniques listed with partial explanation of how they work.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that green spaces soak up water.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which water insecurity is caused by human rather than physical factors.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — human factors:\n• Demand growth from population, urbanisation and rising affluence; agricultural abstraction at around 70% of withdrawals, much of it through inefficient flood irrigation.\n• Pollution rendering physically available water unusable.\n• Infrastructure failure and leakage — economic scarcity exists in basins with abundant rainfall, which is decisive evidence that availability is not the binding constraint.\n• Governance — subsidised water and unmetered abstraction remove any incentive to conserve; weak regulation permits over-abstraction.\n• The Aral Sea is the clearest case: a catastrophic loss caused entirely by irrigation diversion, not by climate.\n\nIndicative content — physical factors:\n• Aridity and seasonality set the resource available; no governance makes the Arabian Peninsula water-abundant.\n• Geology determines whether groundwater storage exists.\n• Climatic variability — ENSO, multi-year droughts — produces shortage independent of human action.\n• Climate change is altering the physical baseline, though this is itself human-caused, which blurs the categories.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Physical factors determine the resource; human factors determine whether that resource meets need — so insecurity is better understood as a mismatch than as a shortage.\n• The strongest evidence for the human side is that countries with similar physical endowments have very different levels of security, and that scarcity is often worst where water is plentiful.\n• Scale matters: at global scale there is enough water; insecurity is a problem of distribution, timing and access.\n• A defensible conclusion attributes the existence of physical limits to nature and the existence of insecurity largely to human decisions, while noting climate change collapses the distinction.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with explicit criteria, accurate contrasting examples, and a judgement that engages with the physical/human distinction rather than listing both.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both covered with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists causes with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of large dams as a strategy for improving water security.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — effective:\n• Store seasonal surplus, converting a highly variable supply into a reliable one — the core function, and one no demand-side measure achieves.\n• Enable irrigation, raising agricultural output and food security.\n• Generate low-carbon electricity and provide flood control; the Three Gorges Dam has substantial generating capacity and flood-storage volume.\n• Support navigation and industrial development; a single asset delivers multiple benefits, which is why they remain attractive to governments.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Displacement — the Three Gorges Dam displaced approximately 1.3 million people; compensation and resettlement are frequently inadequate.\n• Ecological disruption — blocked fish migration, altered flow and temperature regimes, loss of downstream floodplain fertility as sediment is trapped.\n• Sediment trapping reduces reservoir capacity over time and starves deltas, accelerating coastal erosion — the Nile delta since the Aswan High Dam.\n• Evaporation losses can be very large in hot climates, partly offsetting the storage gained.\n• Very high capital cost and long construction times; cost overruns are the norm, and debt burdens can constrain other spending.\n• Transboundary conflict where the dam is upstream of another state.\n• Seismic risk and, in tropical reservoirs, methane emissions from decomposing vegetation, undermining the low-carbon claim.\n• Opportunity cost: demand management, leakage reduction and irrigation efficiency typically deliver more water per unit of expenditure.\n\nJudgement: effective at the specific task of storing and regulating flow, and in some contexts irreplaceable, but frequently chosen for political visibility over cheaper alternatives, and with costs borne by displaced and downstream populations who do not receive the benefits — so effectiveness depends on whose water security is being measured.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): balanced assessment with named examples and figures, explicit criteria, and a judgement addressing distribution of costs and benefits.\n• Band 2 (5–8): advantages and disadvantages with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes dams with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that demand management is more sustainable than increasing water supply.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Reduces abstraction, so it addresses the cause rather than the symptom and does not transfer the problem to another basin or to the future.\n• Far cheaper per cubic metre: leakage reduction, metering and efficient appliances cost a fraction of new reservoirs or desalination.\n• Low environmental impact — no displacement, habitat loss or sediment trapping.\n• Scalable and incremental, so it can be implemented without large capital or long lead times.\n• Singapore reduced domestic use from about 165 to 141 litres per person per day through metering, pricing and education.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• There is a floor to demand reduction — basic needs cannot be compressed indefinitely, so in severely water-scarce or fast-growing places supply augmentation is unavoidable.\n• Requires functioning institutions: metering, billing and enforcement presuppose state capacity that economically water-scarce countries often lack.\n• Pricing raises equity concerns, since water is a basic need and demand is inelastic for the poorest — tariffs can ration by income.\n• Behavioural change is slow and can rebound; efficiency savings may be absorbed by increased use (the Jevons effect).\n• Agriculture, not households, dominates abstraction, so household demand management addresses a small share of the problem; agricultural efficiency is harder to achieve.\n• Supply measures can be sustainable — recycled wastewater and rainwater harvesting increase supply with low impact — so the dichotomy is false.\n\nJudgement: demand management is generally the more sustainable first resort and is systematically under-used because it is politically less visible than infrastructure; but in rapidly growing or physically arid contexts it is insufficient alone, and the sustainable strategy combines both — which is what integrated water resource management proposes.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained evaluation engaging with "sustainable" across environmental, economic and social dimensions, with evidence, and challenging the dichotomy.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both approaches covered with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes strategies with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the importance of governance in determining water security.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — governance as decisive:\n• Economic water scarcity is by definition a governance failure — water is present but investment, institutions or distribution are lacking.\n• Pricing and metering determine whether users have any incentive to conserve; subsidised power for pumping has driven aquifer depletion across South Asia.\n• Regulation of abstraction and pollution determines whether the resource remains usable.\n• Institutional integration matters: Singapore’s single agency managing the whole cycle enables planning that fragmented systems cannot achieve.\n• Transboundary treaties determine whether shared rivers generate cooperation or conflict.\n• Corruption and conflict prevent maintenance and investment, so infrastructure decays regardless of physical endowment.\n\nIndicative content — limits to governance:\n• Physical endowment sets the ceiling — excellent governance cannot create rainfall, and Gulf states rely on desalination because no institutional arrangement makes them water-abundant.\n• Capital constrains what governance can deliver; good intentions without finance achieve little.\n• Climate change alters the resource irrespective of national policy, and is driven by emissions outside any one state’s control.\n• Population growth can outpace even well-managed supply expansion.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The strongest evidence for governance is comparative: countries with similar physical conditions differ sharply in security, and water-abundant countries can be highly insecure.\n• Governance determines how efficiently and equitably a given endowment is converted into security, so it is the principal variable within physical limits.\n• A defensible conclusion: physical factors set the constraint, governance determines performance within it — and since most of the world’s insecurity occurs well inside physical limits, governance is the more important explanatory factor in practice.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment using comparative evidence, weighing governance against physical and economic constraints, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): identifies the role of governance with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that governments manage water, with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that water scarcity is the most serious resource challenge facing the world. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Water has no substitute — unlike energy sources or many materials, nothing can replace it for drinking, agriculture or most industrial processes.\n• Scale of exposure: around 2 billion people live in countries experiencing high water stress, and roughly half the world’s population experiences severe scarcity for at least one month a year.\n• Water underpins food security, since agriculture accounts for about 70% of withdrawals; a water crisis becomes a food crisis.\n• It underpins energy — thermal and hydroelectric generation both depend on water — producing a water–energy–food nexus in which failure propagates.\n• Health consequences are immediate and severe; unsafe water and sanitation remain leading causes of child mortality.\n• Trends are adverse: demand rising with population and affluence while climate change reduces reliability and glacial storage.\n• Transboundary basins create conflict potential where no effective law applies.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• Climate change is arguably more fundamental, since it drives water insecurity along with sea-level rise, heat stress and ecosystem collapse — water scarcity is partly a symptom of it.\n• Biodiversity loss may be less reversible: extinction is permanent, whereas water systems can recover if abstraction falls.\n• Soil degradation and phosphorus depletion threaten food production independently of water.\n• Energy transition arguably conditions the ability to address everything else, including desalination and water treatment.\n• Water scarcity is largely soluble with known technology and modest investment relative to other challenges — leakage reduction, irrigation efficiency, recycling and desalination all exist and work, so it is a problem of political will rather than of capability.\n• Globally there is enough water; the problem is distribution, timing and access, which makes it different in kind from an absolute resource limit.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Criteria must be established: severity, reversibility, number affected, tractability, and whether the challenge is causal or symptomatic.\n• Water scores highest on immediacy and number affected, lower on tractability-as-obstacle and on being a root cause.\n• Scale matters: for specific regions and populations it unquestionably is the most serious; globally it is one of several interacting challenges.\n• The interconnection argument is strong — the nexus means these cannot be meaningfully ranked in isolation, and a sophisticated answer may argue the ranking itself is the wrong question while still committing to a position.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning engaging directly with "most serious"; explicit criteria; wide range of accurate evidence with figures and examples; genuine comparison against alternative challenges; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation with accurate support and a clear judgement; comparison present but less developed, or criteria implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes water scarcity with limited comparison to other challenges; examples generalised; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of water shortage with little evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the success of water management in one country or region you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a named country or region. Singapore is used below; credit any appropriate case study (Israel, the Colorado Basin, Australia’s Murray–Darling, Egypt) at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — context and strategy:\n• A city-state of 5.9 million with negligible natural freshwater and historic dependence on Malaysian imports under agreements expiring in 2061 — water security is framed as a matter of national survival.\n• The Four National Taps: protected local catchment (two-thirds of the land area, 17 reservoirs, the Marina Barrage), imported water, NEWater recycled wastewater, and desalination.\n• Demand management: universal metering, rising block tariffs and a conservation tax, mandatory water-efficiency labelling, sustained public education.\n• Integrated governance through a single agency, PUB, responsible for the entire water cycle.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Diversification has substantially reduced dependence on imports; NEWater and desalination together can meet a large share of demand.\n• Domestic consumption reduced from about 165 to 141 litres per person per day.\n• Leakage around 5%, among the lowest in the world, against roughly 20% in England and Wales.\n• Universal access to safe piped water and sanitation.\n• Public acceptance of recycled water achieved where other countries have failed, through visible political commitment and the NEWater visitor centre.\n• Water has been turned into an export industry, with Singaporean expertise sold internationally.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Very high energy cost — desalination and reverse osmosis are energy-intensive, so water security is achieved partly by transferring the problem into carbon emissions.\n• High capital cost, affordable only to a wealthy state with strong fiscal capacity.\n• Continued dependence on Malaysian imports until the treaty expires, leaving a geopolitical vulnerability.\n• Rising tariffs raise equity concerns for lower-income households, mitigated but not removed by rebates.\n• Transferability is limited: the approach depends on a small area, a compact population, strong governance and high income, so it is a poor model for most water-scarce countries.\n• Long-term exposure to sea-level rise affecting the Marina Barrage and coastal reservoirs.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — security of supply, affordability, environmental sustainability, equity, resilience — noting different performance against each.\n• Highly successful against security and efficiency; more questionable against carbon sustainability and transferability.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate place-specific evidence including figures, genuine treatment of limitations and trade-offs, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit or limitations underdeveloped.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the strategy with some evaluative comment; detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of water management with little locational detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.2': {
    name: 'Carbon Cycle & Energy Security',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Identify the four major carbon stores and rank them by size.', marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• Lithosphere — carbonate rocks and fossil fuels; much the largest, holding the great majority of Earth’s carbon, of the order of 100 million gigatonnes (1)\n• Oceans — around 38,000 GtC, mostly as dissolved inorganic carbon at depth (1)\n• Terrestrial biosphere and soils — around 2,000–2,300 GtC, of which soil holds roughly three times as much as living vegetation (1)\n• Atmosphere — around 875 GtC, the smallest of the four but the one whose change drives climate (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a carbon source and a carbon sink.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A carbon source releases more carbon than it absorbs, adding to the atmospheric store (1)\n• A carbon sink absorbs more than it releases, removing carbon from the atmosphere (1)\n• The same system can switch between the two: a mature forest is a sink while growing, but becomes a source if it burns, is cleared, or if warming raises respiration above photosynthesis (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between the fast and slow carbon cycles.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The fast carbon cycle operates over years to decades, moving carbon between the atmosphere, biosphere and surface ocean (1)\n• Its principal flows are photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition and ocean–atmosphere exchange, moving of the order of 100–120 GtC per year (1)\n• The slow carbon cycle operates over millions of years, moving carbon between rocks, soil, ocean and atmosphere (1)\n• Its flows are chemical weathering, sedimentation, burial, subduction and volcanic outgassing, moving only around 0.1–0.2 GtC per year — which is why burning fossil fuels transfers slow-cycle carbon into the fast cycle far faster than it can be returned (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of photosynthesis and respiration in the carbon cycle.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Photosynthesis removes CO₂ from the atmosphere, using solar energy to fix carbon into organic compounds in plant tissue — the principal input to the biosphere store (1)\n• Respiration by plants, animals and decomposers releases CO₂ back to the atmosphere as organic compounds are broken down for energy (1)\n• Net primary productivity is the difference between the two, and the seasonal imbalance in the Northern Hemisphere produces the annual sawtooth in the Keeling curve (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how the biological pump transfers carbon to the deep ocean.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Phytoplankton in the surface ocean photosynthesise, fixing dissolved CO₂ into organic matter (1)\n• This reduces the surface concentration of CO₂, allowing further uptake from the atmosphere (1)\n• When the organisms die, or are eaten and excreted, the organic matter and carbonate shells sink through the water column (1)\n• Most is remineralised at depth, but a fraction reaches the sea floor and is buried in sediment, transferring carbon into the slow cycle for millions of years (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the carbonate pump and how it differs from the biological pump.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The carbonate pump involves marine organisms — coccolithophores, foraminifera, molluscs and corals — building shells and skeletons of calcium carbonate (1)\n• On death these sink and accumulate as carbonate sediment, eventually forming limestone and chalk — the largest long-term carbon store (1)\n• It differs from the biological pump in transferring inorganic rather than organic carbon, and because the precipitation of carbonate actually releases CO₂ at the surface, so its short-term effect is opposite to its long-term one (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how carbon is stored in soil and why soil carbon is significant.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Soil carbon accumulates as organic matter from dead plant material and roots, which is only partly decomposed (1)\n• Storage is greatest where decomposition is slow — in cold, waterlogged or acidic conditions, so peatlands and permafrost hold disproportionate amounts (1)\n• Soils hold roughly three times as much carbon as living vegetation and more than the atmosphere, so small proportional changes have large absolute effects (1)\n• Significance: ploughing, drainage and warming accelerate decomposition, converting a vast sink into a source, and UK peatlands are currently net emitters because of historic drainage (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the process of chemical weathering as part of the carbon cycle.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Atmospheric CO₂ dissolves in rainwater to form weak carbonic acid (1)\n• This reacts with silicate and carbonate rocks, releasing ions that are carried in solution to the oceans, where they are used by organisms to build carbonate shells (1)\n• Silicate weathering is therefore a long-term atmospheric sink, and acts as a thermostat — warmer, wetter conditions accelerate weathering, drawing down more CO₂ over geological time (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by energy security and state the four dimensions usually used to assess it.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Energy security is the uninterrupted availability of energy sources at an affordable price (1)\n• Availability — whether sufficient physical supply exists, domestically or through imports (1)\n• Accessibility — whether it can be obtained, given geography, infrastructure and geopolitics (1)\n• Affordability and reliability — whether the price is sustainable for consumers and the economy, and whether supply is consistent rather than intermittent (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between primary and secondary energy, and between renewable and non-renewable sources.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Primary energy is found in nature and used in its raw form — coal, oil, gas, wind, solar radiation (1)\n• Secondary energy has been converted from a primary source, such as electricity or refined petrol; conversion always involves losses (1)\n• Renewable sources are replenished on a human timescale and are not depleted by use — solar, wind, hydro, geothermal, tidal (1)\n• Non-renewable sources exist in finite quantities formed over geological time, so use depletes the stock — coal, oil, gas and uranium (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by an energy mix and state two factors that influence a country’s choice of mix.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An energy mix is the combination of different sources a country uses to meet its total energy demand, usually expressed as percentage shares (1)\n• Factor: domestic resource endowment — Norway’s hydro potential, Saudi Arabia’s oil, Iceland’s geothermal (1)\n• Factor: level of development and wealth determining capital available; also accept government policy and climate commitments, public opinion (particularly on nuclear), and physical geography such as insolation or wind resource (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by an energy pathway.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An energy pathway is the route by which energy travels from producer to consumer (1)\n• It includes pipelines, tankers, shipping routes, transmission grids and the chokepoints they pass through, such as the Strait of Hormuz or the Suez Canal (1)\n• Pathways are a source of vulnerability because they can be disrupted by conflict, piracy, accident or political decision — which is why diversification of routes matters as much as diversification of sources (1)` },

      { q: 'Atmospheric CO₂ rose from 315 ppm in 1958 to 421 ppm in 2023. Calculate the total increase, the percentage increase and the mean annual rate.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Increase = 421 − 315 = 106 ppm (1)\n• Percentage increase = 106 ÷ 315 × 100 = 33.7% (1)\n• Period = 2023 − 1958 = 65 years (1)\n• Mean annual rate = 106 ÷ 65 = 1.63 ppm per year (1)\n• The mean understates the present rate: increase averaged under 1 ppm per year in the 1960s and now exceeds 2.4 ppm, so the rise is accelerating and a linear mean misrepresents the trend (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires all calculations correct plus recognition of acceleration.` },

      { q: 'A country’s energy mix is: coal 38%, gas 24%, nuclear 14%, hydro 11%, wind 8%, other renewables 5%. Total consumption is 1,840 TWh. Calculate the TWh from fossil fuels and from all renewables combined.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Fossil fuels = coal 38% + gas 24% = 62% (1)\n• 0.62 × 1,840 = 1,140.8 TWh (1)\n• Renewables = hydro 11% + wind 8% + other renewables 5% = 24% (1)\n• 0.24 × 1,840 = 441.6 TWh (1)\n• Fossil fuels supply roughly 2.6 times as much as all renewables combined; note nuclear at 14% is low-carbon but not renewable, so "low-carbon" (38%) and "renewable" (24%) give very different pictures (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and the low-carbon/renewable distinction.` },

      { q: 'A forest sequesters 6.2 tonnes of carbon per hectare per year and covers 48,000 hectares. Deforestation removes 3% of the area annually. Calculate the annual sequestration before clearance and the tonnage of sequestration lost each year.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Annual sequestration = 6.2 × 48,000 (1)\n• = 297,600 tonnes of carbon per year (1)\n• Area lost annually = 0.03 × 48,000 = 1,440 hectares (1)\n• Sequestration lost = 6.2 × 1,440 = 8,928 tonnes per year (1)\n• This counts only the lost future uptake; clearance also releases the carbon already stored in the biomass and disturbed soil, which is far larger, so the total climate effect greatly exceeds the figure calculated (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct arithmetic and recognition that lost sequestration is only part of the effect.` },

      { q: 'Ocean pH fell from 8.21 in 1750 to 8.10 in 2020. Given that pH is logarithmic, calculate the percentage increase in hydrogen ion concentration and comment on the significance.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• The change in pH = 8.21 − 8.10 = 0.11 (1)\n• A logarithmic scale means H⁺ concentration changes by a factor of 10^0.11 (1)\n• 10^0.11 = 1.29 (1)\n• So acidity has increased by approximately 29% (1)\n• Significance: an apparently trivial change of 0.11 units represents a near-30% rise in acidity, which reduces carbonate ion availability for shell and skeleton formation — the logarithmic scale systematically understates the change to a non-specialist reader (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct handling of the logarithmic scale and the interpretive point.` },

      { q: 'Global emissions are 36.8 Gt CO₂ per year. A country of 68 million people emits 340 Mt. Calculate its share of global emissions and its per-capita emissions in tonnes.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 36.8 Gt = 36,800 Mt (1)\n• Share = 340 ÷ 36,800 × 100 = 0.92% (1)\n• Per capita = 340,000,000 tonnes ÷ 68,000,000 people (1)\n• = 5.0 tonnes per person per year (1)\n• The two measures support different arguments: the small national share is used to argue that unilateral action is futile, while per-capita emissions above the global mean of about 4.7 tonnes support responsibility — and territorial accounting excludes emissions embodied in imports, which would raise the figure further (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and a developed interpretive point.` },

      { q: 'A wind farm has a capacity of 640 MW and a capacity factor of 42%. Calculate its annual output in TWh and compare it with a gas plant of 640 MW running at an 85% capacity factor.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Hours in a year = 8,760 (1)\n• Wind: 640 MW × 8,760 × 0.42 = 2,354,688 MWh = 2.35 TWh (1)\n• Gas: 640 MW × 8,760 × 0.85 = 4,765,440 MWh = 4.77 TWh (1)\n• The gas plant produces roughly twice the output from the same nameplate capacity (1)\n• This is why installed capacity alone is a misleading comparison between intermittent and dispatchable sources; the wind farm also cannot choose when it generates, so system costs of storage or backup must be counted alongside output (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and the point about capacity versus output.` },

      { q: 'Explain the natural factors that cause the carbon cycle to vary over time.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Seasonal variation — Northern Hemisphere vegetation dominates the land surface, so photosynthesis exceeds respiration in summer and the reverse in winter, producing the annual oscillation visible in the Keeling curve of around 6 ppm.\n• Diurnal variation — photosynthesis ceases at night while respiration continues.\n• Volcanic activity — outgassing releases CO₂ from the slow cycle; major eruptions also inject aerosols that cool the surface and temporarily alter productivity.\n• Wildfire — releases stored biomass carbon rapidly, with regrowth reabsorbing it over decades.\n• Ocean circulation — upwelling brings carbon-rich deep water to the surface, releasing CO₂; ENSO alters this, so El Niño years show faster atmospheric CO₂ growth.\n• Solubility — cold water dissolves more CO₂, so ocean temperature changes alter the atmosphere–ocean balance.\n• Milankovitch cycles — orbital variation drives glacial cycles, with CO₂ varying between roughly 180 and 280 ppm through feedbacks involving ocean temperature and circulation.\n• Long-term tectonic control — rates of mountain building and weathering, and of subduction and outgassing.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): variation explained across several timescales with mechanisms and correct terminology.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some causes identified with partial explanation, or one timescale only.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about seasons or volcanoes.` },

      { q: 'Explain how human activity has altered the carbon cycle.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Fossil fuel combustion transfers carbon from the lithosphere — a slow-cycle store isolated for hundreds of millions of years — into the atmosphere in decades, at around 36 Gt CO₂ per year.\n• Deforestation releases biomass carbon and removes future sequestration capacity, contributing roughly 10–15% of emissions.\n• Agriculture — ploughing accelerates oxidation of soil organic matter; rice paddies and livestock emit methane; fertiliser production and use release CO₂ and N₂O.\n• Peatland drainage converts a major long-term sink into a source; UK peatlands are currently net emitters.\n• Cement production releases CO₂ chemically from limestone as well as through the fuel used, accounting for around 8% of global emissions.\n• Ocean effects — roughly a quarter of emitted CO₂ is absorbed by the ocean, causing acidification and reducing the efficiency of the sink over time.\n• Net result — atmospheric concentration has risen from about 280 ppm pre-industrial to over 420 ppm, a level not seen for several million years, and the rate of change is far faster than any natural analogue.\n• Counter-direction: afforestation, peatland restoration and carbon capture attempt to reverse some flows but operate at a small fraction of the scale.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several human influences explained accurately with quantification and reference to the transfer between slow and fast cycles.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several activities identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that burning fuel causes CO₂.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of ocean acidification.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• The ocean absorbs roughly a quarter of anthropogenic CO₂ emissions.\n• Dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid, which dissociates to release hydrogen ions, lowering pH.\n• Those hydrogen ions combine with carbonate ions, reducing the carbonate available for organisms to build shells.\n• Surface pH has fallen from about 8.21 to 8.10 since pre-industrial times — a near-30% rise in acidity on the logarithmic scale.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Calcifying organisms — corals, molluscs, pteropods, coccolithophores — find shell and skeleton formation harder and more energetically costly; existing structures may dissolve where water becomes undersaturated.\n• Coral reefs weaken and their growth rate falls, compounding bleaching from warming and threatening the ecosystems and coastal protection they provide.\n• Food web effects: pteropods and coccolithophores are near the base of many food chains, so impacts propagate upward to fisheries.\n• Economic consequences for shellfish aquaculture, already documented on the US Pacific coast, and for reef-dependent tourism and fisheries.\n• Feedback: reduced carbonate pump efficiency weakens the ocean sink, so a greater share of emissions remains in the atmosphere.\n• The change is effectively irreversible on human timescales, since restoring ocean chemistry takes tens of thousands of years.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): chemistry explained accurately and a range of ecological and economic consequences developed, including the feedback.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes or consequences covered adequately with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that the sea becomes acidic and harms sea life.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of forests in the carbon cycle.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Forests are a major carbon store, holding carbon in living biomass above and below ground, in litter, and in forest soils, which frequently hold more than the trees themselves.\n• They act as a sink through photosynthesis; the terrestrial biosphere absorbs roughly a quarter of anthropogenic emissions, with forests the dominant component.\n• Rates vary: young, fast-growing forest sequesters rapidly; old-growth forest sequesters more slowly but holds a far larger accumulated store, so the two are not interchangeable.\n• Tropical forests both store most carbon and are cleared fastest; boreal forests hold vast quantities in permafrost soils.\n• Forests influence the cycle indirectly through evapotranspiration and recycled precipitation, maintaining the conditions for their own survival — significant in Amazonia.\n• Forests become sources when cleared, burned, or when drought and warming raise respiration above photosynthesis; parts of Amazonia have been measured as net sources in recent drought years.\n• Management implications: protecting existing forest prevents emissions immediately, whereas planting delivers sequestration only over decades — so avoided deforestation and afforestation are not equivalent policies.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): treats forests as both store and flux, distinguishes forest types and ages, and recognises the source/sink switch.\n• Band 2 (3–5): explains forests as absorbing CO₂ with limited development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that trees take in carbon dioxide.` },

      { q: 'Explain the factors affecting a country’s energy security.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Domestic resource endowment — whether fossil fuels, uranium, or renewable resources such as hydro potential, insolation or wind are physically present.\n• Degree of import dependence and the diversity of suppliers; reliance on a single supplier creates leverage, as European dependence on Russian gas before 2022 demonstrated.\n• Energy pathways — vulnerability of pipelines, shipping routes and chokepoints such as the Strait of Hormuz, through which a large share of seaborne oil passes.\n• Infrastructure — grid capacity and resilience, storage, interconnectors, refining capacity.\n• Wealth and technology — capital to invest in generation, storage and efficiency.\n• Demand profile — industrial structure, climate-driven heating and cooling loads, efficiency of the building stock.\n• Political stability, both domestic and of supplier states, and the quality of regulation.\n• Policy choices — climate commitments, nuclear policy, subsidy regimes; Germany’s nuclear phase-out increased gas dependence.\n• Intermittency management as renewables grow — storage, interconnection and demand flexibility become security issues in themselves.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several factors across the dimensions of security explained with examples and their interaction recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): factors identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that countries need enough energy.` },

      { q: 'Explain the advantages and disadvantages of nuclear power as part of an energy mix.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — advantages:\n• Very low operational carbon emissions, comparable to wind over the lifecycle.\n• High energy density — a small land area and fuel volume produce very large output.\n• Dispatchable baseload, available regardless of weather, which complements intermittent renewables.\n• Fuel is cheap relative to output and can be stockpiled, so it is less exposed to short-term supply disruption than gas.\n• Supports energy independence where uranium can be secured.\n\nIndicative content — disadvantages:\n• Very high capital cost and long construction times; Hinkley Point C has seen substantial cost and schedule overruns, and financing costs dominate the price of output.\n• Radioactive waste requires isolation for tens of thousands of years, and no country has yet operated a permanent deep geological repository at scale.\n• Accident risk — Chernobyl and Fukushima had consequences out of proportion to their statistical frequency, and drive public opposition.\n• Decommissioning costs are large, uncertain and fall long after revenue ends.\n• Proliferation concerns where enrichment capability exists.\n• Inflexible output makes it a poor partner for high-renewable systems that need rapid ramping.\n• Uranium is finite and requires mining with its own impacts.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): balanced coverage with accurate technical and economic detail and examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several points each side with limited development, or unbalanced.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that nuclear is clean but dangerous.` },

      { q: 'Explain the environmental and economic issues associated with unconventional fossil fuels.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the resources:\n• Shale gas and tight oil extracted by hydraulic fracturing; tar sands in Alberta; oil shale; deepwater and Arctic reserves.\n\nIndicative content — environmental issues:\n• Fracking requires large volumes of water and produces contaminated flowback, with risks of aquifer contamination from faulty well casings.\n• Induced seismicity, which halted UK shale operations.\n• Fugitive methane emissions, significant because methane has far greater short-term warming potential than CO₂, potentially eroding the climate advantage of gas over coal.\n• Tar sands extraction destroys boreal forest and peatland, uses large quantities of water and energy, and leaves toxic tailings ponds.\n• Arctic and deepwater extraction carries high spill risk in environments where clean-up is extremely difficult.\n• Land use, traffic and noise impacts on local communities.\n\nIndicative content — economic issues:\n• High extraction cost makes viability dependent on world prices; many projects are uneconomic below a threshold oil price.\n• The US shale boom transformed its energy security and made it a net exporter, altering global markets and reducing OPEC pricing power.\n• Risk of stranded assets if climate policy tightens, since long-lived capital investment may not recover its cost.\n• Local economic benefits in employment and tax revenue, against costs to tourism, agriculture and property values.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): both environmental and economic issues covered accurately with named examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): one dimension covered adequately with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that fracking is bad for the environment.` },

      { q: 'Explain how carbon capture and storage works and the barriers to its widespread adoption.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — how it works:\n• Capture — CO₂ is separated from flue gases at a power station or industrial plant (post-combustion), or fuel is converted before burning (pre-combustion), or burned in pure oxygen (oxyfuel).\n• Transport — the captured CO₂ is compressed and moved by pipeline or ship.\n• Storage — injected into depleted oil and gas fields or deep saline aquifers, typically over 800 m down where pressure keeps it in a dense phase, trapped beneath impermeable cap rock.\n• Direct air capture removes CO₂ from the atmosphere itself, allowing negative emissions but at far greater energy cost because atmospheric concentration is so low.\n\nIndicative content — barriers:\n• Energy penalty — capture consumes 10–30% of a plant’s output, raising fuel use and cost per unit delivered.\n• High capital cost with no revenue stream unless carbon pricing is high enough or subsidy is provided.\n• Limited deployment to date — global capacity remains a tiny fraction of emissions despite decades of development.\n• Storage site availability and public acceptance; concerns about leakage and induced seismicity.\n• Monitoring liability over centuries, and who bears it.\n• Moral hazard argument — that CCS is used to justify continued fossil fuel use rather than to decarbonise hard-to-abate sectors such as cement and steel, where alternatives genuinely do not exist.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): process explained accurately through all stages with several substantive barriers, including economic and political ones.\n• Band 2 (3–5): outline of the process with some barriers identified.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that CO₂ is buried underground.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of different players in the global energy system.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• OPEC and OPEC+ — coordinate production quotas among member states to influence world oil prices; hold a large share of proven reserves, giving collective pricing power that varies with non-OPEC supply.\n• Transnational energy corporations — explore, extract, refine and distribute; control technology and capital, and their investment decisions shape future supply for decades.\n• National governments — set energy policy, subsidies, taxation and climate targets; own national oil companies which control most global reserves.\n• Consumers — demand patterns and efficiency choices; rising affluence in emerging economies is the main driver of demand growth.\n• International organisations — the IEA providing data and coordinating emergency stocks; the UNFCCC and COP process setting the framework for emissions reduction.\n• Environmental NGOs and campaign groups — shaping public opinion, litigation and divestment pressure, which has affected corporate investment decisions.\n• Financial institutions — lending and insurance decisions increasingly determine whether fossil projects proceed.\n• Key point: interests conflict, the system has no central authority, and energy security objectives of individual states frequently conflict with collective climate objectives.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): a range of players with their motivations and means of influence explained, and conflicts between them recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): players identified with partial explanation of their role.\n• Band 1 (1–2): lists organisations with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the carbon cycle and the water cycle are linked.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Photosynthesis links them directly — plants take up CO₂ through stomata and lose water vapour through the same openings, so carbon uptake and transpiration are physically coupled.\n• Vegetation therefore drives both the terrestrial carbon sink and a major flow in the water cycle; the Amazon recycles precipitation through transpiration, sustaining the rainfall its own carbon store depends on.\n• Chemical weathering requires water — CO₂ dissolves in rainwater to form carbonic acid, so the rate of the long-term carbon sink depends on precipitation and temperature.\n• Ocean circulation transports both heat and dissolved carbon; the solubility pump depends on water temperature and thermohaline circulation.\n• Soil moisture controls decomposition rates: waterlogged peat preserves carbon, and drainage releases it, so a change in the water cycle directly changes the carbon store.\n• Feedbacks: warming from higher CO₂ intensifies the water cycle; drought reduces photosynthesis, weakening the carbon sink and raising atmospheric CO₂ further.\n• Permafrost thaw releases both water and long-stored carbon as CO₂ and methane.\n• Human activity alters both simultaneously — deforestation reduces transpiration and releases carbon; irrigation alters both stores.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several genuine linkages explained with mechanisms and at least one feedback developed.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some linkages identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that both cycles involve plants.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of changing global energy demand.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Population growth, concentrated in lower-income countries.\n• Economic development and industrialisation — China and India account for much of the growth in global demand since 2000.\n• Rising affluence changing consumption: vehicle ownership, air conditioning, appliances and air travel.\n• Urbanisation increasing per-capita energy use through construction, transport and services.\n• Technology — digitalisation and data centres creating substantial new demand; electrification of heat and transport shifting demand between vectors.\n• Counteracting factors — efficiency improvement, deindustrialisation in high-income countries, and the outsourcing of energy-intensive production, which reduces territorial demand without reducing consumption-based demand.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Rising emissions and consequent climate change, unless decoupled through low-carbon supply.\n• Resource depletion and the opening of more marginal, higher-impact reserves.\n• Price volatility affecting inflation and living standards; fuel poverty where prices rise faster than incomes.\n• Geopolitical consequences — competition for resources and pathways, and leverage for supplier states.\n• Infrastructure investment requirements, particularly grid capacity for electrification.\n• Environmental impacts of extraction and of the mineral demand created by the energy transition itself — lithium, cobalt, copper.\n• Equity dimension: demand growth is largely in countries with low historic emissions and low per-capita use, which frames the fairness of restricting it.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): causes and consequences both covered thoroughly with accurate examples and recognition of the equity dimension or of counteracting factors.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound coverage but unbalanced or with limited exemplification.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that demand is rising because of population.` },

      { q: 'Explain the impacts of the global energy transition on people and places.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — positive impacts:\n• Employment in renewable manufacturing, installation and maintenance, frequently in regions that had lost industrial employment.\n• Improved air quality where coal is displaced, with substantial health benefits; air pollution causes millions of premature deaths annually.\n• Energy access through decentralised solar in areas without grid connection, particularly in sub-Saharan Africa, delivering lighting, refrigeration and connectivity.\n• Reduced import dependence and exposure to fossil price volatility.\n• Falling costs — solar and wind are now the cheapest new generation in most markets.\n\nIndicative content — negative impacts:\n• Job losses in coal, oil and gas regions, often geographically concentrated and with limited alternative employment — the basis of "just transition" policy.\n• Mineral demand: lithium, cobalt, nickel and rare earths, with extraction impacts and labour concerns, notably cobalt mining in the DRC; new dependencies replace old ones.\n• Land use conflict over wind and solar sites, and visual and ecological impacts.\n• Costs of transition falling regressively where funded through consumer bills.\n• Stranded assets and fiscal shock for petro-states dependent on hydrocarbon revenue.\n• Grid instability and the cost of storage and backup as intermittent share rises.\n\nIndicative content — distribution:\n• Benefits and costs fall on different people and places, and rarely on the same ones — which is the central political difficulty of the transition.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): balanced coverage with specific examples and explicit attention to who gains and who loses.\n• Band 2 (4–6): impacts identified with partial explanation or one-sided coverage.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that renewables are good for the environment.` },

      { q: 'Explain the significance of feedback mechanisms in the carbon cycle.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — positive (amplifying) feedbacks:\n• Permafrost thaw — warming releases CO₂ and methane from soils frozen for millennia, causing further warming; the store is of the order of 1,400 GtC, far exceeding the atmosphere.\n• Albedo feedback — ice loss exposes darker surfaces, increasing absorption and warming, indirectly accelerating carbon release.\n• Forest dieback — drought and fire convert forests from sink to source; parts of Amazonia have been measured as net sources.\n• Ocean solubility — warmer water dissolves less CO₂, so the ocean sink weakens as warming proceeds.\n• Methane hydrates in continental shelf sediments potentially destabilised by warming.\n• Wildfire frequency increasing with heat and drought, releasing stored carbon.\n\nIndicative content — negative (stabilising) feedbacks:\n• CO₂ fertilisation — higher concentrations can increase photosynthesis, though limited by nutrient and water availability.\n• Silicate weathering — warmer, wetter conditions accelerate weathering, drawing down CO₂, but over hundreds of thousands of years, so it is irrelevant on policy timescales.\n• Increased vegetation growth in warming high latitudes.\n\nIndicative content — significance:\n• Feedbacks determine climate sensitivity — how much warming a given emission produces — and are the principal source of uncertainty in projections.\n• They create the possibility of tipping points beyond which change becomes self-sustaining and effectively irreversible.\n• Because amplifying feedbacks operate on decadal timescales and stabilising ones on geological timescales, the system is currently biased toward amplification.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): distinguishes positive and negative feedbacks accurately with several examples and explains why they matter for projections and tipping points.\n• Band 2 (4–6): some feedbacks described with the distinction partly maintained.\n• Band 1 (1–3): isolated examples with the term used loosely.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for variations in energy security between countries.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical geography:\n• Resource endowment — Norway and Saudi Arabia have large hydrocarbon reserves; Japan has almost none and imports over 90% of its primary energy.\n• Renewable potential — Iceland’s geothermal and hydro give near-total domestic supply; Norway’s relief and precipitation give abundant hydro.\n• Climate — heating and cooling demand, and insolation for solar.\n\nIndicative content — economic and technological:\n• Wealth determines capacity to invest in generation, grid, storage and efficiency, and to pay world prices during shortage.\n• Technology and expertise to exploit difficult resources — the US shale revolution depended on horizontal drilling and fracturing techniques.\n• Industrial structure determines demand intensity.\n\nIndicative content — political:\n• Relations with supplier and transit states; Ukraine and central European dependence on Russian gas and pipeline routes.\n• Domestic stability and governance quality affecting investment and maintenance.\n• Policy choices — Germany’s simultaneous nuclear phase-out and coal reduction increased gas dependence; France’s nuclear programme reduced import reliance.\n• Membership of alliances and access to interconnectors and shared reserves.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• Security is relational rather than absolute — it depends on who a country depends on and how substitutable that dependence is, not only on what it possesses.\n• Resource wealth does not guarantee security: countries with abundant reserves can be insecure through conflict, underinvestment or lack of refining capacity.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers physical, economic and political factors with contrasting named examples and recognises that security is relational.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several factors with partial explanation or limited exemplification.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that some countries have more resources.` },

      { q: 'Explain how carbon emissions can be reduced at different scales.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — international:\n• The UNFCCC and Paris Agreement, with nationally determined contributions and the 1.5–2°C goal; the mechanism relies on peer pressure and transparency rather than enforcement.\n• Carbon markets and offsetting; border carbon adjustment to prevent leakage.\n• Technology transfer and climate finance to lower-income countries.\n\nIndicative content — national:\n• Carbon pricing — taxes or emissions trading schemes — making emissions costly and letting the market find reductions.\n• Legally binding targets, as in the UK Climate Change Act and its carbon budgets.\n• Regulation — vehicle emission standards, building regulations, phase-out dates for coal generation and petrol vehicles.\n• Subsidy and contracts for difference supporting renewable deployment; removal of fossil fuel subsidies.\n• Infrastructure investment in grid, rail and charging networks.\n\nIndicative content — local and individual:\n• Local authority planning requirements, district heating, low-traffic neighbourhoods, retrofit programmes.\n• Business action — corporate targets, supply chain requirements, and investor pressure.\n• Individual choices — diet, travel, home efficiency; significant in aggregate but constrained by the infrastructure and prices individuals face.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Scales interact: individual action is enabled or blocked by national policy, and national action is shaped by international agreement.\n• Emphasis on individual responsibility can divert attention from the structural changes that deliver most reduction — a point worth credit.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): strategies covered at all three scales with named examples and recognition of how scales interact.\n• Band 2 (4–6): strategies at two scales with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): lists actions such as recycling or using less electricity.` },

      { q: 'Explain the concept of the carbon budget and its implications for policy.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the concept:\n• A carbon budget is the cumulative quantity of CO₂ that can still be emitted while retaining a given probability of limiting warming to a stated level.\n• It follows from the near-linear relationship between cumulative emissions and peak warming — what matters is the total emitted, not the rate in any one year.\n• The remaining budget for a 50% chance of limiting warming to 1.5°C is of the order of 250 GtCO₂ from the early 2020s, around six years of current emissions.\n\nIndicative content — implications:\n• Delay is costly in a way annual targets conceal: emitting more now requires steeper cuts later, or overshoot and reliance on unproven removal.\n• It reframes the question from "when do we reach net zero" to "how much do we emit on the way", making the shape of the trajectory decisive.\n• Distribution — if the budget is finite, allocating it between countries is unavoidable and contested; historic responsibility, per-capita equity and capacity give very different allocations.\n• Stranded assets — known fossil reserves greatly exceed the budget, so most cannot be burned, with implications for valuation and investment.\n• Negative emissions technologies effectively extend the budget but are unproven at scale, so relying on them transfers risk to the future.\n• Uncertainty — the budget depends on climate sensitivity and on non-CO₂ forcings, so it is a probabilistic rather than exact figure, which complicates its use in policy.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): concept explained accurately with quantification, and several policy implications developed including equity and stranded assets.\n• Band 2 (4–6): concept understood with limited implications drawn.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that there is a limit on emissions.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which renewable energy can replace fossil fuels.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Cost has fallen dramatically — solar and onshore wind are now the cheapest sources of new generation in most markets, so the economic barrier has largely gone.\n• Resource is abundant: solar energy reaching the Earth vastly exceeds human demand.\n• Deployment is accelerating; several countries already generate the majority of electricity from renewables, and Iceland and Norway effectively all of it.\n• Storage costs are falling rapidly, and grid interconnection, demand flexibility and diversity of sources reduce the intermittency problem.\n• Electrification of heat and transport allows renewables to displace fossil use beyond the power sector.\n\nIndicative content — limits:\n• Intermittency remains the central difficulty: matching supply to demand requires storage, backup or interconnection at a scale not yet built, and seasonal storage in particular is unsolved.\n• Electricity is only around a fifth of final energy; industrial heat, aviation, shipping, and chemical feedstocks are hard to electrify.\n• Cement and steel production emit CO₂ chemically, not only from fuel, so renewable electricity alone does not decarbonise them.\n• Mineral requirements — lithium, cobalt, copper, rare earths — create new supply chains, environmental impacts and dependencies, with extraction concentrated in few countries.\n• Land use and public opposition constrain siting; grid infrastructure lags generation.\n• Pace: fossil fuels still supply around 80% of global primary energy, and historical energy transitions have taken many decades.\n\nJudgement: renewables can plausibly replace fossil fuels in electricity generation and, through electrification, in much of heat and light transport; complete replacement in hard-to-abate sectors requires hydrogen, CCS or nuclear alongside — so the realistic answer is substantial but not total replacement by renewables alone, with the constraint being pace and system integration rather than resource.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment distinguishing sectors and timescales, with accurate evidence and a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): advantages and limitations with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes renewables with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that international agreements are the most effective way of reducing carbon emissions.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Climate change is a global commons problem: emissions anywhere affect everyone, so unilateral action risks leakage as production relocates.\n• Agreements create shared expectations and reporting, making national commitments visible and comparable; the Paris Agreement secured near-universal participation where Kyoto did not.\n• They mobilise climate finance and technology transfer to countries that could not otherwise act.\n• They set a direction that shapes private investment, since firms plan around expected policy.\n• The ratchet mechanism has produced successively stronger NDCs.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• No enforcement — Paris commitments are voluntary, and current NDCs collectively fall well short of 1.5°C; there is no penalty for missing them.\n• Free-rider incentives persist; withdrawal is possible, as the US demonstrated.\n• Negotiation is slow and produces lowest-common-denominator outcomes.\n• Much effective action has been national or sub-national — the UK Climate Change Act, EU ETS, Chinese renewable deployment, US state-level policy — and was driven by domestic politics and cost, not treaty obligation.\n• Technology cost reduction, particularly in solar and batteries, has arguably done more to reduce emissions than any agreement, by making low-carbon options cheaper.\n• Market forces and investor pressure increasingly constrain fossil investment independently of policy.\n\nJudgement: agreements are necessary but not sufficient — they provide the framework, legitimacy and finance within which national action occurs, but delivery depends on domestic policy and technology cost. A defensible position is that they are the most effective coordinating mechanism while not being the most effective direct lever.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment comparing agreements against alternatives with accurate evidence and a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): strengths and weaknesses of agreements identified with partial evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes Paris or Kyoto with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the importance of the ocean in regulating the global carbon cycle.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — evidence of importance:\n• The ocean holds around 38,000 GtC, roughly 45 times the atmospheric store, so small proportional exchanges have large atmospheric effects.\n• It has absorbed roughly a quarter to a third of anthropogenic CO₂ emissions, substantially slowing atmospheric accumulation.\n• The solubility pump transfers CO₂ to depth in cold high-latitude water sinking as part of thermohaline circulation.\n• The biological and carbonate pumps transfer carbon to deep water and ultimately to sediment, the pathway into the slow cycle.\n• Without ocean uptake, atmospheric concentration and warming would be substantially greater than observed.\n\nIndicative content — qualifications:\n• The terrestrial biosphere absorbs a comparable share, so the ocean is not uniquely important.\n• The lithosphere is by far the largest store, though it exchanges very slowly.\n• Ocean uptake is not costless — it causes acidification, so the regulation is achieved by degrading the ocean itself.\n• The sink is weakening: warmer water dissolves less CO₂, and stratification reduces the mixing that carries carbon to depth, so the ocean’s regulatory capacity declines as warming proceeds.\n• Deep ocean uptake operates over centuries, so it does not moderate change on policy-relevant timescales.\n• Human influence on ocean processes is limited, so its importance is explanatory rather than a lever for action.\n\nJudgement: the ocean is the single most important long-term regulator of atmospheric CO₂ and has substantially moderated observed warming, but its capacity is finite, declining, and purchased at the cost of acidification — so its importance lies in explaining the system rather than in offering a solution.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): assesses importance against other stores and fluxes with quantification, and recognises the weakening sink and acidification trade-off.\n• Band 2 (5–8): describes ocean processes with some assessment of importance.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that the ocean absorbs CO₂.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that energy security concerns will always outweigh climate commitments.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Energy security is immediate and politically salient; climate consequences are diffuse and delayed, so governments discount them.\n• The 2022 European gas crisis saw coal generation restarted and new LNG infrastructure commissioned, despite climate targets — revealed preference under pressure.\n• Germany extended coal use after the Russian gas cut-off; several countries reversed nuclear phase-out decisions.\n• Fuel poverty and industrial competitiveness create strong domestic pressure against measures that raise prices.\n• Petro-states have fiscal dependence on hydrocarbon revenue that climate action directly threatens.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• The two increasingly align: domestic renewables reduce import dependence, so decarbonisation is itself an energy security strategy — the 2022 crisis accelerated EU renewable deployment as well as coal use.\n• Cost has shifted the calculus: renewables are now the cheapest new generation, so security and climate objectives point the same way for new investment.\n• Legally binding targets and independent advisory bodies constrain short-term reversal in some jurisdictions.\n• Climate impacts themselves threaten energy security — drought reducing hydro output, heat reducing thermal plant efficiency and grid capacity.\n• Investor and insurer behaviour increasingly prices climate risk regardless of government preference.\n\nJudgement: security dominates in acute crises and in the short term, but the conflict is weakening structurally as low-carbon options become the cheaper and more secure choice; a defensible conclusion distinguishes short-run emergency behaviour from long-run investment direction, and notes that "always" is too strong.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with contemporary evidence, distinguishing timescales and engaging with the absolute claim in the question.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): asserts that countries care more about energy or climate with little support.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that human activity has fundamentally disrupted the carbon cycle. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Atmospheric CO₂ has risen from about 280 ppm pre-industrial to over 420 ppm, a level not seen for several million years.\n• The rate of change is the critical point: the rise has occurred in roughly two centuries, whereas comparable natural changes took tens of thousands of years, so systems cannot adjust.\n• Fossil fuel combustion transfers slow-cycle carbon, isolated for hundreds of millions of years, into the fast cycle — a transfer with no natural analogue at this rate.\n• Land use change has converted major sinks into sources: deforestation, peatland drainage, soil carbon loss from agriculture.\n• Ocean acidification has increased hydrogen ion concentration by around 30%, altering ocean chemistry on a timescale of centuries rather than the tens of thousands of years required for natural buffering.\n• Amplifying feedbacks — permafrost thaw, forest dieback, weakening sinks — raise the possibility that change becomes self-sustaining.\n• Humans now move more carbon annually than volcanic outgassing by roughly two orders of magnitude.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• The absolute quantities are small against the largest stores: the lithosphere holds vastly more carbon, and cumulative emissions are a tiny fraction of it.\n• The cycle continues to function — photosynthesis, respiration, weathering and ocean exchange all operate; the system is perturbed rather than broken.\n• Natural sinks still absorb roughly half of annual emissions, demonstrating continued regulatory capacity.\n• The Earth has experienced far higher CO₂ concentrations in the geological past without the cycle ceasing to operate.\n• Negative feedbacks such as silicate weathering will eventually restore balance, so the disruption is temporary on geological timescales.\n• "Fundamentally disrupted" may overstate: the flows and stores remain the same, only their magnitudes have changed.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Everything turns on what "fundamentally" and "disrupted" mean, and on the timescale chosen. On geological timescales the perturbation is modest and self-correcting; on human and ecological timescales it is unprecedented and consequential.\n• The strongest case rests on rate rather than magnitude — the system can accommodate the quantity, but not at this speed, and it is rate that determines whether ecosystems and societies can adapt.\n• A distinction worth drawing: the cycle is not disrupted in the sense of ceasing to function, but the equilibrium it had maintained through the Holocene has been decisively broken.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning that interrogates the terms of the question; wide range of accurate quantified evidence; resolves by timescale or by distinguishing rate from magnitude; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation with accurate support and a clear judgement, less fully resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes human impacts on the carbon cycle with limited evaluation; evidence generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of emissions and climate change with little evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the energy strategy of one country you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a named country. Germany is used below; credit any appropriate case study (France, China, the UK, Norway, Denmark) at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — the strategy:\n• The Energiewende, launched formally in 2010 and accelerated after Fukushima in 2011: rapid renewable expansion, nuclear phase-out completed in 2023, and a coal exit legislated for 2038.\n• Mechanism: feed-in tariffs guaranteeing prices for renewable generation, funded through a levy on consumer electricity bills; grid expansion to connect northern wind to southern industry.\n• Targets: 80% renewable electricity by 2030 and climate neutrality by 2045.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Renewable share of electricity rose from under 7% in 2000 to over 50% in recent years — a transformation of the generation mix.\n• German demand was instrumental in driving down global solar PV costs, a benefit accruing worldwide rather than only domestically.\n• Substantial employment created in renewable industries.\n• Nuclear phase-out delivered as promised, honouring a long-standing democratic commitment.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Sequencing: phasing out nuclear before coal meant low-carbon capacity was replaced first, so emissions fell more slowly than they need have; lignite generation persisted.\n• Increased gas dependence, overwhelmingly on Russia, which became an acute security failure in 2022 and forced a return to coal.\n• Among the highest household electricity prices in Europe, with the levy falling regressively while energy-intensive industry was exempted.\n• Grid expansion has lagged badly, with north–south transmission delayed by local opposition, so wind is curtailed while southern plants run.\n• Intermittency managed partly by exporting surpluses and importing from neighbours, including French nuclear — so the achievement depends on the wider European system.\n• Transport and heating decarbonisation has lagged far behind electricity.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — emissions reduction, security, affordability, deliverability, and wider contribution — noting sharply different performance against each.\n• Highly successful at deploying renewables and at driving global cost reduction; questionable on emissions per euro spent, on security, and on equity.\n• Timescale matters: judged against 2045 the strategy may yet succeed; judged against the last decade its sequencing was costly.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate country-specific evidence including figures and dates, genuine engagement with trade-offs and sequencing, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the strategy with some evaluative comment; detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of energy policy with little locational detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.3': {
    name: 'Global Migration',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Define the terms migration, immigration and emigration, and state what net migration measures.', marks: 4, command: 'Define', markScheme: `• Migration is the movement of people from one place to another, involving a change of permanent residence (1)\n• Immigration is movement into a country or area (1)\n• Emigration is movement out of a country or area (1)\n• Net migration is immigration minus emigration — a positive figure means the population is gaining through migration, a negative figure that it is losing (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between voluntary and forced migration, giving one example of each.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Voluntary migration is undertaken by choice, usually to improve circumstances — economic opportunity, study, retirement or family reunion (1)\n• Example: EU citizens moving to the UK for work after 2004 accession (1)\n• Forced migration occurs where people have no realistic choice but to leave — conflict, persecution, disaster or development projects (1)\n• Example: Syrians displaced after 2011, or those displaced by the Three Gorges Dam; in practice the distinction is a spectrum, since economic desperation constrains choice (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the legal difference between a refugee, an asylum seeker and an economic migrant.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A refugee is defined by the 1951 Refugee Convention as a person outside their country of nationality with a well-founded fear of persecution on grounds of race, religion, nationality, political opinion or membership of a social group, and unable or unwilling to return (1)\n• An asylum seeker has applied for refugee status and is awaiting a decision, so the status is temporary and procedural (1)\n• An economic migrant moves primarily to improve their standard of living and has no protection under the Convention (1)\n• The distinction matters because refugees have a right to non-refoulement — not to be returned to danger — while economic migrants may be lawfully removed; the categories are often conflated in public debate (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by an internally displaced person and why their situation differs from that of a refugee.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An internally displaced person has been forced to leave their home but has not crossed an international border, remaining within their own country (1)\n• IDPs outnumber refugees globally, at over 70 million (1)\n• They fall outside the Refugee Convention and remain the legal responsibility of the state that may itself have displaced them, so international agencies require that state’s consent to assist — which frequently makes protection harder than for refugees (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain Lee’s push-pull model of migration and state one limitation.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Push factors are negative conditions in the origin driving people away — unemployment, conflict, drought, persecution (1)\n• Pull factors are positive conditions in the destination attracting people — jobs, safety, services, family networks (1)\n• Intervening obstacles lie between them — distance, cost, borders, visa regimes, physical barriers (1)\n• Limitation: it treats migrants as fully informed rational actors, whereas decisions rest on perception, rumour and social networks; it also underplays that the poorest are often too poor to migrate at all, so migration rises rather than falls with initial development (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a migration corridor and give one example.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A migration corridor is a well-established route between a specific origin and destination along which large and persistent flows occur (1)\n• Corridors form and persist because of colonial or linguistic ties, geographical proximity, labour demand and the migrant networks that reduce cost and risk for those who follow (1)\n• Examples: Mexico to the United States, the largest single corridor; Bangladesh to India; Turkey to Germany; India to the Gulf states (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by chain migration.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Chain migration is the process by which established migrants facilitate the migration of others from their home community (1)\n• Early migrants provide information, accommodation, employment contacts and financial support, sharply reducing the cost and risk for those following (1)\n• It explains why migration concentrates in particular destination neighbourhoods and originates from particular villages or regions, and why flows persist after the original economic driver has weakened (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a remittance and foreign direct investment as financial flows to a developing country.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Remittances are transfers of money from migrants working abroad to households in their country of origin (1)\n• They go directly to families and are typically spent on food, housing, healthcare and education, so they reduce poverty immediately and reach the poorest (1)\n• FDI is investment by foreign firms in productive assets, directed by corporate strategy toward profitable sectors (1)\n• Remittances are more stable and counter-cyclical — rising when the home economy struggles — whereas FDI is volatile and concentrated in a few countries and sectors; global remittances now substantially exceed official development aid (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by brain drain and brain gain.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Brain drain is the emigration of skilled and educated workers from a country, which loses the public investment made in their training — acute in healthcare, where over half of some African countries’ trained doctors work abroad (1)\n• Brain gain is the corresponding benefit to the receiving country, which acquires skilled labour without bearing the training cost (1)\n• Brain circulation describes the return flow — migrants returning with enhanced skills, capital and international contacts, as in the Indian and Chinese technology sectors, which complicates the simple loss narrative (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a dependency ratio and why migration affects it.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The dependency ratio compares the population aged under 15 and over 64 with the working-age population between them (1)\n• Migrants are disproportionately young adults of working age, so immigration lowers the dependency ratio in the destination and raises it in the origin (1)\n• This is why ageing countries look to immigration to sustain the tax base and workforce — though the effect is temporary, since migrants themselves age (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between assimilation and multiculturalism as approaches to integration.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Assimilation expects migrants to adopt the language, customs and values of the host society, with distinct cultural identity diminishing over time (1)\n• France is often cited, where public institutions are formally secular and ethnic monitoring is not conducted (1)\n• Multiculturalism accepts that distinct cultural identities persist and should be accommodated within a shared civic framework (1)\n• Canada and, historically, the UK are cited; critics argue multiculturalism risks parallel communities, while critics of assimilation argue it demands erasure of identity and can itself produce exclusion (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a diaspora and how diasporas influence their country of origin.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A diaspora is a population dispersed from its original homeland while retaining a connection to it (1)\n• Influence through remittances, which can be a major share of the origin country’s GDP, and through investment and business networks (1)\n• Also through political influence — lobbying host governments, funding parties or movements at home — and through transfer of skills, ideas and social norms on return or through contact (1)` },

      { q: 'International migrants numbered 173 million in 2000 and 281 million in 2020, against a world population of 7.8 billion in 2020. Calculate the percentage increase in migrants and the proportion of world population who are international migrants.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Increase = 281 − 173 = 108 million (1)\n• Percentage increase = 108 ÷ 173 × 100 = 62.4% (1)\n• Proportion of world population = 281 ÷ 7,800 × 100 (1)\n• = 3.6% (1)\n• The two figures support opposite impressions: absolute numbers have risen sharply, but the share of humanity that migrates internationally remains small and has changed little — which is why choice of measure matters in this debate (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and the interpretive contrast.` },

      { q: 'A country of 12 million receives remittances of $7.8 billion against a GDP of $31 billion. Calculate remittances as a percentage of GDP and per head of population.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Percentage of GDP = 7.8 ÷ 31 × 100 (1)\n• = 25.2% (1)\n• Per head = $7,800,000,000 ÷ 12,000,000 (1)\n• = $650 per person per year (1)\n• At a quarter of GDP the economy is heavily dependent on remittances, which is stabilising in a crisis but creates exposure to conditions in destination countries and to exchange rates — and may mask weak domestic job creation (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and a developed interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Net migration to a country was +45,000, +62,000, +38,000, +71,000 and +54,000 over five years. Calculate the mean, the median and the range, and state which best summarises the data.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mean = (45 + 62 + 38 + 71 + 54) ÷ 5 = 270 ÷ 5 = 54,000 (1)\n• Ordered: 38, 45, 54, 62, 71 — median = 54,000 (1)\n• Range = 71,000 − 38,000 = 33,000 (1)\n• Mean and median coincide, so the distribution is symmetrical with no distorting outlier (1)\n• Either measure summarises the central tendency well here, but the range shows substantial year-to-year variation — almost two-thirds of the mean — so a single average conceals volatility relevant to planning services (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires all three statistics and a reasoned comment.` },

      { q: 'A city’s population is 18% foreign-born, compared with 9% nationally. The city has 640,000 residents. Calculate the number of foreign-born residents and how many more this is than the national proportion would predict.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Foreign-born = 0.18 × 640,000 = 115,200 (1)\n• At the national rate = 0.09 × 640,000 = 57,600 (1)\n• Difference = 115,200 − 57,600 = 57,600 (1)\n• The city has exactly double the national proportion, so 57,600 more foreign-born residents than the national rate would predict (1)\n• This reflects the concentration of migration in cities, driven by labour demand and established networks; the aggregate figure conceals further concentration within particular neighbourhoods (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Of 6.8 million refugees from one country, 3.6 million are hosted by a neighbouring state, 1.1 million elsewhere in the region, and 0.9 million in Europe. Calculate the percentage in each and comment on the pattern.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Neighbouring state: 3.6 ÷ 6.8 × 100 = 52.9% (1)\n• Elsewhere in the region: 1.1 ÷ 6.8 × 100 = 16.2% (1)\n• Europe: 0.9 ÷ 6.8 × 100 = 13.2% (1)\n• Around 69% remain within the region, and the remainder — 1.2 million, about 17.6% — are elsewhere or unaccounted for (1)\n• The pattern contradicts the common perception that refugees predominantly reach high-income countries: most remain close to the country of origin, in states with far fewer resources to host them (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires accurate percentages and the interpretive point.` },

      { q: 'A migrant earns $2,400 a month abroad against $310 at home, and sends 35% of earnings as remittances while paying $180 a month for accommodation. Calculate the monthly remittance and the amount retained after accommodation.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Remittance = 0.35 × 2,400 = $840 per month (1)\n• Remaining income = 2,400 − 840 = $1,560 (1)\n• After accommodation = 1,560 − 180 = $1,380 (1)\n• The remittance alone is 2.7 times the wage available at home, which is the calculation that makes migration rational despite its costs (1)\n• The figures exclude the substantial costs not shown — travel, recruitment fees and often debt, visa costs, and the non-financial cost of family separation — so the net benefit is overstated (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct arithmetic and recognition of the excluded costs.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes of international migration.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — economic:\n• Wage differentials between countries are the single strongest predictor of flows; a worker can multiply earnings several times by moving.\n• Unemployment and underemployment at origin; labour demand at destination, often in sectors the resident population avoids — agriculture, care, construction, hospitality.\n• Active recruitment by destination states and employers, historically through guest-worker schemes.\n\nIndicative content — social and political:\n• Conflict and persecution producing forced migration — Syria, Ukraine, Afghanistan, Myanmar.\n• Family reunion, a major legal route in most high-income countries.\n• Education, with international students an increasingly significant flow.\n• Networks — chain migration lowering cost and risk for successive movers.\n\nIndicative content — environmental:\n• Drought, flooding, sea-level rise and disaster displacing populations; usually internal in the first instance, and interacting with poverty rather than acting alone.\n\nIndicative content — enabling factors:\n• Cheaper transport and communications; visa regimes and free-movement areas; smuggling networks where legal routes are closed.\n• The migration hump: initial development increases emigration because more people can afford to move, so rising prosperity does not immediately reduce flows.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): causes covered across several categories with accurate examples and enabling factors recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several causes identified with partial explanation, largely economic.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated push and pull factors listed.` },

      { q: 'Explain the impacts of international migration on countries of origin.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — positive:\n• Remittances reduce poverty directly, funding food, housing, healthcare and school fees, and are more stable than aid or FDI; in several countries they exceed a fifth of GDP.\n• Reduced pressure on employment, housing and services where unemployment is high.\n• Return migration brings skills, capital and international business contacts — brain circulation.\n• Diaspora investment and trade links.\n• Social remittances — transfer of ideas, including on education and the position of women.\n\nIndicative content — negative:\n• Brain drain — loss of skilled workers whose training was publicly funded, most damaging in health and education.\n• Loss of young working-age adults raises the dependency ratio and can leave an ageing rural population.\n• Family separation, with children raised by grandparents and long-term social costs.\n• Depopulation of rural areas, closing schools and services.\n• Remittance dependency — exposure to destination-country recession and exchange rates, and possible reduced pressure for domestic reform.\n• Inflation in local property markets where remittances concentrate.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): balanced coverage with specific examples and quantification where possible.\n• Band 2 (3–5): impacts identified, unbalanced or with limited development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that countries lose workers and gain money.` },

      { q: 'Explain the impacts of international migration on host countries.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — economic:\n• Fills labour shortages, particularly in health and social care, agriculture, construction and hospitality; the NHS depends substantially on internationally trained staff.\n• Migrants are concentrated in working ages, so they tend to be net fiscal contributors, improving the dependency ratio and tax base.\n• Entrepreneurship and business formation rates are typically above average.\n• Contested effects on wages: evidence suggests small overall effects, with possible downward pressure at the lowest end of the wage distribution in specific sectors.\n\nIndicative content — social and cultural:\n• Cultural enrichment — food, music, festivals, languages — and demographic renewal of declining areas.\n• Pressure on housing, schools and health services where arrival is rapid and funding does not follow, which is a question of planning as much as of numbers.\n• Segregation and, where integration fails, parallel communities; conversely, well-integrated diverse cities function successfully.\n• Political tension and the rise of anti-immigration politics, which is partly a response to the pace and visibility of change rather than to aggregate economic effects.\n\nIndicative content — distribution:\n• Benefits are national and diffuse while costs are local and concentrated, which is why local opposition can coexist with net national gain — a key analytical point.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): balanced coverage of economic, social and political impacts with examples and attention to distribution.\n• Band 2 (3–5): impacts listed with partial explanation or one-sided.\n• Band 1 (1–2): asserts that migrants take jobs or do jobs others will not.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for the growth of international migration since 1950.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Post-war labour shortages in Western Europe prompted active recruitment — the UK from the Caribbean and South Asia, Germany’s Gastarbeiter programme from Turkey.\n• Decolonisation created ties, language links and legal rights of entry between former colonies and metropoles.\n• Widening income gaps between regions as growth diverged, increasing the incentive to move.\n• Falling transport costs and time — air travel making intercontinental movement affordable.\n• Communications — telephone, then internet and mobile money — making it possible to maintain family ties and send remittances, reducing the psychological and practical cost of migrating.\n• Established networks producing self-sustaining chain migration.\n• Free movement areas, notably the EU and its 2004 enlargement, creating large legal flows.\n• Oil wealth creating enormous labour demand in the Gulf from the 1970s, drawing South and Southeast Asian workers.\n• Conflict and state collapse — the Cold War, the Balkans, Syria, Afghanistan — producing successive refugee movements.\n• Growth of international education and of skilled labour markets.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several distinct drivers explained with historical examples and periodisation.\n• Band 2 (3–5): some reasons identified with limited development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that travel became easier.` },

      { q: 'Explain how migration policies attempt to manage international migration.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — restrictive measures:\n• Visa requirements, quotas and caps on numbers.\n• Points-based systems selecting by skill, salary, language and qualification — Australia, Canada, and the UK since 2021.\n• Physical barriers and enhanced border enforcement; biometric and database controls.\n• Externalisation — agreements with transit countries to prevent departure, such as the EU–Turkey deal of 2016, and offshore processing arrangements.\n• Employer sanctions and restrictions on access to benefits and services.\n\nIndicative content — enabling measures:\n• Free movement areas such as the EU and ECOWAS.\n• Seasonal and temporary worker schemes matching labour demand.\n• Shortage occupation lists fast-tracking needed skills.\n• Refugee resettlement programmes and humanitarian visas.\n• Integration policy — language provision, qualification recognition, citizenship routes.\n\nIndicative content — effectiveness:\n• Restriction of legal routes tends to displace rather than reduce movement, increasing irregular migration, smuggling and deaths in transit.\n• Policies frequently conflict with labour market needs, producing shortages after restriction — evident in UK agriculture and care after 2021.\n• Selective policies attract skilled migrants at the cost of brain drain elsewhere.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): both restrictive and enabling policies explained with named examples and some assessment of effectiveness.\n• Band 2 (3–5): policies listed with limited explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that countries control borders.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and characteristics of rural-to-urban migration in lower-income countries.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — push factors:\n• Agricultural mechanisation and land fragmentation reducing rural employment and viability.\n• Land degradation, drought and declining yields, increasingly linked to climate change.\n• Poor rural services — schools, healthcare, electricity, transport.\n• Rural poverty and indebtedness; in some contexts conflict and land dispossession.\n\nIndicative content — pull factors:\n• Perceived employment opportunity, particularly in construction, manufacturing and informal services.\n• Higher wages, even in informal work, and access to education and healthcare.\n• Urban lifestyle and the influence of media representations; escape from social constraint.\n\nIndicative content — characteristics:\n• Dominated by young adults, producing a distorted age structure in both origin and destination.\n• Often stepwise — village to regional town to major city — and frequently circular, with seasonal return for harvest.\n• Sustained by networks, so migrants from one village cluster in one district.\n• Arrivals often exceed formal housing and job supply, producing informal settlements and informal employment — Dharavi in Mumbai, Kibera in Nairobi.\n• Rapid urbanisation results: most urban growth in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia is driven by a combination of migration and natural increase among young urban migrants.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): causes and characteristics both covered with accurate examples and the selectivity of migration recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes listed with limited attention to characteristics.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that people move to cities for jobs.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of environmental migration.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Sudden-onset events — floods, cyclones, earthquakes — displacing large numbers rapidly, usually temporarily and within the country.\n• Slow-onset processes — drought, desertification, soil salinisation, sea-level rise and coastal erosion — eroding livelihoods until migration becomes the only option.\n• Loss of habitability in low-lying states such as Tuvalu and Kiribati, and in delta regions such as Bangladesh.\n• Environmental factors rarely act alone: they interact with poverty, weak governance and existing vulnerability, so the same hazard displaces very different numbers in different places.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Most movement is internal and short-distance; the image of mass international flows is not supported by evidence.\n• Pressure on receiving areas, usually cities already struggling with informal settlement.\n• Loss of land, livelihood and cultural identity, particularly acute where whole island communities relocate.\n• Legal gap: environmental migrants are not covered by the Refugee Convention, so there is no protected status — the term "climate refugee" has no legal standing.\n• Potential for conflict over resources in receiving areas.\n• Planned relocation as adaptation, as in Fiji’s village relocation programme, raising questions of consent and compensation.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): distinguishes sudden and slow onset, covers consequences including the legal gap, and recognises interaction with other vulnerabilities.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes or consequences covered with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that climate change makes people move.` },

      { q: 'Explain the challenges faced by migrants during migration and on arrival.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — during transit:\n• Cost and debt — recruitment fees and smuggler payments frequently requiring families to borrow, creating bonded obligations.\n• Physical danger — desert and sea crossings; thousands die annually in the Mediterranean.\n• Exploitation by smugglers and traffickers; risk of forced labour and sexual violence, borne disproportionately by women and unaccompanied minors.\n• Detention in transit countries and pushbacks at borders.\n\nIndicative content — on arrival:\n• Legal status — lengthy asylum processing, restrictions on the right to work, and precarity if status is refused.\n• Language barriers restricting employment, healthcare access and social participation.\n• Non-recognition of qualifications, producing deskilling — trained professionals working far below their qualification level.\n• Labour exploitation in informal work: low pay, unsafe conditions and limited recourse where status is insecure.\n• Housing — discrimination, overcrowding and cost.\n• Discrimination, racism and hostility; social isolation and mental health effects, compounded by trauma among forced migrants.\n• Separation from family and obligations to remit, constraining the migrant’s own standard of living.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): challenges covered at both stages with specific detail and recognition of differential vulnerability.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several challenges identified with limited development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that migration is difficult and dangerous.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of different players in the governance of international migration.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• National governments — retain sovereign control over entry and citizenship; set visa systems, asylum procedures and integration policy. Migration is one of the least internationalised policy areas precisely because states guard this.\n• UNHCR — mandated to protect refugees, supervise the Refugee Convention and coordinate assistance; depends on voluntary funding and on state consent to operate.\n• IOM — the UN migration agency, providing operational support, data and return programmes.\n• Regional blocs — the EU with free movement internally and Frontex externally; ECOWAS and Mercosur with their own mobility arrangements.\n• The Global Compact for Migration (2018) — the first comprehensive international framework, but explicitly non-binding, and several states declined to adopt it.\n• NGOs — search and rescue, legal advice, advocacy; sometimes in direct conflict with state policy.\n• Employers and recruitment agencies — driving demand and shaping recruitment practice, including its abuses.\n• Smuggling and trafficking networks — an illegal but organised set of players that grows when legal routes close.\n• Key point: there is no binding global migration regime comparable to trade, so governance is fragmented, largely bilateral, and dominated by destination-state interests.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): a range of players with roles and limitations explained, and the weakness of global governance recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): players identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names organisations with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain how migration contributes to the growth of megacities and world cities.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Megacities in lower-income countries grow substantially through rural-to-urban migration, added to natural increase among a young migrant population — Lagos, Dhaka, Karachi.\n• World cities such as London, New York and Singapore grow through international migration at both ends of the labour market: highly skilled workers in finance, law and technology, and low-paid workers in cleaning, hospitality, care and delivery that sustain them.\n• This produces a polarised occupational structure and sharp spatial inequality within the same city.\n• Migrants concentrate in cities because labour demand, established networks and anonymity are all greatest there, so migration is a disproportionately urban phenomenon: London is around 40% foreign-born against 9% for the UK overall.\n• Consequences: cultural diversity and economic dynamism; pressure on housing and transport; informal settlement where formal provision cannot keep pace.\n• Ethnic enclaves form through chain migration, providing support networks and business opportunity, though they can also indicate exclusion from wider housing markets.\n• Migration sustains city populations that would otherwise decline through low fertility and internal out-migration.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): distinguishes megacity and world city dynamics with examples and explains polarisation.\n• Band 2 (3–5): explains urban growth through migration with limited differentiation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that migrants move to cities.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of one major refugee crisis you have studied.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named crisis. Syria is used below; credit Ukraine, Afghanistan, Rohingya or South Sudan at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — causes:\n• Civil war from 2011, following repression of protests during the Arab Spring, escalating into multi-party conflict with international involvement.\n• Deliberate targeting of civilians, siege warfare and use of chemical weapons; destruction of housing, hospitals and schools.\n• Collapse of the economy and of basic services; a severe preceding drought (2006–2010) had already driven rural-to-urban displacement, a contributing background factor.\n• Duration — over a decade — converting temporary displacement into permanent exile.\n\nIndicative content — scale and pattern:\n• Over 6.8 million refugees and a comparable number internally displaced, from a pre-war population of about 21 million.\n• Turkey hosts the largest number, around 3.6 million; Lebanon and Jordan host very large numbers relative to their own populations — Lebanon at one point around one refugee to four residents.\n• Around 1 million reached Europe, concentrated in 2015–16.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• For refugees — loss of home, livelihood and education; a generation of children with interrupted schooling; restricted right to work in several host states.\n• For neighbouring hosts — severe pressure on housing, water, schools and health systems; labour market effects; strain on states with limited fiscal capacity.\n• For Europe — the 2015 crisis drove the EU–Turkey deal, reintroduction of internal border checks, and significant political consequences including growth of anti-immigration parties.\n• For Syria — loss of skilled population, demographic distortion, and enormous reconstruction requirement.\n• Positive consequences worth credit: significant integration and entrepreneurship among Syrian communities in host countries.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): accurate, detailed case-specific account with figures, covering causes and consequences for multiple groups.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound outline with some specific detail; generalised in places.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generic account of refugees with little specific detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain the economic and social impacts of migration on the UK.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — economic:\n• Labour supply in sectors with persistent shortages — health and social care, agriculture, food processing, hospitality, construction; a substantial share of NHS doctors and nurses trained abroad.\n• Fiscal contribution — migrants are concentrated in working ages and, particularly for recent EEA migration, have been net contributors to public finances.\n• Business formation and innovation rates above the national average.\n• Wage effects small in aggregate, with some evidence of downward pressure at the bottom of the distribution in particular sectors.\n• Post-2021 points-based system reduced EU inflows and contributed to shortages in agriculture, haulage and care, illustrating dependence.\n\nIndicative content — social:\n• Demographic effect — higher fertility and younger age profile partially offsetting population ageing, though only temporarily.\n• Cultural change — food, music, religious institutions, festivals; London around 40% foreign-born.\n• Pressure on housing, schools and primary care where arrival is rapid and concentrated, and where funding follows with a lag.\n• Integration outcomes vary; second-generation educational attainment is high in several communities.\n• Political salience — immigration was central to the 2016 referendum, indicating the depth of public concern regardless of aggregate economic evidence.\n• Distribution point: benefits are national and diffuse, pressures local and visible, which explains the gap between economic analysis and public opinion.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers economic and social impacts with UK-specific evidence and attention to distribution and to contested evidence.\n• Band 2 (4–6): impacts covered with limited UK-specific detail or one-sided.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generalised assertions about immigration.` },

      { q: 'Explain the patterns of international migration at a global scale.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — overall scale and direction:\n• Around 281 million international migrants in 2020, about 3.6% of world population — a share that has risen only modestly.\n• The largest flows are South-to-North (to Europe, North America, Australasia) and South-to-South, which is of comparable magnitude and frequently overlooked.\n• North-to-North flows include intra-EU movement and skilled professional mobility.\n\nIndicative content — major corridors and destinations:\n• Mexico–USA, the largest single corridor; the USA hosts the largest absolute number of migrants.\n• South and Southeast Asia to the Gulf states, driven by construction and domestic labour demand; the UAE and Qatar have among the highest proportions of foreign-born residents in the world.\n• Intra-EU movement following 2004 enlargement, from central and eastern Europe westward.\n• Intra-African and intra-Asian movement, much of it short-distance and regional.\n\nIndicative content — characteristics:\n• Increasing feminisation, with women now roughly half of international migrants and dominant in care and domestic work corridors.\n• Refugees are around 10% of international migrants, and are overwhelmingly hosted in low- and middle-income countries neighbouring the origin.\n• Migration is selective — young adults, and rarely the poorest, who cannot afford to move.\n• Concentration in cities rather than evenly across destination countries.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): describes and explains patterns with accurate figures, named corridors and recognition of South–South flows and selectivity.\n• Band 2 (4–6): some patterns identified with limited quantification.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that people move from poor to rich countries.` },

      { q: 'Explain how migration can be both a cause and a consequence of development.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — migration as a cause of development:\n• Remittances exceed official development aid globally, reaching households directly and reducing poverty without institutional intermediation.\n• Return migration brings skills, capital and business networks — significant in Indian and Chinese technology sectors.\n• Diaspora investment and trade links lower transaction costs between origin and destination.\n• Social remittances — transfer of ideas about education, health and gender roles.\n• Destination countries gain labour and skills that support economic growth.\n\nIndicative content — migration as a consequence of development:\n• The migration hump: emigration initially rises with development because more households can afford the cost, then falls once income differentials narrow — so development in the poorest countries increases migration in the short run.\n• Education expansion creates aspirations and qualifications that local labour markets cannot absorb.\n• Improved transport, communications and access to information enable movement.\n• Structural transformation displaces agricultural labour, driving rural-to-urban and then international movement.\n\nIndicative content — negative feedbacks:\n• Brain drain removes the skilled workers development requires, particularly in health.\n• Remittance dependence may reduce pressure for domestic reform and can raise the exchange rate, harming other exports.\n• Loss of young workers raises the dependency ratio.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• The relationship is circular and non-linear, which is why policies premised on "reducing migration through development" are unlikely to work in the short term — a conclusion with direct policy relevance.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): explains causation in both directions with examples, including the migration hump and negative feedbacks.\n• Band 2 (4–6): one direction explained well with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that poor people migrate to get richer.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for, and consequences of, the feminisation of migration.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the phenomenon:\n• Women now constitute roughly half of international migrants, and increasingly migrate independently as primary economic migrants rather than as dependants.\n\nIndicative content — reasons:\n• Demand in feminised sectors — domestic work, childcare, elder care, nursing, hospitality — growing as women in high-income countries entered paid employment and populations aged.\n• Active recruitment for care work, with dedicated migration channels; the Philippines has institutionalised labour export, with substantial state involvement.\n• Changing gender norms and rising female education in origin countries.\n• Family strategies — households may send a daughter because women are perceived as more reliable remitters.\n• Escape from constraint, violence or restrictive social expectations.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Global care chains — women migrate to care for others’ children while their own are cared for by relatives or by other migrants, transferring care work down an international chain.\n• Higher remittance rates: evidence suggests women remit a larger proportion of lower earnings.\n• Vulnerability — domestic work is often outside labour law, in private homes, with status tied to a single employer; the kafala system in Gulf states has been widely criticised for facilitating abuse.\n• Deskilling, with qualified professionals working in domestic roles.\n• Origin-country effects on family structure and children left behind.\n• Empowerment and changed gender relations on return, though contested.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): reasons and consequences both developed with the care chain concept and specific examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): identifies the trend with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that more women migrate now.` },

      { q: 'Explain why international migration is a politically contested issue.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — sources of contestation:\n• Distribution of costs and benefits: economic gains are national, diffuse and gradual; pressures on housing, schools and primary care are local, visible and immediate, so aggregate benefit coexists with concentrated cost.\n• Sovereignty — control of borders is central to the idea of the nation state, so migration engages questions of identity as well as economics.\n• Cultural anxiety about pace of change, particularly where arrival is rapid and concentrated.\n• Labour market competition, real or perceived, at the lower end of the wage distribution.\n• Conflation of categories — refugees, asylum seekers and economic migrants are legally distinct but frequently merged in public debate, so arguments proceed at cross purposes.\n• Media representation emphasising irregular arrivals, which are a small fraction of total migration but visually salient.\n• Evidence is genuinely contested — fiscal impact estimates depend on assumptions and time horizons, so both sides can cite research.\n• Moral disagreement about obligations to non-citizens, which is a question of values rather than of fact and therefore not resolvable by evidence.\n• Political incentives — migration is a mobilising issue, so parties have reasons to sustain rather than settle the dispute.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Policy volatility and short-termism; Brexit as an example of migration shaping a major constitutional decision.\n• Gap between economic requirement and political feasibility, producing shortages after restriction.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several distinct sources of contestation explained, including the distributional and evidential points, with examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): identifies reasons with partial explanation, largely economic or cultural.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that people disagree about immigration.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which international migration benefits countries of origin.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — benefits:\n• Remittances exceed $650 billion annually to low- and middle-income countries, more than official development aid and FDI combined in many cases; they reach households directly and reduce poverty measurably.\n• They are counter-cyclical, rising when the home economy weakens — a stabilising property aid lacks.\n• Reduced unemployment pressure where labour supply exceeds demand.\n• Brain circulation — returnees bring skills, capital and networks; the Indian IT sector is the standard example.\n• Diaspora trade and investment links, and social remittances affecting education and gender norms.\n\nIndicative content — costs:\n• Brain drain, most acute in health: several African countries have more trained doctors abroad than at home, with the training cost borne domestically and the benefit accruing to wealthy states.\n• Loss of young working-age adults raising the dependency ratio and depopulating rural areas.\n• Family separation with long-term social and psychological costs for children.\n• Remittance dependence creating exposure to destination-country recession and possibly reducing pressure for domestic reform; potential exchange rate effects harming other exports.\n• Remittances often fund consumption rather than productive investment, so growth effects are limited.\n\nJudgement: benefits are substantial and well evidenced at household level, while costs fall on national capacity, particularly in skilled sectors — so the answer depends on the scale of analysis (household versus state) and on which sectors migrants leave. A defensible conclusion distinguishes low-skilled emigration, where the balance is clearly positive, from health-sector emigration, where it is not.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with quantified evidence, distinguishing scales or sectors, and reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): benefits and costs identified with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists impacts with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of policies designed to control international migration.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — evidence of effectiveness:\n• Points-based systems demonstrably alter the composition of migration — the UK’s post-2021 system reduced EU inflows and raised the skill profile of entrants.\n• Visa regimes and carrier sanctions effectively prevent most unauthorised arrival by regular routes.\n• Bilateral externalisation agreements reduce arrivals along particular routes; the EU–Turkey deal sharply cut Aegean crossings after 2016.\n• Free movement areas successfully enable managed mobility where that is the objective.\n\nIndicative content — limits:\n• Substitution rather than reduction: closing one route displaces movement to others, usually more dangerous — Mediterranean crossings shifted to the longer central route with higher mortality.\n• Restriction increases reliance on smugglers, raising prices and criminal profits.\n• Policies frequently conflict with labour demand, producing shortages and subsequent partial reversal — UK agriculture, haulage and care after 2021.\n• Push factors such as war are unaffected by destination policy, so forced migration responds weakly to deterrence.\n• Deterrence assumes information and rational calculation that many migrants do not have.\n• Enforcement is expensive; walls and detention have high costs per person deterred.\n• Legal constraints — non-refoulement and human rights obligations limit what states may do, and offshore processing schemes have faced repeated legal challenge.\n\nJudgement: policies are effective at shaping who enters legally and through which route, and much less effective at altering the total number of people who move — particularly forced migrants. A defensible conclusion distinguishes composition from volume, and notes that effectiveness depends on which objective is being measured.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): assesses against explicit objectives, uses named policies and evidence, and distinguishes composition from volume.\n• Band 2 (5–8): describes policies with some evaluation of effectiveness.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists border controls with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the impacts of migration are felt most strongly at the local scale.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Migrants concentrate in particular cities and neighbourhoods rather than distributing evenly, so demographic change is locally intense — London around 40% foreign-born against 9% nationally.\n• Pressure on school places, primary care, housing and English-language provision is experienced by specific schools and surgeries, often before funding formulas adjust.\n• Cultural change in the built environment — shops, places of worship, languages heard — is a neighbourhood-scale phenomenon.\n• Origin-country impacts are equally local: specific villages lose their young adults and receive the remittances.\n• Local labour markets in particular sectors feel wage and employment effects that are invisible in national aggregates.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Fiscal and macroeconomic impacts are national — tax contribution, GDP, and the dependency ratio operate at state level.\n• Political consequences are national: migration shaped the 2016 referendum outcome and party systems across Europe.\n• Global effects — remittance flows exceeding aid, and global care chains linking labour markets across continents.\n• Policy is set nationally and internationally, so the scale at which impacts are managed differs from the scale at which they are felt.\n• Diaspora and transnational networks operate across scales simultaneously.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The distinction that resolves the question is between where impacts are experienced and where they are measured or managed: costs are concentrated and visible locally while benefits are diffuse and national, which is precisely why local opposition coexists with national gain.\n• Type of impact determines scale: service pressure is local, fiscal effect national, remittances international.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment distinguishing types of impact by scale, with evidence, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): impacts identified at more than one scale with some comparison.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes impacts without reference to scale.` },

      { q: 'Assess the importance of migration in addressing population ageing in high-income countries.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting its importance:\n• Migrants are concentrated in working ages, so immigration immediately lowers the dependency ratio and expands the tax base.\n• It supplies labour directly to the sectors ageing populations need most — health and social care depend heavily on migrant workers.\n• Fertility among some migrant groups is higher, contributing to natural increase.\n• It acts faster than any alternative: raising fertility takes twenty years to affect the labour force, whereas migration affects it immediately.\n• Several countries — Germany, Japan by contrast — illustrate the difference between using and not using this lever.\n\nIndicative content — limits:\n• Migrants age too, so immigration postpones rather than solves the problem; sustaining a dependency ratio would require continuously increasing inflows.\n• The numbers required to offset ageing fully are politically unachievable in most democracies.\n• Alternatives exist — raising the retirement age, increasing female and older-worker participation, automation and productivity growth.\n• Political feasibility is the binding constraint, not demographic logic.\n• Ethical concern: recruiting healthcare workers from countries with worse shortages transfers the problem to those least able to bear it.\n• Distributional effects — pressure on housing and services may offset fiscal gains locally.\n\nJudgement: migration is an effective short-to-medium-term instrument and the only one that acts quickly, but it is a palliative rather than a solution, and cannot substitute for pension reform and productivity growth. A defensible conclusion weighs demographic effectiveness against political and ethical constraints.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment comparing migration with alternatives, recognising the ageing-migrant point, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): identifies benefits and limits with some evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that migrants are young and help the economy.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the benefits of international migration outweigh the costs. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — benefits:\n• For migrants — the largest single gain: earnings can multiply several times, and migration is among the most effective poverty-reduction mechanisms available to individuals; this is frequently omitted from debates conducted only from the destination’s perspective.\n• For origin countries — remittances exceeding $650 billion annually to low- and middle-income countries, reaching households directly; return migration bringing skills and capital; diaspora trade links.\n• For destination countries — filling labour shortages, particularly in care, agriculture and construction; net fiscal contribution from working-age migrants; higher rates of business formation; cultural enrichment; demographic support for ageing populations.\n• Globally — more efficient allocation of labour; estimates suggest liberalising movement would raise world output substantially.\n\nIndicative content — costs:\n• For migrants — transit danger and death, debt, exploitation, deskilling, discrimination, family separation and trauma.\n• For origin countries — brain drain, particularly in health; loss of young workers; dependency on remittances; rural depopulation.\n• For destination countries — localised pressure on housing, schools and health services where funding lags; possible wage pressure at the lowest end; integration failures and social tension; political polarisation.\n• Distributional problem — benefits national and diffuse, costs local and concentrated, so aggregate calculations do not reflect lived experience.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Everything depends on whose perspective is adopted: for migrants the benefit is usually overwhelming; for origin states in skilled sectors it may be negative; for destination states it is positive in aggregate but unevenly distributed.\n• Type of migration matters: forced migration carries far higher costs and fewer chosen benefits than skilled voluntary migration.\n• Timescale matters: short-run adjustment costs against long-run gains, and second-generation outcomes frequently better than first.\n• Measurement is contested: fiscal estimates vary with assumptions about time horizon and the treatment of public goods, so "the evidence" does not settle the question by itself.\n• A strong answer commits to a position — most defensibly that aggregate benefits exceed aggregate costs while their distribution is the real problem, which makes this a question about compensation and planning rather than about numbers.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning covering multiple perspectives; wide range of accurate quantified evidence; distinguishes types of migration, scales and timescales; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation with accurate support and a clear judgement, less fully resolved or narrower in perspective.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes benefits and costs with limited evaluation; largely destination-focused; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–5): isolated assertions about immigration with little evidence.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the response to one major migration event or flow you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named event. The 2015–16 European migration crisis is used below; credit the Ukrainian displacement from 2022, the Rohingya crisis, or the Venezuelan exodus at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — the event:\n• Over one million arrivals in Europe in 2015, principally Syrians, Afghans and Iraqis, mostly via Turkey and the Aegean, and via Libya to Italy.\n• Around 3,700 deaths in the Mediterranean in 2015 alone.\n\nIndicative content — the responses:\n• Germany suspended the Dublin Regulation for Syrians and accepted around 890,000 asylum seekers in 2015 — Wir schaffen das.\n• EU relocation quota scheme agreed in 2015 to redistribute asylum seekers between member states.\n• EU–Turkey Statement, March 2016 — €6 billion in exchange for Turkey preventing departures and accepting returns.\n• Operation Sophia and national search-and-rescue operations; Frontex expansion.\n• Several states reintroduced internal border controls; Hungary constructed border fences.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Aegean crossings fell by over 90% after the EU–Turkey Statement, and deaths on that route fell sharply.\n• Germany integrated a substantial proportion into employment within five years, exceeding most forecasts.\n• Search and rescue saved tens of thousands of lives.\n• The crisis prompted reform of the Common European Asylum System, though agreement took until 2024.\n\nIndicative content — limitations and criticisms:\n• The relocation scheme largely failed — only a small fraction of the agreed quota was relocated, with several states refusing outright, exposing the absence of enforceable solidarity.\n• Externalisation transferred responsibility to Turkey and later Libya, with serious human rights concerns in Libyan detention centres; critics argue it purchased reduced arrivals at the cost of refugee protection.\n• Route substitution — crossings shifted to the central Mediterranean, where mortality per crossing is far higher.\n• Political consequences — growth of anti-immigration parties across Europe; contribution to the Brexit vote.\n• Dublin Regulation placed disproportionate burden on frontline states, Greece and Italy, and was never adequately reformed during the crisis.\n• Response was reactive and slow; the crisis had been building for years.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — lives saved, protection provided, integration outcomes, burden sharing, political sustainability, legality — noting sharply different performance against each.\n• Effective at reducing arrivals and, in Germany, at integration; poor at solidarity and at upholding protection standards.\n• Success for whom: European states largely achieved their objective; refugees experienced worse protection than the Convention envisages.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate evidence including figures and dates, genuine engagement with trade-offs and with whose interests were served, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the response with some evaluative comment; detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of a migration crisis with little specific detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.4': {
    name: 'Global Governance of Oceans',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by global governance and state why the oceans require it.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Global governance is the set of rules, norms, institutions and agreements through which states and other actors manage issues that cross national borders (1)\n• It operates without a world government, so it depends on treaties, consent and mutual interest rather than on enforcement by a superior authority (1)\n• The oceans require it because they are largely beyond national jurisdiction — the high seas cover almost half the planet’s surface (1)\n• Fish stocks, pollution and shipping move across boundaries, so no single state can manage them and unilateral restraint would simply advantage others (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a global commons and name the four usually identified.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A global common is a resource domain lying outside the political reach of any single state, to which all have access and over which none has sovereignty (1)\n• The high seas — ocean beyond national jurisdiction (1)\n• The atmosphere, and Antarctica, governed by the Antarctic Treaty System (1)\n• Outer space; all four raise the same difficulty — open access with no owner creates an incentive to exploit and none to conserve (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the tragedy of the commons and apply it to ocean fisheries.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Where a resource is open to all and owned by none, each user gains the full benefit of what they take but bears only a share of the cost of depletion (1)\n• It is therefore individually rational to take more, even though the collective result is exhaustion of the resource (1)\n• In fisheries, each vessel that restrains its catch simply leaves fish for others, so restraint is penalised rather than rewarded (1)\n• The outcome is over-capacity and stock collapse — as with Newfoundland cod in 1992 — unless access is restricted by quota, property right or enforceable agreement (1)` },

      { q: 'State the maritime zones defined by UNCLOS and their seaward limits.', marks: 5, command: 'State', markScheme: `• Territorial sea — up to 12 nautical miles from the baseline; full sovereignty, subject to the right of innocent passage (1)\n• Contiguous zone — up to 24 nm; the state may enforce customs, immigration, fiscal and sanitary law (1)\n• Exclusive Economic Zone — up to 200 nm; sovereign rights over living and non-living resources, but not sovereignty over the water itself (1)\n• Continental shelf — to 200 nm, extendable to 350 nm where the natural prolongation of the landmass justifies it, giving rights to seabed resources (1)\n• High seas — beyond the EEZ, open to all states; the seabed beneath is "the Area", the common heritage of mankind (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between sovereignty in the territorial sea and sovereign rights in the EEZ.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• In the territorial sea the coastal state has full sovereignty over the water, seabed and airspace, as if it were land territory (1)\n• The only significant limit is the right of innocent passage for foreign vessels (1)\n• In the EEZ the state has sovereign rights only over resources — fishing, minerals, energy — while other states retain freedom of navigation, overflight and cable-laying, which is why naval transits through EEZs are lawful but politically contested (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by "the Area" and the principle of the common heritage of mankind.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The Area is the seabed and subsoil beyond the limits of national jurisdiction, covering over half the ocean floor (1)\n• UNCLOS designates it the common heritage of mankind: no state may claim sovereignty over it, and its resources belong to humanity as a whole (1)\n• Activities there are administered by the International Seabed Authority, which must ensure benefits are shared equitably, including with landlocked and developing states — a principle now tested by deep-sea mining applications (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of the International Maritime Organization.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The IMO is the UN agency responsible for the safety and security of shipping and the prevention of marine pollution from ships (1)\n• It develops conventions such as SOLAS on safety of life at sea and MARPOL on pollution, which member states then implement in national law (1)\n• It sets standards including sulphur limits on marine fuel and greenhouse gas targets for shipping, but relies on flag and port states for enforcement, which is its principal weakness (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a flag of convenience and why it weakens ocean governance.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A flag of convenience is registration of a vessel in a state other than that of its owners, chosen for low cost and light regulation — Panama, Liberia and the Marshall Islands hold the largest registries (1)\n• Under UNCLOS, the flag state has primary jurisdiction over a vessel on the high seas (1)\n• Open-registry states often lack the capacity or the incentive to inspect and enforce, so labour, safety and environmental standards go unchecked (1)\n• Ownership is also obscured through shell companies, so responsibility for illegal fishing or pollution is difficult to attribute — the gap between the legal framework and its enforcement (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by IUU fishing and why it is difficult to control.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing — fishing in breach of national or international rules, not reported to authorities, or conducted where no management framework applies (1)\n• It is estimated at up to a fifth of global catch, worth some $10–23 billion annually (1)\n• Control is difficult because the ocean is vast and monitoring expensive; many vessels disable their AIS transponders to avoid detection (1)\n• Transhipment at sea launders catch into legitimate supply chains, flags of convenience obscure ownership, and developing coastal states often lack patrol capacity to police their own EEZs (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what a Marine Protected Area is and the difference between a fully protected and a partially protected MPA.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An MPA is a defined area of ocean where human activity is restricted for conservation purposes (1)\n• A fully protected (no-take) MPA prohibits all extractive activity, including all fishing and mining (1)\n• A partially protected MPA permits some activities under restriction — limited gear types, seasonal closure or restricted vessel size (1)\n• The distinction matters because evidence of ecological benefit is far stronger for fully protected areas; many large MPAs are only lightly protected, giving "paper parks" that count toward targets without delivering recovery (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what the High Seas Treaty (BBNJ Agreement) of 2023 provides for.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Agreed in 2023, it is the first legally binding instrument governing biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (1)\n• It creates a mechanism for establishing Marine Protected Areas on the high seas, which previously could not be designated at all (1)\n• It requires environmental impact assessment for activities in those waters, and addresses the sharing of benefits from marine genetic resources (1)\n• It supports the "30 by 30" target of protecting 30% of the ocean by 2030; its effect depends on ratification and on enforcement capacity, both still uncertain (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a dead zone and how one forms.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A dead zone is an area of water so depleted of oxygen that most marine life cannot survive — hypoxia (1)\n• Nutrient runoff, principally nitrogen and phosphorus from agricultural fertiliser and sewage, causes eutrophication and algal blooms (1)\n• When the algae die, bacterial decomposition consumes dissolved oxygen, and stratification prevents re-oxygenation from the surface; the Gulf of Mexico dead zone, fed by the Mississippi, regularly exceeds 15,000 km² (1)` },

      { q: 'An EEZ extends 200 nautical miles from a coastline. Given 1 nautical mile = 1.852 km, calculate the EEZ radius in kilometres and the approximate area of a semicircular EEZ off a straight coast.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Radius = 200 × 1.852 = 370.4 km (1)\n• Semicircle area = πr² ÷ 2 (1)\n• r² = 370.4² = 137,196 (1)\n• π × 137,196 = 430,999; ÷ 2 = 215,500 km² (accept 215,000–216,000) (1)\n• This is why small island states can hold enormous EEZs — Kiribati has a land area of 811 km² but an EEZ exceeding 3.4 million km², which explains the strategic value of tiny uninhabited features (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and the interpretive point.` },

      { q: 'Global fish catch was 19 million tonnes in 1950 and 96 million tonnes in 2020. Calculate the percentage increase and the mean annual increase in tonnes.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Increase = 96 − 19 = 77 million tonnes (1)\n• Percentage increase = 77 ÷ 19 × 100 = 405% (1)\n• Period = 70 years (1)\n• Mean annual increase = 77 ÷ 70 = 1.1 million tonnes per year (1)\n• The mean conceals the pattern: wild capture rose steeply to a peak around 1996 and has since plateaued or declined, with recent growth coming from aquaculture — so a linear mean misrepresents a curve that has flattened because the resource limit was reached (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and recognition that the mean conceals the plateau.` },

      { q: 'Of assessed global fish stocks, 35.4% are overfished, 57.3% are maximally sustainably fished and 7.2% are underfished. Calculate the proportion fished at or beyond sustainable limits, and comment on what the figures imply.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• At or beyond limits = 35.4 + 57.3 (1)\n• = 92.7% (1)\n• Only 7.2% of stocks have capacity for increased catch (1)\n• This implies there is essentially no room for expansion of wild capture: increased demand must be met by aquaculture or by reducing consumption (1)\n• The figures cover assessed stocks only; many stocks, particularly in developing-country waters, are unassessed and may be in worse condition, so the true picture is likely less favourable (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation, implication and the limitation about assessed stocks.` },

      { q: 'An estimated 11 million tonnes of plastic enter the ocean annually, projected to reach 29 million tonnes by 2040. Calculate the percentage increase and the mean annual rate of increase over the 17-year period from 2023.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Increase = 29 − 11 = 18 million tonnes (1)\n• Percentage increase = 18 ÷ 11 × 100 = 163.6% (1)\n• Period = 2040 − 2023 = 17 years (1)\n• Mean annual increase = 18 ÷ 17 = 1.06 million tonnes per year (1)\n• The projection assumes current trends continue; it also measures input rather than stock, and since plastic persists for centuries the accumulated total in the ocean rises even if annual input were held constant (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct arithmetic and the flow-versus-stock distinction.` },

      { q: 'A country’s fishing fleet lands 180,000 tonnes against a quota of 145,000 tonnes. Calculate the percentage by which the quota was exceeded, and the tonnage that would need to be cut to comply.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Excess = 180,000 − 145,000 = 35,000 tonnes (1)\n• Percentage over quota = 35,000 ÷ 145,000 × 100 (1)\n• = 24.1% (1)\n• A cut of 35,000 tonnes, or 19.4% of current landings, would be required to comply (1)\n• Note the two percentages differ because they use different bases — exceeding a quota by 24% requires only a 19% cut in landings, a distinction frequently confused in reporting (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both percentages correct and the point about differing bases.` },

      { q: 'MPAs cover 8.2% of the global ocean, but only 2.9% is fully protected. The ocean covers 361 million km². Calculate the area under each and the shortfall against the 30% target.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• All MPAs: 0.082 × 361 = 29.6 million km² (1)\n• Fully protected: 0.029 × 361 = 10.5 million km² (1)\n• The 30% target = 0.30 × 361 = 108.3 million km² (1)\n• Shortfall against total MPA coverage = 108.3 − 29.6 = 78.7 million km² (1)\n• Barely a third of designated area is fully protected, so headline coverage figures substantially overstate real protection — the target will not be met by designation alone (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires all calculations and the point about designation versus protection.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of overfishing.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Rising demand from population growth and increased per-capita fish consumption.\n• Technological capacity — factory trawlers, sonar, GPS and refrigeration allow vessels to locate and process far more than stocks can replace.\n• Subsidies, estimated at over $20 billion annually, keeping uneconomic fleets at sea and creating over-capacity.\n• Open access and the tragedy of the commons, so restraint is individually irrational.\n• Weak enforcement, IUU fishing and flags of convenience.\n• Quotas set above scientific advice for political reasons.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Stock collapse — Newfoundland cod collapsed in 1992, 40,000 jobs lost and the stock has not recovered thirty years on, showing collapse can be effectively irreversible.\n• Bycatch and discards; damage to seabed habitat from bottom trawling.\n• Trophic cascade — removal of predators restructures whole ecosystems; fishing down the food web as larger species are depleted.\n• Food security, since fish supplies a significant share of animal protein for over 3 billion people.\n• Economic loss to coastal communities and conflict between fleets over remaining stocks.\n• Distant-water fleets displacing artisanal fishers in West African waters, exporting the problem.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): causes and consequences both covered with accurate examples and reference to the commons problem.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several points with partial explanation or one-sided.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that too many fish are caught.` },

      { q: 'Explain the sources and impacts of marine plastic pollution.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — sources:\n• Around 80% originates on land — inadequate waste management, litter, and drainage carrying plastic to rivers and then the sea; a small number of rivers in Asia and Africa carry a disproportionate share.\n• Marine sources — abandoned, lost or discarded fishing gear ("ghost gear"), which is a large share of large plastic items and the most directly lethal.\n• Microplastics from tyre wear, synthetic textile fibres released in washing, and cosmetic microbeads.\n• Breakdown of larger items by UV and wave action into secondary microplastics.\n\nIndicative content — impacts:\n• Entanglement and drowning of marine mammals, turtles and seabirds in ghost gear.\n• Ingestion causing blockage, false satiation and starvation; plastic found in the majority of seabird species.\n• Microplastics entering food webs and concentrating persistent organic pollutants, with implications for human consumption.\n• Accumulation in gyres — the Great Pacific Garbage Patch — and in deep-sea sediment and Arctic ice, so no part of the ocean is unaffected.\n• Economic damage to fisheries, tourism and shipping.\n• Persistence — plastics last centuries, so the problem accumulates and existing pollution cannot practically be removed at scale.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): sources and impacts covered accurately with quantification and the persistence point developed.\n• Band 2 (3–5): sources or impacts covered with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that plastic harms sea animals.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for disputes over territorial rights in the South China Sea.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — what is claimed:\n• China claims historic rights within the "nine-dash line" covering around 90% of the sea; the Philippines, Vietnam, Malaysia, Brunei and Taiwan hold overlapping claims.\n• Disputed features include the Spratly and Paracel Islands and Scarborough Shoal.\n\nIndicative content — reasons for the dispute:\n• Resources — substantial estimated oil and gas reserves, and fisheries supplying a large share of regional protein.\n• Strategic value — around a third of global shipping passes through, so control affects trade and naval access.\n• UNCLOS mechanics — sovereignty over a tiny feature generates a 12 nm territorial sea and, if it qualifies as an island rather than a rock, a 200 nm EEZ, so minute features have disproportionate value.\n• Island-building — construction of artificial islands with airstrips and harbours to strengthen claims in practice, although UNCLOS states that artificial islands generate no maritime zones.\n• Historical and nationalist claims that predate and are asserted against UNCLOS.\n• The 2016 Permanent Court of Arbitration ruling rejected the nine-dash line; China rejected the ruling, and there is no mechanism to enforce it — which illustrates the central weakness of ocean governance.\n• Great-power competition, with US freedom of navigation operations contesting Chinese claims.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): explains several distinct reasons accurately with specific detail and reference to UNCLOS mechanics and the enforcement gap.\n• Band 2 (3–5): identifies the dispute and some reasons with limited detail.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that countries disagree about islands.` },

      { q: 'Explain why the Arctic Ocean has become an area of increasing geopolitical interest.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — drivers:\n• Sea ice loss — summer extent has declined by over 40% since 1979, making waters navigable and resources accessible for longer each year.\n• Resources — the USGS estimates around 13% of undiscovered oil and 30% of undiscovered gas lie in the Arctic, together with minerals and expanding fisheries as species shift north.\n• Shipping — the Northern Sea Route cuts the Europe–Asia voyage by roughly a third compared with the Suez route, saving time and fuel.\n• Military and strategic value — Russian rebuilding of Arctic bases; NATO expansion with Finland and Sweden.\n\nIndicative content — governance:\n• Extended continental shelf claims under UNCLOS Article 76, with Russia, Canada and Denmark submitting overlapping claims including the Lomonosov Ridge, adjudicated by a scientific commission on geological evidence.\n• The Arctic Council coordinates the eight Arctic states and includes indigenous permanent participants, but has no mandate over security and works by consensus.\n• The 2018 Central Arctic Ocean fisheries agreement imposed a precautionary moratorium — an unusual case of governance preceding exploitation.\n• Cooperation has been the norm historically, though it has been strained since 2022.\n\nIndicative content — concerns:\n• Environmental fragility, slow ecosystem recovery, and the near-impossibility of oil spill clean-up in ice.\n• Rights and livelihoods of indigenous peoples.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several drivers explained with quantification and reference to the governance framework.\n• Band 2 (3–5): identifies resources and shipping with limited governance detail.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that ice is melting so countries want the Arctic.` },

      { q: 'Explain the arguments for and against deep-sea mining.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — what is proposed:\n• Extraction of polymetallic nodules from abyssal plains, notably the Clarion-Clipperton Zone, and of crusts and sulphides from seamounts and vents; nodules contain nickel, cobalt, copper and manganese.\n\nIndicative content — arguments for:\n• Demand for battery metals is rising steeply with the energy transition, and terrestrial supply is concentrated in a few countries.\n• Terrestrial mining has severe impacts too — cobalt from the DRC involves documented labour abuses and deforestation — so the comparison is not with no mining.\n• Nodule collection requires no drilling or blasting and produces no tailings dams.\n• Potential revenue for developing states through the ISA’s benefit-sharing provisions, as the common heritage principle requires.\n\nIndicative content — arguments against:\n• Abyssal ecosystems are almost entirely unstudied; species are being described as exploration proceeds, so impacts cannot be assessed against a known baseline.\n• Extremely slow recovery — nodules form over millions of years, and tracks from 1970s test mining remain visible today, so damage is effectively permanent.\n• Sediment plumes could smother life over areas far larger than the mined site; noise and light pollution in a dark, quiet environment.\n• Possible disruption of deep-ocean carbon storage.\n• Over 20 states support a moratorium; several manufacturers have pledged not to use deep-sea minerals.\n• Recycling and battery chemistry change may reduce demand, so the need itself is contested.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): balanced coverage with specific detail on both sides and reference to the ISA and the common heritage principle.\n• Band 2 (3–5): arguments identified with limited development or one-sided.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that mining the seabed damages the environment.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of UNCLOS in the governance of the oceans.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Agreed in 1982 and in force since 1994, UNCLOS is described as the constitution for the oceans, with over 165 parties.\n• It defines maritime zones — territorial sea, contiguous zone, EEZ, continental shelf, high seas — converting an area of competing claims into a rule-based framework.\n• It establishes rights and duties: freedom of navigation and overflight, innocent passage, transit through international straits, and the obligation to protect the marine environment.\n• It designates the deep seabed as the common heritage of mankind, administered by the International Seabed Authority.\n• It provides dispute settlement through the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea, the ICJ or arbitration, used in the 2016 South China Sea case.\n• It created the Commission on the Limits of the Continental Shelf to assess extended shelf claims on scientific evidence.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Enforcement depends on states, principally flag states, many of which are open registries with little capacity or incentive.\n• Rulings cannot be enforced against a state that rejects them, as China did in 2016.\n• The United States has signed but not ratified, weakening its authority.\n• It left biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction largely ungoverned until the 2023 BBNJ Agreement.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): explains the framework accurately and identifies substantive limitations.\n• Band 2 (3–5): describes zones with limited attention to enforcement.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that UNCLOS is a law of the sea treaty.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and effects of coral reef degradation.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Ocean warming causing bleaching — corals expel their symbiotic zooxanthellae under thermal stress, losing their colour and their principal energy source; the Great Barrier Reef suffered mass bleaching events in 2016, 2017, 2020, 2022 and 2024.\n• Ocean acidification reducing carbonate availability, slowing skeletal growth.\n• Local stressors — sediment and nutrient runoff from agriculture and deforestation reducing light and promoting algal overgrowth.\n• Destructive fishing — blast and cyanide fishing; overfishing of herbivorous fish that control algae.\n• Coastal development, dredging and tourism damage; crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks linked to nutrient runoff.\n\nIndicative content — effects:\n• Biodiversity loss — reefs occupy under 1% of the ocean floor but support around a quarter of marine species.\n• Fisheries collapse affecting food security for hundreds of millions in tropical coastal communities.\n• Loss of coastal protection — reefs dissipate a large proportion of wave energy, so degradation increases erosion and flood risk.\n• Economic loss to tourism, a principal income source for many small island states.\n• Loss of potential pharmaceutical resources and of cultural significance.\n• Key point: local stressors can be managed nationally, but warming and acidification cannot, so no amount of local protection saves reefs without global emissions reduction.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): distinguishes global and local causes, covers ecological and human effects, with examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes or effects covered with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that coral is dying because of warming.` },

      { q: 'Explain the strategies used to manage fisheries sustainably.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — input and output controls:\n• Quotas (total allowable catch) set from scientific stock assessment; individual transferable quotas give fishers a property right in a share of the stock, aligning private and collective interest.\n• Effort limits — restricting vessel numbers, days at sea, or engine power.\n• Technical measures — minimum mesh and landing sizes so juveniles can breed; gear modification such as turtle excluder devices; closed seasons during spawning.\n• Spatial measures — no-take MPAs allowing stock recovery and spillover into fished areas.\n• Landing obligations banning discards, so unwanted catch counts against quota.\n\nIndicative content — governance and market measures:\n• Regional Fisheries Management Organisations coordinating management of straddling and highly migratory stocks.\n• The Port State Measures Agreement (2016) denying port access to vessels suspected of IUU fishing — effective because enforcement happens where vessels are, not at sea.\n• WTO agreement on removing harmful fishing subsidies.\n• Certification such as the Marine Stewardship Council, using consumer demand to reward sustainable practice.\n• Satellite monitoring, AIS and vessel monitoring systems; Global Fishing Watch.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Success depends on quotas following scientific advice and on enforcement capacity; well-managed fisheries in Iceland, Norway and New Zealand demonstrate recovery is possible.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): a range of strategies across regulation, governance and markets, with examples and conditions for success.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several strategies listed with limited explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that catches should be limited.` },

      { q: 'Explain the importance of the oceans to the global economy and to human wellbeing.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — economic:\n• Shipping carries around 80–90% of world trade by volume, so the ocean is the physical basis of globalisation.\n• Fisheries and aquaculture provide employment for some 60 million people and a significant share of animal protein for over 3 billion.\n• Offshore oil and gas; increasingly offshore wind, a major component of decarbonisation.\n• Tourism — coastal and marine tourism is a principal income source for many island and coastal economies.\n• Submarine cables carry over 95% of international data traffic.\n• The "blue economy" is estimated to contribute of the order of $1.5 trillion annually.\n\nIndicative content — environmental and wellbeing:\n• Climate regulation — the ocean has absorbed over 90% of excess heat and roughly a quarter of anthropogenic CO₂, moderating warming substantially.\n• Oxygen production — marine phytoplankton generate around half of atmospheric oxygen.\n• Coastal protection from mangroves, reefs and salt marsh.\n• Cultural, spiritual and recreational value, particularly for indigenous and coastal communities.\n• Pharmaceutical compounds derived from marine organisms.\n• Interdependence point: the services that matter most — climate regulation, oxygen — are unpriced, which is precisely why they are under-protected in economic decision-making.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): covers economic and environmental importance with quantification and the point about unpriced services.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several uses identified with limited quantification.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that oceans provide fish and transport.` },

      { q: 'Explain the challenges of enforcing international agreements on the high seas.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Scale — the high seas cover around 43% of the Earth’s surface, far exceeding any realistic patrol capacity.\n• Jurisdiction — UNCLOS gives primary authority to the flag state, so a vessel flying an open registry is effectively policed by a state with neither capacity nor incentive.\n• Ownership is obscured through shell companies and frequent reflagging, making legal responsibility hard to attribute.\n• No global enforcement body exists; there is no ocean police force, and boarding another state’s vessel on the high seas is generally unlawful without consent.\n• Consent-based treaty law — states that do not ratify are not bound, and may withdraw.\n• Rulings cannot be enforced against a non-compliant state, as the 2016 South China Sea arbitration demonstrated.\n• Detection — AIS transponders can be switched off; transhipment at sea launders illegal catch.\n• Cost of monitoring falls on states that gain little directly, creating a free-rider problem in enforcement itself.\n\nIndicative content — what is working:\n• Port state control shifts enforcement to where vessels must come ashore, which is far more tractable than policing the sea.\n• Satellite monitoring and machine learning — Global Fishing Watch — have made dark-vessel detection increasingly feasible.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several structural challenges explained accurately, with recognition of emerging solutions.\n• Band 2 (3–5): identifies challenges with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that the sea is large and hard to police.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of marine pollution from shipping.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes and types:\n• Oil — catastrophic spills from tanker accidents (Exxon Valdez 1989, Deepwater Horizon 2010), but chronic operational discharge from bilge and tank washing accounts for more oil overall.\n• Air emissions — heavy fuel oil is high in sulphur; shipping accounts for roughly 3% of global CO₂ emissions and significant SO₂ and NOₓ, contributing to acid deposition and coastal air pollution.\n• Ballast water — transporting invasive species between ecosystems; zebra mussel and comb jelly invasions are major examples.\n• Antifouling paints releasing biocides such as tributyltin.\n• Noise pollution disrupting cetacean communication and navigation.\n• Solid waste and lost containers; ghost gear from fishing vessels.\n• Ship-strike mortality of whales.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Oiling of seabirds and mammals, destruction of intertidal habitat, long-term contamination of sediment; Exxon Valdez effects persisted for decades.\n• Economic damage to fisheries and tourism, and clean-up costs in billions.\n• Invasive species restructuring ecosystems irreversibly, with costs to fisheries and infrastructure.\n• Human health effects from port-area air pollution.\n\nIndicative content — governance:\n• MARPOL restricts operational discharge; the IMO 2020 sulphur cap cut the limit from 3.5% to 0.5%, with measurable air quality improvement.\n• The Ballast Water Management Convention requires treatment; double-hull tanker requirements followed Exxon Valdez.\n• Enforcement depends on flag and port states, which remains the weak point.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers several pollution types with consequences and the regulatory response, using examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): focuses on oil spills with limited breadth.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that ships spill oil.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the oceans are used as a strategic resource by different players.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — states:\n• Territorial claims to extend EEZs and secure resources — South China Sea island-building; Arctic continental shelf submissions.\n• Naval power projection and control of chokepoints — Hormuz, Malacca, Bab-el-Mandeb, Suez; the Ever Given grounding in 2021 demonstrated the vulnerability of a single chokepoint to global trade.\n• Freedom of navigation operations asserting legal interpretations against rival claims.\n• Submarine-based nuclear deterrence, requiring deep-water access.\n• Protection of submarine cables, an increasingly recognised security concern.\n\nIndicative content — corporations:\n• Shipping lines controlling global logistics; container alliances with substantial market power.\n• Offshore energy companies, and now offshore wind developers.\n• Distant-water fishing fleets, some state-subsidised, operating in other states’ EEZs under access agreements.\n• Emerging deep-sea mining contractors sponsored by small states.\n\nIndicative content — other players:\n• IGOs — the IMO, ISA and RFMOs setting rules; the UN framework through UNCLOS and BBNJ.\n• NGOs — Greenpeace, Sea Shepherd, campaigning and direct action.\n• Coastal and indigenous communities dependent on marine resources, often with least influence over decisions.\n• Non-state armed actors — piracy off Somalia and in the Gulf of Guinea, and attacks on shipping in the Red Sea disrupting global trade routes.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• Interests conflict, power is highly unequal, and the absence of a central authority means outcomes reflect capability as much as law.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): a range of players with their strategic interests explained and examples, recognising unequal power.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several players identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that countries want resources from the sea.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for, and consequences of, rising sea temperatures for marine ecosystems.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — reasons:\n• The ocean has absorbed over 90% of the excess heat trapped by greenhouse gases, so ocean warming is the dominant expression of global heating.\n• Warming is uneven — fastest in the Arctic and in surface waters; marine heatwaves have become more frequent, longer and more intense.\n• Stratification increases as surface water warms, reducing vertical mixing.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Coral bleaching — sustained thermal stress expels zooxanthellae; repeated events prevent recovery, and the Great Barrier Reef has bleached five times since 2016.\n• Species range shifts poleward and to greater depth, redistributing fish stocks across national boundaries and creating disputes over quotas — the North Atlantic mackerel dispute is the standard example.\n• Mismatch in timing between species and their food supply as phenology shifts at different rates.\n• Reduced oxygen solubility, expanding oxygen minimum zones; stratification compounds this by limiting resupply to depth.\n• Reduced nutrient upwelling through stratification, lowering primary productivity in some regions.\n• Loss of polar habitat as sea ice declines, affecting ice-dependent species.\n• Weakening of the ocean carbon sink, since warmer water holds less CO₂ — a positive feedback.\n• Consequences for people: fisheries relocation affecting food security and livelihoods; loss of reef-based tourism and coastal protection.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): mechanisms explained accurately with several distinct ecological consequences, examples, and at least one feedback or governance implication.\n• Band 2 (4–6): some consequences identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that warm water harms coral.` },

      { q: 'Explain how Marine Protected Areas are established and the factors determining their success.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — establishment:\n• Within an EEZ, a coastal state designates an MPA under national law, following scientific assessment and stakeholder consultation.\n• On the high seas, designation was previously impossible; the 2023 BBNJ Agreement creates a mechanism, subject to ratification.\n• Some are agreed through RFMOs, as with the Ross Sea MPA agreed by CCAMLR in 2016 — the world’s largest, at 1.55 million km², and notable for requiring consensus among 25 parties.\n• Designation involves defining boundaries, permitted activities, and monitoring and enforcement arrangements.\n\nIndicative content — factors determining success:\n• Level of protection — fully protected no-take areas show substantially greater biomass recovery than partially protected ones; many large MPAs are lightly protected "paper parks".\n• Enforcement capacity — patrol vessels, satellite monitoring and prosecution; without these, designation changes nothing.\n• Size and isolation — large, isolated areas protect whole ecosystems and mobile species; small coastal MPAs may not cover a species’ range.\n• Age — recovery takes a decade or more, so evaluation must allow time.\n• Local community support — where fishers are excluded without alternative livelihood, compliance fails; co-management with communities improves outcomes markedly.\n• Spillover — well-designed MPAs increase catches in adjacent waters, which builds the support needed to sustain them.\n• External stressors — an MPA cannot exclude warming, acidification or plastic, so protection is partial by nature.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): explains establishment at both national and international level and identifies several success factors with evidence.\n• Band 2 (4–6): describes MPAs with limited attention to what makes them work.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that MPAs protect sea life.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ways in which ocean resources contribute to conflict and to cooperation between states.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — conflict:\n• Overlapping territorial and EEZ claims — South China Sea, Aegean, Arctic shelf submissions.\n• Fisheries disputes, including the Cod Wars between the UK and Iceland, and the North Atlantic mackerel dispute as stocks shifted north with warming.\n• Distant-water fleets in West African waters displacing artisanal fishers, with arrests and seizures.\n• Access to hydrocarbon reserves in disputed waters — the Eastern Mediterranean.\n• Chokepoint control and attacks on shipping; piracy off Somalia and in the Gulf of Guinea.\n\nIndicative content — cooperation:\n• UNCLOS itself, a negotiated framework accepted by over 165 states, which converted most potential disputes into rule-governed questions.\n• RFMOs setting shared quotas for straddling and migratory stocks.\n• The 2018 Central Arctic Ocean agreement imposing a precautionary fishing moratorium before exploitation began — governance ahead of the problem.\n• The Ross Sea MPA, agreed by consensus among 25 parties including states with active fishing interests.\n• IMO conventions on pollution and safety with near-universal adherence.\n• Joint development zones where states share revenue from disputed areas rather than resolving sovereignty — a pragmatic mechanism.\n• Counter-piracy naval cooperation involving states otherwise in rivalry.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• Cooperation is more common than conflict, but tends to succeed where the resource is not yet valuable or where mutual benefit is clear, and to fail where sovereignty or major economic value is at stake.\n• Climate change is redistributing resources across boundaries, which will test existing agreements built on historical distributions.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers both with specific named examples and reaches a generalisation about when each occurs.\n• Band 2 (4–6): examples of conflict or cooperation with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that countries argue over fish.` },

      { q: 'Explain why the oceans are described as a global common and the problems this creates.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — why:\n• The high seas lie beyond national jurisdiction and cover around 43% of the planet’s surface; no state may claim sovereignty over them.\n• UNCLOS designates the deep seabed the common heritage of mankind, with benefits to be shared equitably.\n• The services the ocean provides — climate regulation, oxygen production, fisheries — benefit everyone regardless of contribution to their protection.\n\nIndicative content — problems:\n• Tragedy of the commons — open access with no owner means each user gains fully from exploitation while sharing the cost of depletion, so restraint is individually irrational.\n• Free riding — states benefit from others’ conservation without contributing, so agreements are hard to sustain.\n• Enforcement — no authority exists to police the high seas; flag state jurisdiction is exercised largely by open registries.\n• Attribution — pollution and depletion have diffuse sources, so responsibility cannot be assigned.\n• Externalities are unpriced: the ocean’s climate regulation has no market value, so it carries no weight in commercial decisions.\n• Equity — distant-water fleets from wealthy states exploit commons that poorer coastal states cannot access, and deep-sea mining raises the question of who the "mankind" sharing the heritage actually is in practice.\n\nIndicative content — responses:\n• Enclosure through EEZs converted a third of the ocean into managed space, with measurable improvement where governance is strong — evidence that the commons problem is soluble by assigning rights.\n• Ostrom’s work shows commons can be managed successfully where users are identifiable, rules are locally agreed and monitoring is feasible — conditions largely absent on the high seas.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): explains the concept accurately, develops several structural problems, and considers responses.\n• Band 2 (4–6): explains the commons idea with limited development of the problems.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that the sea belongs to everyone.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of global governance in managing the oceans.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — evidence of effectiveness:\n• UNCLOS converted overlapping unilateral claims into a broadly accepted legal framework with over 165 parties, and most maritime boundaries are settled peacefully.\n• EEZs gave coastal states an incentive to manage stocks they now own, with demonstrable recovery in well-governed fisheries — Iceland, Norway, New Zealand.\n• IMO conventions have worked: the 2020 sulphur cap cut emissions measurably; double-hull requirements reduced tanker spills sharply since the 1990s.\n• The Port State Measures Agreement addresses IUU fishing where enforcement is actually feasible.\n• The 2023 BBNJ Agreement closed the largest gap in the framework; the Ross Sea MPA showed consensus is achievable.\n• The 2018 Central Arctic moratorium is a case of pre-emptive governance.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Enforcement remains the structural weakness: flag state jurisdiction, open registries, and no high seas enforcement body.\n• Rulings are unenforceable against unwilling states — China’s rejection of the 2016 arbitration.\n• Consent-based law: the US has not ratified UNCLOS; the BBNJ depends on ratifications not yet complete.\n• Outcomes lag targets: 8.2% of ocean is in MPAs against a 30% goal, and only around 3% is fully protected.\n• 92.7% of assessed stocks are fished at or beyond sustainable limits despite decades of management.\n• Harmful subsidies persisted for two decades of WTO negotiation.\n• Governance is fragmented across bodies with overlapping mandates and no hierarchy.\n\nJudgement: effective at allocating rights and at regulating identifiable, ship-based activity where enforcement is feasible at ports; ineffective at protecting diffuse, unowned resources on the high seas. A defensible conclusion links effectiveness to whether the activity can be observed and attributed — which explains the pattern of successes and failures.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with explicit criteria, accurate evidence on both sides, and a judgement that explains the pattern rather than listing examples.\n• Band 2 (5–8): successes and failures identified with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes agreements with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the oceans cannot be effectively protected while they remain a global common.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• The tragedy of the commons predicts precisely the outcome observed: over-capacity, stock depletion and pollution where access is open.\n• Enforcement on the high seas is structurally weak; flag state jurisdiction fails where registries are open.\n• Free riding undermines agreements: a state that restrains its fleet loses catch to those that do not.\n• Enclosure has worked where applied: EEZs created ownership and, where governance is strong, stocks have recovered — evidence that the commons status is the problem.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Commons can be managed successfully: Ostrom demonstrated that user communities with clear rules, monitoring and sanctions sustain shared resources without privatisation or state control.\n• Ocean commons agreements have worked — the Ross Sea MPA, the Central Arctic moratorium, MARPOL, and the ozone-style success of the IMO sulphur cap.\n• Enclosure has costs and does not guarantee protection: many EEZ fisheries are badly managed, so ownership is not sufficient.\n• Privatising the high seas would exclude states without fleets, offending the common heritage principle and raising serious equity problems.\n• Technology is changing the calculus: satellite monitoring makes the ocean increasingly observable, weakening the assumption that commons cannot be policed.\n• The threats that most endanger the ocean — warming, acidification, plastic — originate on land and would not be solved by changing ocean tenure.\n\nJudgement: commons status makes protection harder but not impossible; the binding constraint is monitoring and enforcement capacity rather than ownership as such, and the largest threats are not governed by ocean tenure at all. A defensible conclusion distinguishes extraction problems, where the commons argument bites, from pollution and climate problems, where it does not.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): engages with commons theory and counter-evidence, distinguishes types of threat, and reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): restates the tragedy of the commons with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the relative importance of different threats to the health of the oceans.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — the threats:\n• Climate change — warming, acidification and deoxygenation; affects the entire ocean simultaneously, is effectively irreversible on human timescales, and cannot be addressed by any marine policy.\n• Overfishing — 92.7% of assessed stocks at or beyond limits; direct, large-scale and immediate, but demonstrably reversible with management, as recovered stocks show.\n• Pollution — plastics (persistent, ubiquitous, but with effects concentrated in particular species and locations); nutrient runoff causing dead zones (severe but regional and reversible); oil and chemical contamination.\n• Habitat destruction — bottom trawling, coastal development, mangrove clearance for aquaculture, dredging.\n• Invasive species via ballast water — locally transformative and irreversible.\n• Emerging — deep-sea mining, with unknown but potentially permanent impacts.\n\nIndicative content — criteria for comparison:\n• Scale — climate change affects all waters; others are regional.\n• Reversibility — fish stocks recover; acidification and species extinction do not.\n• Immediacy — overfishing and habitat destruction are damaging now; some climate effects are prospective.\n• Tractability — overfishing has known solutions requiring only political will; climate requires global decarbonisation.\n• Interaction — threats compound: warming plus overfishing plus nutrient runoff destroys reefs together in ways none would alone, so ranking them separately may mislead.\n\nJudgement: climate change is most important by scale and irreversibility, overfishing by immediacy and magnitude of current damage; the defensible position selects criteria explicitly and notes that the threats are not independent, so addressing the tractable ones buys resilience against the intractable one.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): compares threats against explicit criteria with accurate evidence, recognises interaction, reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): describes several threats with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists threats with little assessment of relative importance.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which the interests of developing countries are protected in ocean governance.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — evidence of protection:\n• UNCLOS gave every coastal state a 200 nm EEZ regardless of power, transferring enormous resource areas to small states — Kiribati holds an EEZ of over 3.4 million km² from 811 km² of land.\n• The common heritage principle requires equitable sharing of deep seabed benefits, including with landlocked and developing states, and the ISA must take their interests into account.\n• The BBNJ Agreement includes capacity building and technology transfer provisions and benefit sharing from marine genetic resources.\n• Special treatment in some RFMO allocations and in the WTO fisheries subsidies agreement.\n• Small island states have exercised significant diplomatic influence through coalitions such as AOSIS.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of inadequate protection:\n• Rights without capacity: many developing states cannot monitor or police their own EEZs, so distant-water fleets fish them with impunity — IUU fishing in West African waters is a standard example.\n• Access agreements are often negotiated on unfavourable terms, with revenue far below the value of the catch extracted.\n• Deep-sea mining requires capital and technology held by a few firms; small sponsoring states bear legal liability while corporations take the profit.\n• Climate impacts fall hardest on those least responsible — sea-level rise threatens the existence of low-lying states, and could extinguish the EEZs on which their economies depend.\n• Marine genetic resource patents are overwhelmingly held by a handful of wealthy countries and firms.\n• Participation costs — sustained representation at multiple treaty bodies is expensive, so the poorest states are under-represented where rules are made.\n\nJudgement: formally well protected, substantively much less so — the framework grants rights without the capacity to exercise them, so protection on paper coexists with continued extraction of value. A defensible conclusion distinguishes formal from effective protection.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment distinguishing formal rights from effective capacity, with accurate examples and a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): identifies protections and shortcomings with some evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that developing countries are treated unfairly.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that national self-interest will always prevent effective global governance of the oceans. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• States are the only actors with enforcement capacity, and they act on national interest; there is no authority above them.\n• The 2016 South China Sea arbitration was simply rejected by the losing party, with no consequence — the clearest demonstration that law binds only the willing.\n• Free riding is structurally rewarded: a state restraining its fleet loses catch to those that do not.\n• Harmful fishing subsidies persisted through two decades of WTO negotiation because domestic fishing constituencies are politically powerful.\n• The US has signed but not ratified UNCLOS, protecting freedom of action.\n• Flags of convenience exist because states profit from offering weak regulation.\n• Arctic cooperation has weakened sharply since 2022, showing that cooperation is contingent on wider geopolitics.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• UNCLOS itself is a major achievement of collective self-limitation, with over 165 parties, and most maritime boundaries are settled peacefully.\n• The BBNJ Agreement (2023) was concluded after two decades of negotiation, showing the system can still deliver.\n• The Ross Sea MPA was agreed by consensus among 25 parties, including states with active fishing interests — so interests can be reconciled.\n• The IMO 2020 sulphur cap was implemented globally with measurable results, as were double-hull requirements.\n• Self-interest can favour cooperation: EEZs work because ownership aligns private and collective interest; port state measures succeed because states control their own ports.\n• Reputation, reciprocity and linkage across issues give states reasons to comply beyond immediate gain.\n• Non-state actors — NGOs, consumers, insurers, corporations — exert pressure independently of states; certification and satellite transparency change incentives.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• "Always" is the weak point: the record shows failure where interests conflict sharply and success where they align or where enforcement is feasible at low cost.\n• The design question matters more than the motive: governance succeeds when it works with self-interest (property rights, port control, observable compliance) rather than against it.\n• A strong answer distinguishes issue types — sovereignty and high-value extraction resist governance; shipping standards and pollution control do not — and commits to a position rather than concluding neutrally.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning engaging directly with "always"; wide range of accurate evidence and named agreements; explains the pattern of success and failure rather than listing cases; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation of both sides with accurate support and a clear judgement, less fully resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes examples of cooperation and conflict with limited evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–5): isolated assertions about countries acting selfishly, with little evidence.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the effectiveness of attempts to manage one ocean resource or issue you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named resource or issue. Atlantic and North Sea fisheries under the EU Common Fisheries Policy are used below; credit whaling and the IWC, Antarctic krill under CCAMLR, or marine plastics at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — the issue and the response:\n• European stocks were severely depleted by the 1990s; the CFP, established in 1983 and reformed in 2002 and 2013, manages access and catch across member states.\n• Mechanisms: total allowable catches set annually from ICES scientific advice; relative stability keys allocating national shares; technical measures on mesh and minimum sizes; effort limits; the 2013 landing obligation banning discards; structural funds for fleet reduction.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of effectiveness:\n• The proportion of assessed North East Atlantic stocks fished within sustainable limits rose substantially after the 2013 reform, from a minority to a majority.\n• North Sea cod, plaice and herring showed recovery from very low levels.\n• Fleet capacity was reduced, addressing the over-capacity that drives overfishing.\n• The landing obligation, in principle, ended the waste of discarding.\n• Regional cooperation prevented the unilateral race that would otherwise have followed.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• For decades, ministers set TACs above scientific advice — in some years by 20–40% — because annual negotiation rewarded short-term national interest over stock recovery.\n• The landing obligation has proved very difficult to enforce at sea, with limited observer coverage and widespread non-compliance reported.\n• Relative stability keys are based on 1970s catch patterns and have not adjusted as stocks shifted north with warming, contributing to the mackerel dispute with Norway, Iceland and the Faroes.\n• Mixed fisheries make single-species quotas difficult, producing choke species that tie up quota.\n• Brexit fragmented management of shared stocks, adding a further negotiating layer.\n• Social cost to fishing communities from capacity reduction, with limited alternative employment.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — stock recovery, compliance, economic viability of the fleet, social sustainability, adaptability to change — noting different performance against each.\n• Substantial success on stock status after 2013, poor on enforcement and on adapting allocation to a changing ocean.\n• Timescale matters: judged against the 1990s the improvement is marked; judged against the scientific advice available throughout, the delay was costly and avoidable.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate evidence including figures and dates, genuine engagement with why some elements failed, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the management regime with some evaluative comment; detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of fishing or pollution management with little specific detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.5': {
    name: '21st Century Challenges',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain the difference between economic development and human development.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Economic development measures the growth of output and income, typically through GDP or GNI per capita (1)\n• It says nothing about how that income is distributed, or about what it delivers for people (1)\n• Human development concerns the expansion of people’s capabilities and freedoms — health, education and standard of living (1)\n• The HDI combines life expectancy, schooling and GNI per capita, so a country can have high income and only moderate human development, as in several oil-exporting states (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what the Human Development Index measures and state one limitation.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The HDI is a composite index combining life expectancy at birth, mean and expected years of schooling, and GNI per capita at purchasing power parity (1)\n• Each is normalised to a 0–1 scale and the geometric mean taken, so poor performance on one dimension is not fully offset by another (1)\n• Limitation: it is a national average that conceals inequality — which is why the Inequality-adjusted HDI was introduced (1)\n• It also omits political freedom, security, environmental quality and gender equality, so two countries with identical HDI can offer very different lives (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what the Gini coefficient measures and what values of 0 and 1 represent.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The Gini coefficient measures the degree of inequality in the distribution of income or wealth within a population (1)\n• A value of 0 represents perfect equality, where every person receives an identical share (1)\n• A value of 1 represents perfect inequality, where one person receives everything; most countries fall between about 0.25 (Scandinavia) and 0.60 (South Africa) (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between absolute and relative poverty.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Absolute poverty is an inability to meet basic needs for survival — food, clean water, shelter, sanitation — measured against a fixed international line, currently $2.15 a day at 2017 PPP (1)\n• Relative poverty is measured against the standard of the society in which a person lives, commonly below 60% of median income (1)\n• Absolute poverty can be eliminated in principle; relative poverty is a measure of inequality and persists in wealthy countries — so the two can move in opposite directions (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by interdependence and give one example at the global scale.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Interdependence is the mutual dependence of places and countries on one another through flows of trade, capital, people, information and resources (1)\n• It means events in one place produce consequences elsewhere, and that no country is self-sufficient (1)\n• Example: global supply chains, where a semiconductor shortage or the grounding of a single vessel in the Suez Canal disrupts manufacturing worldwide; also accept financial contagion in 2008 or the spread of COVID-19 (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between globalisation and global interdependence.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Globalisation is the process by which places become more connected through increasing flows of trade, investment, people, technology and culture (1)\n• Interdependence is the resulting condition — the state of mutual reliance those flows create (1)\n• Globalisation describes the process and can slow or reverse (deglobalisation); interdependence describes the outcome and the vulnerability it produces, which persists even where flows reduce (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the development gap.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The development gap is the disparity in living standards and wellbeing between the richest and poorest countries and populations (1)\n• It is measured using indicators such as GNI per capita, HDI, life expectancy and infant mortality (1)\n• The gap between countries has narrowed in recent decades as China, India and others have grown rapidly, while inequality within many countries has widened — so whether "the gap" is closing depends on which gap is meant (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what the Sustainable Development Goals are and how they differ from the Millennium Development Goals.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The SDGs are 17 goals with 169 targets, agreed by the UN in 2015 for achievement by 2030 (1)\n• They cover poverty, hunger, health, education, gender equality, water, energy, work, inequality, cities, consumption, climate, oceans, land, peace and partnership (1)\n• They are universal — applying to every country, not only developing ones — unlike the MDGs, which framed development as something done to poorer countries (1)\n• They integrate environmental sustainability with social and economic aims, recognising the goals interact, and were developed through far wider consultation; they remain non-binding (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by an ecological footprint and by biocapacity.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An ecological footprint is the area of biologically productive land and water required to supply the resources a population consumes and absorb its waste, expressed in global hectares (1)\n• Biocapacity is the area actually available to provide those services (1)\n• Where footprint exceeds biocapacity the population is in ecological deficit, drawing down natural capital or relying on imports — humanity as a whole currently uses the equivalent of roughly 1.7 Earths (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the demographic dividend.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A demographic dividend is the potential economic gain arising when a fall in fertility produces a large working-age population relative to dependants (1)\n• Fewer children to support means households and governments can invest more per person in education, health and capital (1)\n• It is a window rather than a guarantee: it requires jobs, education and health provision to be in place, or a large young population becomes unemployment rather than growth — East Asia captured it, and sub-Saharan Africa’s window is opening now (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between mitigation and adaptation as responses to climate change.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Mitigation reduces the causes of climate change — cutting greenhouse gas emissions or enhancing sinks through renewable energy, efficiency, afforestation and carbon capture (1)\n• Adaptation reduces the consequences — flood defences, drought-resistant crops, early warning systems, managed retreat (1)\n• Mitigation benefits everyone globally and over the long term; adaptation benefits the place that invests in it, and more immediately — which is why adaptation is politically easier and mitigation suffers from free riding (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by climate justice.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Climate justice frames climate change as an ethical and political issue rather than only an environmental one (1)\n• Those least responsible for emissions typically suffer the greatest impacts: sub-Saharan Africa has contributed around 3% of cumulative emissions but faces severe drought and heat exposure (1)\n• It invokes historic responsibility — that countries industrialised earliest have emitted most cumulatively — and capacity to act (1)\n• Practical expressions include the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities, the $100 billion climate finance pledge, and the loss and damage fund agreed at COP27 (1)` },

      { q: 'A country’s GNI per capita is $4,200, life expectancy 68 years and mean years of schooling 7.4. The richest 10% receive 41% of income and the poorest 40% receive 14%. Calculate the ratio between these two groups’ average incomes.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Richest 10% receive 41% of income, so income per person in that decile is 41 ÷ 10 = 4.1 units per 1% of population (1)\n• Poorest 40% receive 14%, so 14 ÷ 40 = 0.35 units per 1% of population (1)\n• Ratio = 4.1 ÷ 0.35 (1)\n• = 11.7 : 1 (1)\n• The richest tenth therefore average nearly twelve times the income of the poorest four-tenths; the GNI per capita figure of $4,200 conceals this entirely, which is why average income is a poor guide to typical living standards (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct per-capita reasoning and the point about averages.` },

      { q: 'The global population living in extreme poverty fell from 1.9 billion in 1990 to 0.7 billion in 2019, while world population rose from 5.3 to 7.7 billion. Calculate the percentage in poverty at each date and the change in that percentage.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1990: 1.9 ÷ 5.3 × 100 = 35.8% (1)\n• 2019: 0.7 ÷ 7.7 × 100 = 9.1% (1)\n• Fall in the proportion = 35.8 − 9.1 = 26.7 percentage points (1)\n• In absolute terms 1.2 billion fewer people, achieved while population grew by 2.4 billion (1)\n• Much of the reduction is accounted for by China alone, so the global figure conceals very uneven progress — poverty has fallen far less in sub-Saharan Africa, where it is increasingly concentrated (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both percentages, the change, and recognition that the aggregate conceals distribution.` },

      { q: 'Country A has an HDI of 0.92 and an Inequality-adjusted HDI of 0.88. Country B has an HDI of 0.78 and an IHDI of 0.56. Calculate the percentage loss due to inequality in each and analyse what this shows.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Country A loss = (0.92 − 0.88) ÷ 0.92 × 100 = 4.3% (1)\n• Country B loss = (0.78 − 0.56) ÷ 0.78 × 100 = 28.2% (1)\n• Country B loses over six times as much of its human development to inequality (1)\n• The headline HDI gap between them is 0.14, but the inequality-adjusted gap is 0.32 — more than double (1)\n• This shows that national averages systematically overstate the position of unequal societies, and that the typical person in Country B is much further behind than the HDI alone suggests (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and the comparative interpretation.` },

      { q: 'A country emits 0.4 tonnes CO₂ per person against a global mean of 4.7 tonnes, with a population of 61 million. Calculate its total annual emissions and the tonnage if its per-capita emissions rose to the global mean.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Current total = 0.4 × 61,000,000 = 24,400,000 tonnes, or 24.4 Mt (1)\n• At the global mean = 4.7 × 61,000,000 (1)\n• = 286,700,000 tonnes, or 286.7 Mt (1)\n• An increase of 262.3 Mt, nearly twelvefold (1)\n• This is the central tension in climate negotiation: development to the current global average would add emissions on this scale, so demanding restraint from such countries without finance or technology transfer asks them to forgo development others already had (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and the equity implication.` },

      { q: 'Aid to a country is $840 million, remittances $3.2 billion, and FDI $1.6 billion. Debt repayments are $2.1 billion. Calculate total inflows, net financial flow, and aid as a percentage of inflows.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Total inflows = 0.84 + 3.2 + 1.6 = $5.64 billion (1)\n• Net flow = 5.64 − 2.1 = $3.54 billion (1)\n• Aid as a percentage of inflows = 0.84 ÷ 5.64 × 100 (1)\n• = 14.9% (1)\n• Remittances are the largest single source at 57% of inflows, nearly four times aid — and debt repayment removes over a third of all inflows, so debt servicing is a larger factor in the country’s finances than aid (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires all calculations and a developed interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Urban population rose from 751 million in 1950 to 4.4 billion in 2020, against world populations of 2.5 billion and 7.8 billion. Calculate the urban proportion at each date and the percentage point change.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1950: 751 ÷ 2,500 × 100 = 30.0% (1)\n• 2020: 4,400 ÷ 7,800 × 100 = 56.4% (1)\n• Change = 56.4 − 30.0 = 26.4 percentage points (1)\n• In absolute terms the urban population rose almost six-fold while world population rose roughly three-fold (1)\n• Future growth is projected to be almost entirely urban and concentrated in Africa and Asia, so the challenge is where urbanisation is fastest and capacity to provide housing and services is weakest (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires accurate calculation and interpretation beyond description.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes of global inequality.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — historical:\n• Colonialism extracted resources and shaped economies toward primary export, leaving weak institutions and arbitrary borders at independence.\n• Early industrialisation gave some countries a cumulative advantage in capital, technology and institutions.\n\nIndicative content — economic:\n• Terms of trade — primary commodity prices are volatile and have tended to decline relative to manufactures, so commodity exporters must export more to import the same.\n• Trade rules and agricultural subsidies in wealthy countries limiting market access.\n• Debt servicing diverting revenue from health and education.\n• Concentration of FDI in a small number of countries and sectors.\n• Capital flight and tax avoidance by transnational corporations.\n\nIndicative content — physical and demographic:\n• Landlocked position raising trade costs; disease burden; climate and soil constraints on agriculture; natural hazard exposure.\n• Resource curse — mineral wealth associated with corruption, currency overvaluation and conflict rather than development.\n\nIndicative content — political:\n• Conflict destroying infrastructure and deterring investment; weak institutions, corruption and insecure property rights.\n• Within countries: unequal access to education, health and land; discrimination by gender, ethnicity and region.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): causes covered across several categories with accurate examples and recognition that they compound one another.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several causes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): isolated statements about some countries being poor.` },

      { q: 'Explain the consequences of global inequality.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — for individuals and societies:\n• Health — large differences in life expectancy, infant and maternal mortality; disease burdens that are treatable at low cost remain untreated.\n• Education — lower enrolment and attainment, perpetuating inequality across generations.\n• Nutrition and food insecurity; vulnerability to price shocks.\n\nIndicative content — economic and political:\n• Inequality within countries is associated with lower social mobility and, on some evidence, slower growth, since talent is not matched to opportunity.\n• Political instability, unrest and erosion of trust in institutions; elite capture of policy.\n• Migration — wage differentials are the strongest driver of international movement, so inequality produces flows that themselves become politically contentious.\n• Brain drain removing skilled workers from the countries that trained them.\n\nIndicative content — global:\n• Conflict over resources and unequal bargaining power in trade and climate negotiation.\n• Unequal capacity to adapt to climate change, so the same hazard produces very different outcomes — vulnerability is a function of inequality.\n• Pandemic risk: unequal vaccine access during COVID-19 prolonged transmission globally, showing that inequality creates shared costs.\n• Environmental — the wealthiest 10% account for around half of consumption emissions, while impacts fall on the poorest.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): consequences at several scales with quantification and recognition that costs are shared, not only borne by the poor.\n• Band 2 (3–5): consequences listed with limited development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that poor people have worse lives.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ways in which countries are interdependent.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — economic:\n• Trade — specialisation and comparative advantage mean no country produces all it consumes; global supply chains distribute production of a single product across many countries.\n• Finance — capital flows, foreign investment and integrated banking, so financial shocks propagate, as in 2008.\n• Labour — migration supplying workers in destination countries and remittances to origin countries.\n• Resources — energy and mineral dependence; European reliance on Russian gas before 2022.\n\nIndicative content — environmental:\n• Shared atmosphere and oceans mean emissions and pollution anywhere affect everyone.\n• Transboundary rivers, migratory species, and shared fish stocks.\n• Deforestation in one country affecting rainfall and carbon storage globally.\n\nIndicative content — political, social and technological:\n• International organisations, alliances and treaty obligations.\n• Information and communication flows; cultural exchange and diaspora networks.\n• Disease — pandemics spreading through the same networks that carry trade and travel.\n• Technology transfer and shared research.\n\nIndicative content — the key analytical point:\n• Interdependence is usually asymmetric: dependence is rarely equal in both directions, so it generates power as well as mutual benefit — which is why it produces vulnerability and leverage rather than simple partnership.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several dimensions covered with examples and the asymmetry point developed.\n• Band 2 (3–5): dimensions identified with limited exemplification.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that countries trade with each other.` },

      { q: 'Explain the challenges created by rapid urbanisation in lower-income countries.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — housing and services:\n• Formal housing supply cannot match arrival rates, so informal settlements develop — around a billion people live in slums globally, with Dharavi and Kibera as standard examples.\n• Water and sanitation provision lags, producing disease risk; waste collection inadequate.\n• Electricity access informal and unreliable.\n\nIndicative content — economic:\n• Formal job creation lags population growth, so a large informal sector develops — insecure, unregulated and untaxed, which limits municipal revenue.\n• Municipal finance is weak precisely where investment need is greatest.\n\nIndicative content — environmental and social:\n• Air and water pollution; traffic congestion.\n• Settlement on hazardous land — steep slopes, floodplains — because safe land is occupied or unaffordable, so urbanisation increases disaster exposure.\n• Inequality and spatial segregation; crime and insecurity.\n• Loss of agricultural land on the urban fringe.\n\nIndicative content — balance:\n• Urbanisation also delivers benefits: higher productivity through agglomeration, better access to education and health than rural areas, and lower per-capita infrastructure costs — so the challenge is managing it, not preventing it.\n• Planned responses: site-and-service schemes, in-situ upgrading, secure tenure, and investment in transport.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): challenges across several dimensions with examples, plus recognition of the benefits and of management responses.\n• Band 2 (3–5): challenges listed with limited development.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that cities become overcrowded.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of global food insecurity.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Conflict is the leading driver of acute food insecurity, disrupting production, markets and aid access — Yemen, Sudan, Ukraine.\n• Climate shocks — drought, flood and heat reducing yields; East African drought and South Asian heatwaves.\n• Poverty and inequality — the world produces enough calories, so hunger is predominantly a problem of access and affordability rather than supply.\n• Price volatility transmitted through globally integrated markets; the Ukraine war raised wheat and fertiliser prices worldwide.\n• Land degradation, water scarcity and competition from biofuels.\n• Dietary shift toward meat, which requires far more land and water per calorie.\n• Post-harvest loss and waste — roughly a third of food produced is lost or wasted.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Malnutrition and stunting, with lifelong effects on cognitive development and earnings, transmitting poverty across generations.\n• Increased mortality, particularly among children.\n• Political instability — food price spikes contributed to unrest preceding the Arab Spring.\n• Migration and displacement.\n• Economic cost through lost productivity and healthcare.\n• Environmental — pressure to expand cultivation into forest and marginal land.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): causes and consequences both covered with examples and the access-not-supply point made.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several causes with limited attention to consequences or vice versa.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that some places do not have enough food.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of transnational corporations in the global economy.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — what they do:\n• Operate across borders, locating each function — research, manufacture, assembly, administration — where conditions are most favourable, creating the global shift of production.\n• Control global value chains, often without owning the factories, through contracting.\n• Move capital, technology and management practice between countries.\n\nIndicative content — positive effects:\n• Employment and wages, frequently above local alternatives; skills and technology transfer.\n• Multiplier effects through local supply chains and infrastructure investment.\n• Tax revenue, and access to export markets that host firms could not reach alone.\n• Consumer benefit through lower prices.\n\nIndicative content — negative effects:\n• Labour conditions — low wages, long hours and poor safety in some supply chains; the Rana Plaza collapse in 2013 killed over 1,100 garment workers.\n• Race to the bottom as states compete on tax, wages and regulation to attract investment.\n• Profit repatriation and transfer pricing reducing host-country tax revenue, prompting the OECD global minimum corporate tax.\n• Footloose behaviour — relocation when incentives expire, leaving unemployment.\n• Environmental impacts, and political influence through lobbying disproportionate to any single government.\n• Scale point: several TNCs have revenues exceeding the GDP of most countries, so the relationship is unequal.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): role explained with balanced effects, named examples and attention to the power asymmetry.\n• Band 2 (3–5): describes TNCs with one-sided effects.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that big companies operate in many countries.` },

      { q: 'Explain the different forms of international aid and their effectiveness.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — forms:\n• Bilateral — government to government, often with conditions attached; historically sometimes tied to purchases from the donor.\n• Multilateral — channelled through the World Bank, UN agencies or regional banks; less politically directed.\n• Emergency/humanitarian — short-term relief after disaster or conflict.\n• Development aid — long-term investment in infrastructure, health and education.\n• NGO aid — typically smaller-scale and community-based.\n• Top-down large infrastructure versus bottom-up intermediate technology approaches.\n\nIndicative content — effectiveness:\n• Successes: vaccination and disease eradication programmes; Gavi and the Global Fund have saved millions of lives at low cost per life.\n• Criticisms: dependency; distortion of local markets, as with food aid undercutting farmers; corruption and leakage; donor priorities overriding recipient needs; volatility and unpredictability hampering planning.\n• Tied aid returns much of the value to the donor economy.\n• Scale — remittances now exceed official development assistance to low- and middle-income countries by a wide margin, so aid is not the principal financial flow.\n• Evidence on aggregate growth effects is genuinely contested; evidence for targeted health interventions is much stronger, which suggests sector matters more than volume.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): forms distinguished accurately with balanced assessment of effectiveness and examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): forms listed with limited evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that rich countries give money to poor ones.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ways in which climate change will affect global development.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Agriculture — yields fall in already-hot regions while some high-latitude areas gain, so impacts are regressive; smallholder farmers dependent on rainfall are most exposed.\n• Water — altered rainfall, glacier loss and increased drought affecting supply and hydroelectric generation.\n• Health — heat stress limiting outdoor labour productivity; expanded range of vector-borne disease; malnutrition.\n• Disasters — more intense cyclones, flooding and wildfire, destroying infrastructure and diverting budgets from development to reconstruction.\n• Sea-level rise threatening delta populations, coastal cities and the existence of low-lying states.\n• Displacement and migration, predominantly internal.\n• Economic — lost productivity, higher insurance costs, and diversion of scarce capital into adaptation rather than growth.\n• Equity — those least responsible are most affected and least able to adapt, so climate change widens existing inequality rather than distributing costs evenly.\n• Development pathways constrained: pressure not to industrialise using fossil fuels imposes a cost on latecomers unless finance and technology are transferred — the core of the climate justice argument.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several development dimensions covered with examples and the equity dimension explicit.\n• Band 2 (3–5): impacts listed with limited development focus.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that climate change is bad for poor countries.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for, and consequences of, the global shift in manufacturing.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — reasons:\n• Labour cost differentials — wages a fraction of those in industrialised countries.\n• Containerisation and falling transport costs making distance less significant.\n• Communications technology allowing management of dispersed operations in real time.\n• Trade liberalisation — WTO accession, tariff reduction, export processing zones offering tax incentives.\n• Less stringent environmental and labour regulation in some destinations.\n• Large domestic markets and improving infrastructure and skills, particularly in China after 1978.\n• Agglomeration — once clusters form, suppliers and skilled labour concentrate, making relocation self-reinforcing.\n\nIndicative content — consequences for receiving countries:\n• Rapid industrialisation, employment and poverty reduction — the principal mechanism behind China’s lifting of hundreds of millions out of poverty.\n• Urbanisation and rural-to-urban migration; rising wages over time.\n• Environmental degradation and pollution; labour exploitation in some sectors.\n• Dependence on export demand, exposing economies to external shocks.\n\nIndicative content — consequences for origin countries:\n• Deindustrialisation, structural unemployment in manufacturing regions, and long-term regional decline.\n• Growth of the service and knowledge economy, widening regional inequality within countries.\n• Cheaper consumer goods, benefiting consumers including those whose jobs were lost.\n• Political consequences — support for protectionism and anti-globalisation politics.\n\nIndicative content — recent change:\n• Rising Chinese wages have shifted production to Vietnam, Bangladesh and Ethiopia; automation and supply chain resilience concerns are prompting some reshoring.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): reasons and consequences for both sets of countries covered accurately with examples and recent developments.\n• Band 2 (4–6): reasons covered with consequences one-sided.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that factories moved to where labour is cheap.` },

      { q: 'Explain why the impacts of global challenges are unevenly distributed.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — exposure:\n• Physical geography determines who faces which hazard — low-lying delta and island states face sea-level rise; the Sahel faces drought; the Caribbean faces hurricanes.\n• Many of the most exposed populations are in tropical regions where climate impacts are most severe.\n\nIndicative content — vulnerability:\n• Poverty limits the ability to prepare, to absorb a shock and to recover; households without savings or insurance sell assets, entrenching poverty.\n• Dependence on climate-sensitive livelihoods — rainfed agriculture, fishing.\n• Weak infrastructure, health systems and housing quality; settlement on hazardous land.\n• Within countries, impacts fall unevenly by income, gender, age, ethnicity and disability.\n\nIndicative content — capacity to respond:\n• Wealthy states can fund defences, early warning, healthcare and reconstruction; the same hazard produces very different mortality — comparable cyclones kill in the hundreds in Bangladesh today against hundreds of thousands in 1970, because of warning systems and shelters.\n• Governance quality determines whether resources reach those affected.\n• Access to international finance, insurance and technology.\n\nIndicative content — responsibility versus impact:\n• Those contributing least to emissions face the greatest consequences, so distribution of impact is close to the inverse of responsibility — the core injustice.\n• The same asymmetry applies to pandemics, financial crises and food price shocks: global integration transmits shocks universally while capacity to absorb them is unequal.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): distinguishes exposure, vulnerability and capacity with examples, and addresses the responsibility mismatch.\n• Band 2 (4–6): identifies uneven impacts with partial explanation of why.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that poor countries suffer more.` },

      { q: 'Explain how globalisation has affected patterns of development.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — convergence:\n• Rapid growth in East and South Asia has narrowed the gap between countries; China and India account for most of the global reduction in extreme poverty since 1990.\n• Access to export markets, FDI and technology transfer enabled industrialisation without the long accumulation earlier industrialisers required.\n• Global middle class has expanded substantially.\n\nIndicative content — divergence:\n• Benefits have been geographically concentrated: much of sub-Saharan Africa has participated far less, and extreme poverty is increasingly concentrated there.\n• Inequality within countries has widened in most cases, even where average incomes rose — so between-country convergence has coincided with within-country divergence.\n• Commodity exporters remain exposed to price volatility and have not diversified.\n• Deindustrialisation in origin countries has produced sharp regional decline within wealthy economies.\n\nIndicative content — mechanisms and qualifications:\n• Countries that captured most benefit generally managed their integration — selective liberalisation, investment in education and infrastructure — rather than opening indiscriminately.\n• Global value chains allow entry at a single stage of production, lowering the barrier to industrialisation but capturing a small share of value.\n• Tax competition and profit shifting limit the revenue states capture from foreign investment.\n• Environmental costs are frequently displaced to producing countries.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers convergence and divergence with quantification and distinguishes between-country from within-country inequality.\n• Band 2 (4–6): effects identified with limited differentiation or evidence.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that globalisation made some countries richer.` },

      { q: 'Explain the challenges of achieving the Sustainable Development Goals.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — structural challenges:\n• Finance — the funding gap is estimated in trillions of dollars annually, far exceeding aid flows.\n• Non-binding — there is no enforcement mechanism, so delivery depends on national will.\n• Breadth — 17 goals and 169 targets dilute focus and make prioritisation unavoidable in practice.\n• Data — many countries lack the statistical capacity to measure progress, so accountability is weak where need is greatest.\n\nIndicative content — conflicts between goals:\n• Economic growth (SDG 8) can conflict with climate action (SDG 13) and responsible consumption (SDG 12) where growth is fossil-fuelled.\n• Agricultural expansion for zero hunger (SDG 2) can conflict with life on land (SDG 15).\n• Trade-offs are rarely acknowledged in the framework, which presents the goals as mutually reinforcing.\n\nIndicative content — shocks and setbacks:\n• COVID-19 reversed progress on poverty, education and health, with learning losses that persist.\n• Conflict — Ukraine, Sudan, Gaza — destroying progress and diverting finance.\n• Debt distress in many low-income countries, with servicing costs exceeding health spending.\n• Climate impacts undermining gains already made.\n\nIndicative content — governance:\n• Requires coordinated action by states with divergent interests and no compulsion.\n• Progress is very uneven between and within countries; global aggregates conceal this.\n• The UN itself assesses that a majority of targets are off track for 2030.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several categories of challenge explained, including conflicts between goals, with evidence.\n• Band 2 (4–6): challenges identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that the goals are difficult and expensive.` },

      { q: 'Explain how global systems create both opportunities and vulnerabilities for places.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — opportunities:\n• Access to markets far larger than the domestic one, allowing specialisation and scale.\n• Inward investment bringing capital, technology and employment — Nissan in Sunderland, electronics assembly in Shenzhen.\n• Remittances and diaspora networks supporting origin communities.\n• Knowledge, technology and medical advances diffusing rapidly.\n• Tourism income and cultural exchange.\n\nIndicative content — vulnerabilities:\n• Dependence on external demand — a place specialised in one export is exposed to a single market; single-employer towns are exposed to one firm’s decisions.\n• Shock transmission — the 2008 financial crisis, COVID-19 and the Suez blockage all propagated globally through the same networks that carry benefit.\n• Footloose capital — investment that arrives can leave, and the threat of leaving constrains local policy.\n• Price volatility for commodity-dependent economies.\n• Competition — the global shift that industrialised some places deindustrialised others.\n• Loss of local distinctiveness and of control over decisions taken elsewhere.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• Opportunity and vulnerability are two aspects of the same connection: what makes a place able to benefit is what makes it exposed.\n• Resilience depends on diversification and on retaining some decision-making capacity locally; places with one function and no alternatives are most exposed.\n• The distribution is uneven — well-connected, diversified places capture opportunity while peripheral or specialised places absorb the vulnerability.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): covers both with examples and develops the point that they are the same phenomenon.\n• Band 2 (4–6): opportunities and vulnerabilities listed with limited connection.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that globalisation helps some places and harms others.` },

      { q: 'Explain the reasons for differing levels of development within a single country.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical factors:\n• Resource distribution — coalfields and ports historically determined where industry located.\n• Relief, soil quality and climate constraining agriculture; remoteness and accessibility.\n\nIndicative content — economic factors:\n• Cumulative causation — Myrdal’s model: initial advantage attracts investment and skilled labour, which reinforces advantage, while backwash draws resources from the periphery.\n• Agglomeration economies concentrating high-value activity in core regions — London and the South East account for a disproportionate share of UK output.\n• Deindustrialisation affecting regions specialised in declining sectors, with long-lasting effects.\n• Infrastructure investment following existing economic weight rather than need.\n\nIndicative content — social and political factors:\n• Educational provision and attainment differences; selective out-migration of the young and qualified from peripheral regions.\n• Discrimination by ethnicity, caste or region affecting access to opportunity.\n• Political power concentrated in the core, so policy reflects core interests.\n• Historical legacy — colonial-era investment in export corridors leaving interiors underdeveloped in many countries.\n\nIndicative content — patterns and responses:\n• Core–periphery structures at national scale; urban–rural divides; inequality within cities often exceeding that between regions.\n• Policy responses: regional development agencies, devolution, transport investment, enterprise zones — with mixed and contested results.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several categories of cause explained with a model or concept applied and located examples.\n• Band 2 (4–6): causes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that some areas are richer than others.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the resource curse.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The resource curse describes the tendency of countries rich in natural resources, particularly oil and minerals, to grow more slowly and develop less than resource-poor countries (1)\n• Export earnings raise the exchange rate, making other exports uncompetitive and discouraging diversification — Dutch disease (1)\n• Concentrated, easily captured revenue encourages corruption and rent-seeking, and weakens the incentive to build a broad tax base and accountable institutions (1)\n• Revenue volatility destabilises public finances, and control of resources frequently fuels conflict; Botswana and Norway show the curse is avoidable with strong institutions and sovereign wealth funds (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how technology can help address 21st century global challenges, and the limits of technological solutions.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — contributions:\n• Energy — collapsing costs of solar, wind and batteries have made decarbonisation economically rational rather than merely desirable; arguably the single largest contributor to emissions reduction.\n• Agriculture — improved varieties, precision application of water and fertiliser, and drought tolerance raising yields on existing land.\n• Health — vaccine platforms developed in months during COVID-19; mobile diagnostics extending healthcare where clinics are scarce.\n• Information — mobile money extending financial services to the unbanked (M-Pesa); satellite monitoring enabling enforcement against illegal fishing and deforestation.\n• Water — desalination and recycling creating supply independent of rainfall.\n\nIndicative content — limits:\n• Access is unequal: technology diffuses to those who can pay, so it can widen inequality before narrowing it.\n• Rebound effects — efficiency gains absorbed by increased consumption, so savings are smaller than predicted.\n• New dependencies and impacts — the mineral demand of the energy transition creates its own extraction problems.\n• Many challenges are political rather than technical: hunger persists despite sufficient global food production, so the binding constraint is distribution and power, not capability.\n• Reliance on future technology — negative emissions at scale — transfers risk to the future and can justify present inaction.\n• Deployment requires institutions, finance and skills that the places most in need often lack.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several contributions explained with examples and substantive limits including the political point.\n• Band 2 (3–5): contributions identified with limited attention to limits.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that technology can solve problems.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that economic growth is the most effective way of reducing poverty.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Historically the strongest association: China’s growth after 1978 lifted hundreds of millions out of extreme poverty, accounting for the majority of the global reduction.\n• Growth generates employment and raises wages, and expands the tax base that funds health, education and social protection.\n• No country has substantially reduced poverty without sustained growth.\n• It is self-sustaining in a way that redistribution or aid is not.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Growth does not automatically reach the poorest — its poverty-reducing effect depends on distribution; where inequality rises, growth benefits the top and poverty falls slowly.\n• Jobless growth in extractive sectors creates little employment; the resource curse illustrates growth without development.\n• Direct interventions can outperform growth per unit of resource: vaccination, cash transfers, and girls’ education deliver measurable poverty reduction quickly.\n• Kerala achieved high human development at low income through public provision, showing the two can be decoupled.\n• Environmental limits — growth based on fossil fuels and resource extraction imposes costs that fall on the poor.\n• Relative poverty is unaffected by growth alone, since it is defined against the median.\n\nJudgement: growth is necessary but not sufficient — its effect on poverty depends almost entirely on whether it is broad-based and on whether the revenue is used for public provision. A defensible conclusion argues that the question is wrongly framed as growth versus redistribution, since sustained poverty reduction requires both.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with contrasting evidence, explicit criteria, and a judgement engaging with the distributional condition.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that growth makes countries richer.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which global interdependence is beneficial.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — benefits:\n• Specialisation and trade raise output and lower consumer prices.\n• Access to capital, technology and markets enabling rapid development — East Asian industrialisation.\n• Diffusion of knowledge and medical advances; COVID-19 vaccines developed and distributed in unprecedented time.\n• Mutual dependence raises the cost of conflict, a stabilising influence between trading partners.\n• Collective action becomes possible on problems no state can solve alone — the Montreal Protocol.\n\nIndicative content — costs:\n• Shock transmission — 2008 financial contagion, pandemic spread, and supply chain failure propagate through the same connections.\n• Asymmetric dependence creates leverage rather than partnership; European gas dependence on Russia became a strategic vulnerability.\n• Loss of policy autonomy — states constrained by capital mobility and by the threat of relocation.\n• Uneven distribution of gains, within and between countries, fuelling political backlash.\n• Environmental costs displaced to producing countries; consumption emissions exported.\n• Concentration — a small number of firms and chokepoints create single points of failure.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Benefits are large in aggregate but unevenly distributed, so aggregate calculation misses the political reality.\n• Interdependence is not optional — the relevant question is how it is governed and how resilience is built, not whether to have it.\n• Type matters: interdependence in knowledge and disease control is almost unambiguously beneficial; dependence on a single supplier for a critical good is not.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment distinguishing types of interdependence and addressing distribution, with evidence and a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): benefits and costs with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists advantages and disadvantages of globalisation.` },

      { q: 'Assess the relative importance of the challenges facing the world in the 21st century.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — the candidates:\n• Climate change — global in scope, effectively irreversible on human timescales, and a threat multiplier that worsens water, food, health and migration challenges simultaneously.\n• Inequality — affects billions directly, drives migration and instability, and determines vulnerability to every other challenge.\n• Resource security — water, food and energy; interlinked through the nexus.\n• Population and demographic change — ageing in some regions, rapid growth in others.\n• Biodiversity loss — arguably the least reversible, since extinction is permanent.\n• Conflict, pandemic risk, and technological disruption including AI.\n\nIndicative content — criteria for comparison:\n• Scale — how many are affected and over what area.\n• Severity and reversibility — climate and biodiversity loss score highest; poverty is severe but demonstrably reversible.\n• Immediacy — inequality and food insecurity harm people now; some climate impacts are prospective.\n• Tractability — poverty and disease have known, affordable solutions; climate requires global coordination.\n• Causal position — whether a challenge drives others or is driven by them.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Climate change scores highest on scale, irreversibility and causal position, and so has the strongest claim.\n• Inequality has the strongest claim on immediacy and on determining who suffers from everything else, so the two are not independent.\n• Ranking may be the wrong exercise: the challenges are interlinked, and addressing inequality improves capacity to address climate, while climate action fails politically without attention to equity.\n• A strong answer establishes criteria, commits to a position, and explains why the interconnection matters rather than using it to avoid judgement.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): explicit criteria, accurate evidence, genuine comparison, and a committed supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): describes several challenges with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists challenges with little assessment of relative importance.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that responsibility for addressing global challenges lies primarily with high-income countries.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Historic responsibility — high-income countries account for the large majority of cumulative emissions; the atmosphere’s capacity was largely used by those already developed.\n• Capacity — they have the wealth, technology and institutions to act, and the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities reflects this.\n• Consumption — emissions and resource use embodied in imported goods are attributable to consumers, so territorial accounting understates their share.\n• Benefit — they gained most from the processes causing the problems, including colonial extraction.\n• Commitments already made — the $100 billion climate finance pledge and the loss and damage fund acknowledge this.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Current emissions are increasingly from middle-income countries; China is now the largest annual emitter, so action confined to high-income countries cannot solve the problem arithmetically.\n• Sovereignty — external direction of policy is resented and often counterproductive.\n• Many challenges have local causes and require local action: governance, corruption, conflict and domestic inequality are not attributable to external actors.\n• Middle-income countries now have substantial capacity, so a binary high/low income division is outdated.\n• Assigning responsibility elsewhere can remove agency and reproduce a paternalistic development model.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Distinguish moral responsibility (strongest for historic emitters), causal responsibility (increasingly shared), and practical capacity (concentrated but spreading).\n• Different challenges give different answers: climate responsibility falls most clearly on high-income countries; governance and domestic inequality do not.\n• The defensible conclusion assigns leadership and finance to high-income countries while rejecting the idea that responsibility is exclusive — and notes that framing it as exclusively theirs is itself an obstacle to action.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): distinguishes types of responsibility, uses accurate evidence, differentiates between challenges, and reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that rich countries caused the problems.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the challenges of the 21st century can only be solved through global cooperation. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• The major challenges are transboundary by nature: emissions, pandemics, ocean health, financial contagion and migration all cross borders, so no state can solve them alone.\n• Free riding — unilateral action imposes costs on the actor while benefits are shared, so cooperation is needed to make action rational.\n• Carbon leakage — unilateral restriction relocates emitting production rather than reducing it.\n• Demonstrated successes: the Montreal Protocol effectively resolved ozone depletion through universal binding commitments with trade sanctions; the eradication of smallpox required coordinated global effort.\n• Scale of finance and technology transfer required exceeds any single state’s capacity.\n• Interdependence means unilateral solutions are undermined by others’ inaction.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• National and sub-national action has delivered much of what progress exists: the UK Climate Change Act, EU ETS, Chinese renewable deployment, US state policy, and city-level networks.\n• Technology cost reduction — driven by national industrial policy and private investment — has arguably reduced emissions more than any treaty, by changing the economics.\n• Market and corporate action: investor pressure, insurance pricing, and supply chain requirements operate independently of intergovernmental agreement.\n• Global processes are slow, consensus-bound and produce lowest-common-denominator outcomes; Paris commitments remain collectively insufficient.\n• Some challenges are genuinely domestic — inequality, governance, education — and global frameworks add little.\n• Adaptation is inherently local, so a substantial part of the climate response does not require cooperation at all.\n• "Global cooperation" can become an excuse for inaction while waiting for others.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Distinguish problem types: pure global commons problems (climate mitigation, ocean, ozone) genuinely require cooperation; adaptation, inequality and governance do not.\n• Cooperation works best where it aligns with self-interest, is verifiable, and has few actors — which explains Montreal’s success and Paris’s difficulty.\n• The relationship is complementary rather than exclusive: global frameworks set direction, legitimacy and finance; national and local action delivers, and unilateral leadership can shift what becomes possible collectively.\n• A strong answer commits to a position — most defensibly that cooperation is necessary for some challenges and neither necessary nor sufficient for others — rather than concluding that everything matters.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning engaging directly with "only"; wide range of accurate evidence including named agreements and outcomes; distinguishes problem types; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation of both sides with accurate support and a clear judgement, less fully resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes cooperation and national action with limited evaluation; examples generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): isolated assertions about countries needing to work together.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the success of attempts to reduce global inequality. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — the attempts:\n• The MDGs (2000–2015) and SDGs (2015–2030) setting targets and mobilising attention.\n• Official development assistance, with the 0.7% of GNI target agreed in 1970 and met by few donors.\n• Debt relief — HIPC and MDRI cancelling over $100 billion of debt for the poorest countries.\n• Trade measures — Everything But Arms, AGOA, preferential access schemes.\n• Global health initiatives — Gavi, the Global Fund, PEPFAR.\n• Microfinance, cash transfer programmes, and the OECD global minimum corporate tax.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Extreme poverty fell from around 36% of world population in 1990 to 9% in 2019 — over a billion fewer people despite population growth.\n• Global under-five mortality more than halved since 1990; life expectancy rose in every region.\n• Primary school enrolment approached universality in most regions; gender gaps in schooling narrowed sharply.\n• Between-country inequality fell as Asian economies converged.\n• Debt relief measurably increased health and education spending in beneficiary countries.\n• Global health initiatives have saved tens of millions of lives at low cost per life.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Attribution — most poverty reduction occurred in China through domestic growth, not through international programmes, so the success may not be the policies’.\n• Within-country inequality has widened in most countries, so convergence between states masks divergence inside them.\n• Poverty is increasingly concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa and in fragile and conflict-affected states, where these approaches have worked least.\n• The 0.7% aid target has been met by only a handful of donors, and several have recently cut aid.\n• Tied aid, conditionality and donor priorities reduce effectiveness; aid is now far smaller than remittances.\n• Debt distress has returned, with many low-income countries spending more on servicing than on health.\n• COVID-19 reversed progress, pushing tens of millions back into extreme poverty.\n• Structural causes — trade rules, tax avoidance, historic extraction — remain largely unaddressed, so interventions treat symptoms.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — absolute poverty, relative inequality, human development, sustainability of gains, and structural change.\n• Strong success on absolute poverty and human development; weak on inequality and on structural reform.\n• Which inequality is meant determines the answer: between countries it narrowed, within countries it widened, and between the very richest and everyone else it widened sharply.\n• Timescale matters: judged from 1990 the record is remarkable; judged from 2019 progress has stalled and partly reversed.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate quantified evidence, engages with attribution and with which inequality is meant, and reaches a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate evidence and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes initiatives with some evaluative comment; evidence generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of aid or poverty with little evaluation.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.1': {
    name: 'Tectonic Hazards',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain the difference between a hazard, a disaster and a risk.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A hazard is a natural event with the potential to cause harm to people or property (1)\n• A disaster is the realisation of that potential — an event causing significant damage, disruption or loss of life that exceeds the community’s capacity to cope using its own resources (1)\n• Risk is the probability of harm occurring, usually expressed as hazard × vulnerability ÷ capacity to cope (1)\n• An identical hazard therefore produces a disaster in one place and not another, so disasters are social as much as physical events (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the structure of the Earth’s interior.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The crust is the thin outer layer — oceanic crust around 5–10 km thick and denser (basaltic), continental crust 30–70 km and less dense (granitic) (1)\n• The mantle extends to about 2,900 km; the rigid uppermost mantle and crust together form the lithosphere, which sits on the semi-molten, ductile asthenosphere (1)\n• The outer core is liquid iron and nickel, and its convection generates the Earth’s magnetic field (1)\n• The inner core is solid iron and nickel, kept solid by immense pressure despite temperatures of around 5,000°C (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the mechanisms thought to drive plate movement.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Convection currents in the mantle, driven by heat from radioactive decay and the core, rise beneath ridges and drag or move the plates above (1)\n• Ridge push — newly formed lithosphere at a ridge is elevated and hot; as it cools and thickens it slides down the gradient away from the ridge under gravity (1)\n• Slab pull — cold, dense subducting lithosphere sinks into the mantle, pulling the rest of the plate behind it (1)\n• Slab pull is now considered the dominant force, since plates with long subducting margins move fastest; the mechanisms operate together rather than in competition (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the evidence that supports the theory of plate tectonics.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Jigsaw fit of continental margins, particularly South America and Africa, noted by Wegener (1)\n• Matching fossil distributions — Mesosaurus in Brazil and southern Africa, Glossopteris across southern continents — species that could not have crossed an ocean (1)\n• Geological continuity — matching rock type, age and mountain chains across ocean basins, as between the Appalachians and the Scottish Highlands (1)\n• Palaeomagnetic striping — symmetrical bands of alternating magnetic polarity either side of mid-ocean ridges, recording seafloor spreading; also accept the distribution of earthquakes and volcanoes along plate margins (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what happens at a constructive (divergent) plate margin and the landforms produced.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Two plates move apart as magma rises beneath, and the gap is filled by new basaltic material (1)\n• Shallow-focus, low-magnitude earthquakes occur as the crust fractures under tension (1)\n• Effusive, basaltic volcanism produces low-viscosity lava and shield volcanoes, with fissure eruptions (1)\n• Landforms: mid-ocean ridges with a central rift valley, transform faults offsetting ridge segments, and volcanic islands such as Iceland; on land, rift valleys as in East Africa (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what happens where oceanic and continental plates converge.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The denser oceanic plate subducts beneath the less dense continental plate at a destructive margin (1)\n• Friction and pressure along the Benioff zone produce earthquakes at increasing depth away from the trench, including deep-focus events (1)\n• Water released from the subducting slab lowers the melting point of the mantle above, generating andesitic magma that is viscous and gas-rich (1)\n• Result: an ocean trench, explosive composite volcanoes and fold mountains — the Andes above the Nazca–South American margin (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what occurs at a conservative (transform) plate margin.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Two plates slide past one another horizontally, either in opposite directions or in the same direction at different rates (1)\n• No crust is created or destroyed, so there is no volcanic activity (1)\n• Friction locks the plates until accumulated strain is released suddenly, producing shallow-focus earthquakes that can be very destructive — the San Andreas Fault is the standard example (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what a hot spot is and how it produces a chain of volcanic islands.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A hot spot is a localised plume of unusually hot mantle material rising beneath the lithosphere, independent of plate boundaries (1)\n• It melts the overlying plate, producing basaltic volcanism at the surface (1)\n• The plume is stationary while the plate moves over it, so each volcano is carried away from the heat source and becomes extinct (1)\n• A chain results, with age increasing away from the current active volcano — the Hawaiian–Emperor chain, where Kilauea is active and older islands to the north-west are progressively eroded (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between the focus and the epicentre of an earthquake, and between magnitude and intensity.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The focus (hypocentre) is the point within the Earth where the rupture and energy release originate (1)\n• The epicentre is the point on the surface directly above the focus, where shaking is usually strongest (1)\n• Magnitude measures the energy released, on the moment magnitude scale; it is a single value for the event and is logarithmic (1)\n• Intensity measures the observed effects on people and structures at a particular place, on the Modified Mercalli scale, so it varies across the affected area and depends on depth, geology and building quality (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain why shallow-focus earthquakes are generally more destructive than deep-focus earthquakes of similar magnitude.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Seismic waves lose energy through attenuation as they travel through rock (1)\n• A shallow focus means the waves travel a shorter distance to the surface, so more energy arrives (1)\n• Ground shaking at the surface is therefore more intense and more concentrated near the epicentre — which is why a magnitude 6 shallow event can cause far more damage than a magnitude 7 at 300 km depth (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by liquefaction and the conditions in which it occurs.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Liquefaction occurs when saturated, loosely packed sediment loses its strength during seismic shaking (1)\n• Shaking increases pore water pressure between grains, so the grains lose contact and the material behaves as a fluid (1)\n• It requires loose, fine-grained, water-saturated sediment and a high water table — common on reclaimed land, river deltas and coastal plains (1)\n• Consequence: buildings tilt or sink even without structural failure, and underground tanks and pipes float upward; Christchurch in 2011 and Niigata in 1964 are standard examples (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain how a tsunami is generated by a submarine earthquake.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Sudden vertical displacement of the sea bed at a subduction zone displaces the entire water column above it (1)\n• The displaced water radiates outward as waves of very long wavelength but small amplitude, travelling at speeds up to 800 km/h in deep water, where they may be imperceptible to ships (1)\n• As the wave enters shallow water it slows and its energy is compressed into a shorter wavelength, so amplitude increases sharply — shoaling (1)\n• Water may first withdraw from the shore before the wave arrives, and inundation can extend kilometres inland; funnel-shaped bays and gentle offshore gradients amplify run-up (1)` },

      { q: 'An earthquake of magnitude 7.2 occurs. Given that each whole number on the moment magnitude scale represents approximately 32 times more energy, calculate how much more energy is released than by a magnitude 5.2 event.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Difference in magnitude = 7.2 − 5.2 = 2 whole units (1)\n• Each unit represents approximately 32 times the energy (1)\n• Energy ratio = 32 × 32 (1)\n• = 1,024, so approximately 1,000 times more energy (1)\n• Amplitude, by contrast, is only 10² = 100 times greater — the distinction between amplitude and energy on a logarithmic scale is frequently confused in reporting (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and the amplitude/energy distinction.` },

      { q: 'A tsunami travels at 720 km/h across 3,600 km of ocean. Calculate the travel time, and state the implication for warning systems.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Time = distance ÷ speed = 3,600 ÷ 720 (1)\n• = 5 hours (1)\n• This allows sufficient time for detection, modelling, warning dissemination and evacuation of distant coasts (1)\n• However, coasts close to the epicentre may have only minutes, so warning systems cannot protect the nearest and usually worst-affected communities (1)\n• The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami reached Sri Lanka roughly two hours after the rupture, time enough to have saved many lives had a warning system existed — which is why one was subsequently installed (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and a developed implication.` },

      { q: 'Two earthquakes of magnitude 7.0 occur: one kills 220,000 in a country with GNI per capita of $1,150, the other kills 63 in a country with GNI per capita of $48,000. Calculate the ratio of deaths and analyse the reasons for the difference.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Ratio = 220,000 : 63 (1)\n• = approximately 3,492 : 1 (1)\n• The hazard magnitude is identical, so the difference lies entirely in vulnerability and capacity to cope (1)\n• Lower-income country: poor building quality with no enforced seismic codes, high population density, weak emergency services, limited healthcare capacity, and no effective preparedness or drills (1)\n• Higher-income country: enforced building regulations and retrofitting, rapid and well-equipped emergency response, public education and drills, and insurance enabling recovery — confirming that disasters are produced by vulnerability rather than by hazards alone (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires the ratio and a developed analysis of vulnerability.` },

      { q: 'A volcano last erupted in 1707 and has an average recurrence interval of 150 years. Calculate the years elapsed since the last eruption to 2025 and comment on what this does and does not tell you.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Elapsed = 2025 − 1707 = 318 years (1)\n• This is 318 ÷ 150 = 2.12 times the average recurrence interval (1)\n• It suggests the volcano is substantially overdue on the average (1)\n• However, eruptions are not periodic: recurrence intervals are averages of highly irregular data, so "overdue" has no predictive force (1)\n• A mean derived from few events is also statistically weak, and magma supply is not a clock — monitoring of seismicity, ground deformation and gas emission gives far better warning than recurrence statistics (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires the calculation and a properly sceptical interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Disaster deaths in a region were: 1980s 41,000; 1990s 28,000; 2000s 310,000; 2010s 76,000. Calculate the mean and median per decade and explain which better represents the typical decade.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mean = (41,000 + 28,000 + 310,000 + 76,000) ÷ 4 = 455,000 ÷ 4 = 113,750 (1)\n• Ordered: 28,000, 41,000, 76,000, 310,000 (1)\n• Median = (41,000 + 76,000) ÷ 2 = 58,500 (1)\n• The 2000s figure is a massive outlier, almost certainly dominated by a single catastrophic event, and it pulls the mean to roughly twice the median (1)\n• The median better represents a typical decade, but the mean is the more relevant figure for long-term planning precisely because such events do occur — low-frequency high-magnitude events dominate the total (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both statistics and a reasoned discussion of which to use and why.` },

      { q: 'Economic losses from a disaster were $210 billion, equal to 3.6% of national GDP. Calculate GDP, and compare with a disaster costing $2.1 billion but 26% of a smaller country’s GDP.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• GDP = 210 ÷ 0.036 (1)\n• = $5,833 billion, approximately $5.83 trillion (1)\n• The second country’s GDP = 2.1 ÷ 0.26 = $8.1 billion (1)\n• The absolute loss in the first case is 100 times greater, but the relative loss in the second is over seven times greater (1)\n• Absolute losses are concentrated in wealthy countries because there is more of value to destroy, but relative losses devastate small and poorer economies — so absolute cost rankings systematically understate the impact on the countries least able to recover (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and the absolute/relative interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the factors that determine the severity of the impacts of an earthquake.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical factors:\n• Magnitude and duration of shaking; focal depth, with shallow events far more damaging.\n• Distance from the epicentre and the direction of rupture propagation.\n• Geology — soft sediment amplifies shaking, and saturated sediment may liquefy; bedrock sites suffer far less.\n• Secondary hazards — tsunami, landslides, fire following ruptured gas mains, dam failure.\n• Time of day, affecting where people are and therefore which buildings matter.\n\nIndicative content — human factors:\n• Building design and, crucially, enforcement of codes; unreinforced masonry is the principal killer.\n• Population density and degree of urbanisation.\n• Level of development — emergency services, medical capacity, communications, and money for reconstruction.\n• Preparedness — drills, public education, early warning, and insurance penetration.\n• Governance — whether codes are enforced rather than merely written, and corruption in construction.\n• Aftershocks hampering rescue and causing secondary collapse.\n• The essential point: risk is hazard × vulnerability ÷ capacity, so human factors typically explain more of the variation in deaths than magnitude does.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): physical and human factors covered accurately with examples and the risk relationship explicit.\n• Band 2 (3–5): factors identified with partial explanation, or physical factors only.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that bigger earthquakes cause more damage.` },

      { q: 'Explain the primary and secondary hazards associated with volcanic eruptions.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — primary hazards:\n• Lava flows — destructive to property but rarely fatal, since flow rates usually allow evacuation.\n• Pyroclastic flows — superheated gas and ash travelling at up to 700 km/h at temperatures over 500°C; almost invariably fatal and the principal cause of death in explosive eruptions.\n• Tephra and ash fall — roof collapse under ash load, respiratory harm, contamination of water and crops.\n• Volcanic gases — SO₂, CO₂ and HF; CO₂ can pool in hollows, as at Lake Nyos.\n\nIndicative content — secondary hazards:\n• Lahars — mudflows of ash mixed with water from rainfall or melted ice, travelling far beyond the volcano; Nevado del Ruiz in 1985 killed over 23,000 in Armero.\n• Jökulhlaups — glacial outburst floods from subglacial eruptions in Iceland.\n• Landslides and sector collapse; tsunami from flank collapse or caldera formation, as at Krakatoa.\n• Climatic effects — stratospheric sulphate aerosols causing cooling; Pinatubo in 1991 lowered global mean temperature by around 0.5°C.\n• Economic disruption — Eyjafjallajökull in 2010 closed European airspace for six days with no fatalities, showing that disruption need not correlate with physical destruction.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): both categories covered accurately with named examples and correct terminology.\n• Band 2 (3–5): hazards listed with the primary/secondary distinction partly maintained.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that volcanoes produce lava and ash.` },

      { q: 'Explain why volcanic eruptions at different plate margins have contrasting characteristics.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — constructive margins and hot spots:\n• Magma is basaltic — low in silica, low viscosity, low gas content.\n• Gas escapes readily, so eruptions are effusive rather than explosive.\n• Lava flows far before solidifying, producing shield volcanoes with gentle slopes and fissure eruptions; Hawaii and Iceland.\n• Eruptions are frequent, relatively predictable and rarely fatal, though property loss can be high.\n\nIndicative content — destructive margins:\n• Subducting oceanic crust carries water and sediment; water lowers the mantle melting point and the resulting magma assimilates continental crust.\n• Magma is andesitic or rhyolitic — high silica, high viscosity, high gas content.\n• Viscous magma traps gas until pressure is released catastrophically, producing violently explosive eruptions.\n• Composite (strato-) volcanoes with steep sides built of alternating ash and lava; pyroclastic flows, lahars and calderas; Mount St Helens, Pinatubo, Merapi.\n• Eruptions are infrequent, less predictable and far more lethal.\n\nIndicative content — the controlling variable:\n• Silica content determines viscosity, viscosity determines whether gas escapes gradually or explosively, and that determines eruption style, volcano shape and hazard profile — a single causal chain explaining the contrast.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): contrast explained through magma chemistry with accurate terminology and examples for each setting.\n• Band 2 (3–5): differences described with partial explanation of cause.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that some volcanoes are more explosive than others.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the impacts of tectonic hazards differ between countries at different levels of development.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — mortality:\n• Deaths are overwhelmingly concentrated in lower-income countries: Haiti 2010 (M7.0) killed around 220,000; comparable magnitude events in Japan, New Zealand or California kill in the tens or hundreds.\n• Reasons: unenforced or absent building codes, unreinforced masonry construction, dense informal settlement on unstable ground, limited search and rescue and medical capacity.\n\nIndicative content — economic losses:\n• Absolute losses are greatest in wealthy countries because asset values are high — Tōhoku 2011 cost around $235 billion.\n• Relative losses are far greater in poorer countries: a disaster can exceed a quarter of GDP, wiping out years of development progress.\n• Insurance penetration is high in wealthy countries and negligible in poor ones, so recovery is financed very differently.\n\nIndicative content — recovery:\n• Wealthy countries rebuild within years using domestic resources; poorer countries may remain in temporary shelter for a decade and depend on aid.\n• Secondary effects — disease outbreaks following Haiti’s earthquake, and long-term displacement.\n\nIndicative content — qualifications:\n• Development is not the only variable: Japan’s 2011 tsunami exceeded design assumptions, and Cuba achieves very low mortality at modest income through effective civil defence — so governance and preparedness can substitute for wealth.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): differences explained across mortality, economic loss and recovery with contrasting examples and a qualification.\n• Band 2 (3–5): differences identified with limited exemplification.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that poor countries suffer more.` },

      { q: 'Explain why people continue to live in areas at risk from tectonic hazards.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — positive attractions:\n• Fertile volcanic soils — weathered ash and lava are rich in minerals, supporting dense agricultural populations on Java and around Etna.\n• Geothermal energy and hot springs — Iceland heats most buildings this way.\n• Mineral resources associated with volcanic activity; building materials.\n• Tourism income from volcanic landscapes.\n• Plate margins often coincide with coastal plains and harbours that have economic value for other reasons.\n\nIndicative content — constraints and perception:\n• Inertia — established cities, infrastructure and investment cannot simply be relocated; Tokyo and Los Angeles exist where they do for historical reasons.\n• Poverty removes choice: the poorest occupy hazardous land precisely because it is cheap or unclaimed.\n• Family, community and cultural ties, and in some cases religious significance attached to the volcano.\n• Risk perception — low-frequency events are discounted; long intervals since the last eruption breed complacency, and people systematically underestimate low-probability high-consequence risks.\n• Confidence in protection — building codes, monitoring and warning systems lead residents to judge the risk manageable, which is often rational.\n• Lack of alternative — no capital to move, and nowhere to go.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): covers both attractions and constraints with examples and addresses risk perception explicitly.\n• Band 2 (3–5): reasons identified with limited development, usually only fertile soils.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that people have always lived there.` },

      { q: 'Explain how tectonic hazards can be monitored and predicted.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — volcanoes:\n• Seismometers detecting swarms of small earthquakes as magma fractures rock on its way up.\n• Tiltmeters, GPS and satellite InSAR measuring ground deformation as the edifice inflates.\n• Gas spectrometry — rising SO₂ and CO₂ emissions indicating magma nearing the surface.\n• Thermal imaging and changes in hydrothermal activity.\n• Hazard mapping of past flow and lahar paths to define evacuation zones.\n• Volcanic prediction is genuinely achievable: the successful evacuation of around 60,000 people before Pinatubo in 1991 saved thousands of lives.\n\nIndicative content — earthquakes:\n• Seismic networks and GPS measuring strain accumulation across locked faults.\n• Seismic gap analysis identifying segments that have not ruptured recently.\n• Probabilistic forecasting — statements of likelihood over decades — rather than prediction of time, place and magnitude.\n• Short-term precursors such as radon emission and foreshocks are inconsistent and unreliable, so accurate short-term earthquake prediction remains impossible.\n• Early warning systems detect the fast P-wave and issue alerts seconds before the damaging S-wave arrives — enough to halt trains and shut gas lines; Japan and Mexico operate such systems.\n\nIndicative content — tsunami:\n• Seabed pressure sensors (DART buoys) and tide gauges feeding regional warning centres.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): techniques explained for more than one hazard with examples and the crucial distinction between volcanic prediction and earthquake forecasting.\n• Band 2 (3–5): techniques listed with limited explanation of what they detect.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that scientists monitor volcanoes.` },

      { q: 'Explain the strategies used to reduce the impacts of tectonic hazards.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — prediction and warning:\n• Volcanic monitoring and alert levels enabling timed evacuation; tsunami warning networks; earthquake early warning giving seconds of notice.\n\nIndicative content — protection:\n• Aseismic design — cross-bracing, base isolation, damping systems, flexible frames; retrofitting of existing stock.\n• Enforcement of codes, which matters more than their existence.\n• Lava diversion barriers and spraying, used with partial success at Heimaey and Etna; lahar channels and sabo dams.\n• Tsunami sea walls, breakwaters and vertical evacuation towers; coastal forest belts.\n\nIndicative content — preparedness:\n• Education and regular drills — Japan’s annual Disaster Prevention Day; emergency kits.\n• Evacuation planning and hazard mapping; exclusion zones around volcanoes.\n• Emergency services training, stockpiles and rehearsal.\n• Insurance and government reserve funds to finance recovery.\n\nIndicative content — land use and recovery:\n• Zoning to keep critical infrastructure off hazardous ground; relocation of settlements in extreme cases.\n• Building back better — using reconstruction to raise standards rather than restore vulnerability.\n• Key point: prediction is unreliable for earthquakes, so protection and preparedness deliver most of the risk reduction, and enforcement rather than technology is usually the binding constraint.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): strategies across several categories with examples and judgement about which are most effective.\n• Band 2 (3–5): strategies listed with limited explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that buildings should be made stronger.` },

      { q: 'Explain the Park model of disaster response and what it shows.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the model:\n• The Park (disaster response) curve plots quality of life, or level of economic activity, against time before, during and after a hazard event.\n• Stage 1 — pre-disaster: a stable baseline, with the level determined by development and preparedness.\n• Stage 2 — disruption: a steep fall over hours or days as the event strikes; the depth of the trough reflects vulnerability.\n• Stage 3 — relief: immediate search, rescue and medical care, typically over hours to days, often involving external aid.\n• Stage 4 — rehabilitation: temporary shelter, restoration of services and utilities, over weeks to months; the curve begins to rise.\n• Stage 5 — reconstruction: rebuilding to at least the former state, over months to years.\n\nIndicative content — what it shows:\n• The shape varies with development and preparedness: wealthier, better-prepared places have a shallower trough and faster recovery.\n• Recovery may end below the original quality of life (a poorer country that cannot fully rebuild) or above it, where reconstruction improves on what existed — "build back better".\n• It allows comparison of events and of the effectiveness of response.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Generalised and qualitative; quality of life is hard to measure on a single axis.\n• Assumes a single discrete event, so it handles aftershocks or prolonged eruptions poorly.\n• Averages across a population, concealing that recovery is highly unequal — the poorest recover slowest.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): stages described accurately with what the model reveals and substantive limitations.\n• Band 2 (3–5): stages described with limited evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): names the model with little detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and impacts of one major tectonic event you have studied.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named event. Tōhoku 2011 is used below; credit Haiti 2010, Nepal 2015, Eyjafjallajökull 2010 or Pinatubo 1991 at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — causes:\n• Magnitude 9.0–9.1 megathrust earthquake on 11 March 2011, off the Pacific coast of Honshu.\n• The Pacific Plate subducting beneath the North American (Okhotsk) Plate at a destructive margin; around 500 km of fault ruptured, with up to 50 m of slip.\n• Focus at approximately 29 km depth — shallow, and offshore, so vertical seabed displacement generated a major tsunami.\n\nIndicative content — impacts:\n• Around 18,500 dead or missing, the overwhelming majority drowned by the tsunami rather than killed by shaking — evidence that Japan’s aseismic building standards worked.\n• Tsunami run-up reached almost 40 m in places and travelled up to 10 km inland; sea walls designed for smaller events were overtopped.\n• Around 470,000 people displaced; 120,000 buildings destroyed.\n• Fukushima Daiichi nuclear accident when the tsunami disabled backup generators and cooling failed, causing meltdown and a 20 km exclusion zone; a secondary hazard with consequences longer-lasting than the primary event.\n• Economic cost around $235 billion, the costliest natural disaster on record.\n• Global supply chain disruption in electronics and vehicle manufacturing.\n• Long-term: national nuclear shutdown and increased fossil imports; some coastal areas never resettled; the coastline subsided by up to a metre.\n\nIndicative content — analysis:\n• Preparedness prevented a far higher death toll, but design assumptions were based on historical events smaller than what occurred — a failure of hazard assessment rather than of engineering.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): accurate tectonic explanation plus detailed impacts with figures, distinguishing primary and secondary.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound account with some specific detail; causes or impacts thinner.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generic account with little specific detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain the factors affecting the vulnerability of a population to tectonic hazards.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — physical exposure:\n• Proximity to plate margins and to specific hazard paths — lahar channels, tsunami inundation zones.\n• Local geology, particularly soft sediment amplification and liquefaction potential.\n• Relief and drainage affecting landslide susceptibility.\n\nIndicative content — built environment:\n• Building materials and design; unreinforced masonry and poorly built concrete are the principal causes of earthquake death.\n• Whether codes exist, and whether they are enforced — corruption in construction is a documented factor in high mortality.\n• Population density and urbanisation; settlement on hazardous or informal land.\n• Infrastructure resilience — hospitals, bridges, water, power.\n\nIndicative content — socio-economic:\n• Poverty limits housing quality, the ability to evacuate, and recovery capacity; insurance access.\n• Age, disability and health status affecting ability to evacuate; the elderly were disproportionately among Tōhoku’s dead.\n• Education and hazard awareness; language barriers for migrant populations.\n\nIndicative content — institutional:\n• Governance quality, emergency service capacity, and the existence and rehearsal of evacuation plans.\n• Monitoring and warning systems; communications reliability.\n• Prior experience and cultural memory, which raises compliance with warnings.\n• Synthesis: vulnerability is socially produced — the same hazard meets very different populations, which is why mortality correlates far better with development and governance than with magnitude.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several categories of factor explained with examples and the conclusion that vulnerability is socially produced.\n• Band 2 (4–6): factors identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that poor countries are more vulnerable.` },

      { q: 'Explain how hazard management strategies vary between countries at different levels of development.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — higher-income countries:\n• Capital-intensive protection — base isolation, retrofitting, sea walls, sabo dams.\n• Dense monitoring networks and early warning systems; Japan’s earthquake early warning halts trains automatically.\n• Enforced building codes and land use zoning.\n• Institutionalised preparedness — drills, public education, stockpiles; well-resourced emergency services.\n• Insurance and government reserve funds financing rapid reconstruction.\n\nIndicative content — lower-income countries:\n• Limited capital for engineering solutions; codes may exist but go unenforced.\n• Reliance on community-based disaster risk reduction — local volunteer networks, simple warning systems, community mapping.\n• Appropriate technology — low-cost aseismic construction techniques using local materials.\n• Dependence on international aid and NGOs for response and reconstruction.\n• Competing priorities: hazard mitigation competes with immediate needs in health, education and housing, so a low-probability future risk is rationally deprioritised.\n\nIndicative content — qualifications:\n• Wealth is not the only determinant — Cuba achieves very low disaster mortality at modest income through effective civil defence and evacuation, showing organisation can substitute for capital.\n• Preparedness and education are cheap relative to engineering, so the most cost-effective measures are available to all; the constraint is often political priority rather than money.\n• Wealthy countries can also fail, as Fukushima showed, when design assumptions are wrong.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): contrasts approaches with specific examples and qualifies the wealth-determines-outcome assumption.\n• Band 2 (4–6): differences described with limited exemplification.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that rich countries can afford better protection.` },

      { q: 'Explain the concept of the hazard management cycle and the disaster risk equation.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the cycle:\n• A continuous four-stage framework rather than a linear sequence, emphasising that management is ongoing.\n• Mitigation/prevention — long-term measures reducing the impact of future events: building codes, land use zoning, defences, education.\n• Preparedness — planning for an event: warning systems, evacuation plans, drills, stockpiles, training.\n• Response — immediate action during and after: search and rescue, medical care, emergency shelter, restoring essential services.\n• Recovery — rehabilitation and reconstruction, and the opportunity to build back better so the next event causes less harm, which closes the loop into mitigation.\n\nIndicative content — the risk equation:\n• Risk = (hazard × vulnerability) ÷ capacity to cope.\n• Hazard is the physical event, and is largely beyond human control — nothing reduces the magnitude of an earthquake.\n• Vulnerability and capacity are socially determined and can be changed, which is where all effective management acts.\n• This explains why identical hazards produce radically different outcomes, and why disasters are correctly described as socially produced.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The cycle usefully directs attention to mitigation and preparedness, which attract less funding and political attention than visible response.\n• Limitation: it implies orderly progression, whereas recovery in poorer countries may never complete before the next event, and stages overlap.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): both concepts explained accurately with the analytical implication drawn and some evaluation.\n• Band 2 (4–6): stages described with limited use of the risk relationship.\n• Band 1 (1–3): lists stages with no explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the global distribution of tectonic hazards and the reasons for it.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the distribution:\n• Earthquakes and volcanoes are concentrated in narrow linear belts along plate margins rather than distributed evenly.\n• The Pacific Ring of Fire accounts for around 75% of active volcanoes and 90% of earthquakes, following subduction zones around the Pacific.\n• The Alpine–Himalayan belt from the Mediterranean through Iran to the Himalayas, at collision margins.\n• Mid-ocean ridges — the mid-Atlantic ridge — with frequent but low-magnitude activity, mostly submarine and therefore rarely hazardous.\n• Isolated intraplate activity at hot spots — Hawaii, Yellowstone — and rare intraplate earthquakes on ancient faults, such as New Madrid.\n\nIndicative content — reasons:\n• Destructive margins: subduction generates deep and shallow earthquakes along the Benioff zone, and water-fluxed melting generates explosive volcanism.\n• Constructive margins: tensional fracturing gives shallow low-magnitude earthquakes, and decompression melting gives effusive basaltic volcanism.\n• Conservative margins: friction between plates sliding past one another gives shallow, potentially destructive earthquakes but no volcanism.\n• Collision margins: no subduction of continental crust, so earthquakes without volcanoes — the Himalayas.\n• Hot spots: mantle plumes independent of margins.\n\nIndicative content — hazard versus risk:\n• The distribution of disasters differs from the distribution of hazards, because it also depends on where people and vulnerability are concentrated — submarine ridge activity is frequent but harms no one.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): distribution described accurately with margin-type explanation and the hazard/risk distinction.\n• Band 2 (4–6): distribution described with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that earthquakes happen at plate boundaries.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a seismic gap and how the concept is used.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A seismic gap is a segment of an active fault that has not ruptured for an unusually long period compared with adjacent segments (1)\n• Strain is assumed to be accumulating there, so the segment is considered more likely to rupture than one that has released strain recently (1)\n• It is used for probabilistic long-term forecasting and to prioritise monitoring and retrofitting, but it gives no indication of timing, and some major earthquakes have occurred outside identified gaps (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a collision margin and a destructive margin.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• At a destructive margin, denser oceanic crust subducts beneath continental or other oceanic crust, producing a trench, deep-focus earthquakes and explosive volcanism (1) \n• At a collision margin, two plates of continental crust converge; both are too buoyant to subduct, so neither descends far into the mantle (1)\n• Crust is instead crumpled and thickened into fold mountains, producing powerful shallow-focus earthquakes but no volcanism — the Himalayas along the Indian–Eurasian margin (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the effects of volcanic eruptions on the global atmosphere and climate.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — mechanisms:\n• Large explosive eruptions inject sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere, where it converts to sulphate aerosols.\n• These aerosols reflect incoming solar radiation, reducing the energy reaching the surface — the dominant climatic effect.\n• Ash falls out within days to weeks and has little climatic significance; stratospheric aerosols persist for one to three years because there is no rain to remove them.\n• CO₂ is also released, but volcanic emissions are around two orders of magnitude smaller than anthropogenic ones, so the net effect of an eruption is cooling, not warming.\n\nIndicative content — evidence and consequences:\n• Pinatubo (1991) injected around 20 Mt of SO₂ and lowered global mean temperature by approximately 0.5°C for about two years.\n• Tambora (1815) produced the "year without a summer" in 1816, with crop failure and famine across Europe and North America.\n• Regional effects on monsoon strength and precipitation patterns.\n• Aviation disruption from ash, which melts in jet engines and solidifies on turbine blades — Eyjafjallajökull in 2010 closed European airspace for six days.\n• Acid deposition downwind; localised air quality and respiratory effects near the vent.\n• Only sufficiently explosive eruptions reaching the stratosphere have global effect, so effusive eruptions such as Hawaii’s do not, however voluminous.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): mechanism explained accurately with the stratospheric condition and quantified examples.\n• Band 2 (3–5): identifies cooling with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that ash blocks the sun.` },

      { q: 'Assess the extent to which the impacts of tectonic hazards are determined by human rather than physical factors.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — human factors:\n• The comparison of Haiti 2010 (M7.0, ~220,000 deaths) with Christchurch 2011 (M6.3, 185 deaths) shows that at similar magnitude, mortality varied by three orders of magnitude — the difference lies entirely in vulnerability.\n• Building quality and code enforcement is the single strongest determinant of earthquake mortality.\n• Preparedness, warning, evacuation and emergency capacity determine survival after the event.\n• Poverty determines who lives on hazardous ground and who can recover.\n• Governance and corruption in construction; Turkey 2023 raised precisely this.\n\nIndicative content — physical factors:\n• Magnitude, focal depth and duration set the upper limit on what any preparation can withstand — Tōhoku’s tsunami overtopped well-engineered defences.\n• Geology — soft sediment amplification and liquefaction explain damage patterns within a single city, independent of wealth.\n• Secondary hazards such as tsunami and lahar can be lethal regardless of preparation; Nevado del Ruiz killed 23,000 despite warnings having been issued.\n• Timing and location of the event relative to population centres involve genuine chance.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Physical factors determine the potential for harm; human factors determine how much of that potential is realised — so they operate sequentially rather than competing.\n• Human factors explain most of the variance in deaths between events of similar magnitude, which is the strongest evidence for their primacy.\n• Physical factors dominate at the extremes: an M9 megathrust or a VEI 6 eruption overwhelms any preparation.\n• Scale of impact considered matters — mortality is largely human-determined, whereas economic loss correlates strongly with the physical size of the event.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with paired contrasting examples, explicit criteria, and a judgement recognising the sequential relationship.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both covered with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists factors with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of prediction and warning in reducing the impacts of tectonic hazards.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — effective:\n• Volcanic eruptions can genuinely be forecast from seismicity, deformation and gas emission; the Pinatubo evacuation in 1991 moved around 60,000 people and saved thousands of lives.\n• Tsunami warning systems provide hours of notice for distant coasts; the Pacific system has repeatedly enabled evacuation, and the Indian Ocean system was built after 2004 precisely because the absence of warning cost so many lives.\n• Earthquake early warning gives seconds to tens of seconds — enough to stop trains, halt surgery, close gas valves and take cover; Japan and Mexico operate such systems successfully.\n• Hazard mapping and probabilistic forecasting inform zoning and building codes over the long term, which is where most lives are saved.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Short-term earthquake prediction remains impossible; precursors are inconsistent and no method reliably gives time, place and magnitude.\n• Warnings only work if acted on: Nevado del Ruiz in 1985 killed over 23,000 in Armero despite hazard maps and warnings having been issued, because of institutional failure to evacuate.\n• False alarms erode compliance and carry economic cost, creating pressure not to warn.\n• Coasts near a tsunami source have minutes at most, so the nearest and worst-affected receive least benefit.\n• Warning without the means to evacuate — transport, shelter, somewhere to go — achieves little, so it is inseparable from preparedness.\n• Monitoring is unevenly distributed: many volcanoes in poorer countries are unmonitored.\n\nJudgement: highly effective for volcanoes and distant tsunami, marginal but real for earthquakes; effectiveness depends less on the science than on the institutional capacity to act on it, so warning is necessary but not sufficient and protection and preparedness deliver more.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): differentiates between hazard types, uses named examples of success and failure, and reaches a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): strengths and limitations with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes monitoring with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that megadisasters are becoming more frequent.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Recorded disaster numbers and economic losses have risen substantially over recent decades.\n• Population growth and urbanisation concentrate people and assets in hazardous locations — more megacities sit on plate margins than ever before.\n• Increasing asset values mean any given event costs more; Tōhoku at $235 billion would have cost far less in 1950.\n• Interconnection amplifies consequences: Fukushima and global supply chain disruption after Tōhoku; Eyjafjallajökull closed European airspace though it killed no one.\n• Climate-related disasters are genuinely increasing in frequency, raising the overall total.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Tectonic hazard frequency is not increasing — plate motion is constant, so the physical driver is unchanged.\n• Reporting and recording have improved enormously; apparent increases partly reflect better data and media coverage rather than more events.\n• Deaths per event and per capita have fallen markedly, reflecting improved warning, building standards and response — so by the measure that matters most, outcomes have improved.\n• Rising economic losses partly reflect inflation and asset accumulation, so real trends require normalisation.\n• Definitional issue — "megadisaster" has no agreed threshold, so the claim is not straightforwardly testable.\n\nJudgement: tectonic hazards are not more frequent, but disasters arising from them are more consequential because exposure and interconnection have grown — while mortality has fallen. A defensible conclusion distinguishes hazard frequency from disaster impact, and economic loss from loss of life, noting that the claim is true on one measure and false on another.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): distinguishes measures and addresses the reporting bias, with evidence and a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): asserts disasters are increasing with little support.` },

      { q: 'Assess the importance of governance in determining the impacts of tectonic hazards.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — governance as decisive:\n• Building codes only save lives if enforced; the difference between Chile 2010 (M8.8, ~500 deaths) and Haiti 2010 (M7.0, ~220,000) reflects enforcement more than wealth alone.\n• Corruption in construction converts codes into paper requirements; this was central to criticism after Turkey 2023.\n• Land use planning determines whether people live in inundation zones and on liquefiable ground.\n• Emergency planning, evacuation authority and rehearsal determine response quality; Armero died because a warning was not acted upon, an institutional rather than scientific failure.\n• Cuba’s low disaster mortality at modest income demonstrates that organisation can substitute for capital.\n\nIndicative content — limits to governance:\n• Wealth sets what is affordable — base isolation and dense monitoring networks require capital that good governance alone cannot conjure.\n• Physical magnitude can overwhelm any preparation; Tōhoku exceeded design assumptions in a well-governed state.\n• Competing priorities are real: a government facing immediate health and education needs may rationally deprioritise a low-probability risk.\n• Geology and exposure are not choices for established cities.\n• Scientific uncertainty limits what even good governance can plan for.\n\nJudgement: governance is the principal variable explaining differences in outcome between similar events, because the measures that save most lives — enforcement, planning, education, evacuation — are organisational rather than expensive. Wealth sets the ceiling; governance determines how close a country gets to it.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment using paired comparisons, weighing governance against wealth and physical magnitude, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): identifies the role of governance with some evaluation.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that governments should prepare better.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the impacts of tectonic hazards can be effectively managed. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Building design works: Japan’s aseismic construction meant almost all Tōhoku deaths came from the tsunami rather than from shaking, in a magnitude 9 event.\n• Volcanic monitoring and evacuation work: Pinatubo 1991 evacuated around 60,000 people before a VEI 6 eruption, saving thousands.\n• Warning systems: tsunami networks give hours of notice to distant coasts; earthquake early warning halts trains and industrial processes in seconds.\n• Preparedness and education demonstrably raise survival — Japan’s drills, Cuba’s civil defence.\n• Deaths per event have fallen substantially over the last century despite far greater exposure, which is the strongest single piece of evidence.\n• Land use zoning and hazard mapping reduce exposure where applied.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• Earthquakes cannot be predicted in the short term, so preparation must be permanent and generalised, which is expensive and politically difficult to sustain.\n• Extreme events exceed design assumptions: Tōhoku overtopped sea walls, and Fukushima’s backup generators were sited where a tsunami could reach them.\n• Management requires resources that the most vulnerable lack; Haiti had neither codes nor enforcement nor emergency capacity.\n• Governance failure can nullify good science, as at Armero in 1985.\n• Rapid urbanisation is adding exposure faster than protection is being built, particularly in Asian megacities.\n• Some hazards — pyroclastic flows, near-field tsunami — permit no effective response beyond prior evacuation.\n• Economic losses continue to rise even where mortality falls, so "effective" depends on the measure.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Distinguish mortality from economic loss: management has been markedly successful at reducing deaths and much less so at reducing cost.\n• Distinguish hazard types: volcanoes are manageable through forecasting and evacuation; earthquakes only through long-term protection; near-field tsunami barely at all.\n• Distinguish contexts: effective management is demonstrably achievable, but it is achieved unevenly, so the limiting factor is political and economic rather than technical.\n• A strong answer commits to a position — most defensibly that impacts can be managed effectively where capacity and governance exist, which makes the failures a distributional problem rather than evidence that management does not work.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning; wide range of accurate located evidence with figures; distinguishes hazard types, measures of impact and contexts; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation of both sides with accurate support and a clear judgement, less fully resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes management strategies with limited evaluation; examples generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of hazards with little evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the response to one tectonic disaster you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named disaster. Haiti 2010 is used below; credit Tōhoku 2011, Nepal 2015 or Pinatubo 1991 at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — the event:\n• Magnitude 7.0 earthquake, 12 January 2010, focus 13 km deep and 25 km from Port-au-Prince on the Enriquillo–Plantain Garden fault, a conservative margin.\n• Around 220,000 deaths, 300,000 injured, 1.5 million displaced; approximately 250,000 residences and 30,000 commercial buildings destroyed.\n• Pre-existing conditions: the poorest country in the Americas, no enforced building codes, dense informal settlement, weak state capacity, and the collapse of government buildings including the Presidential Palace and UN headquarters, which destroyed the coordinating capacity needed to respond.\n\nIndicative content — the response:\n• Immediate: search and rescue teams from many countries; the single runway airport became a bottleneck and the port was damaged, severely constraining aid delivery.\n• Over $13 billion pledged internationally; large NGO presence, with thousands of organisations operating.\n• Emergency shelter in camps; medical care largely provided by foreign organisations.\n\nIndicative content — limitations and criticisms:\n• Coordination failure — so many agencies operating without a functioning government counterpart that duplication and gaps were widespread; Haiti was described as a "republic of NGOs".\n• Very little funding passed through Haitian institutions or businesses, so the response did not build local capacity and arguably weakened it further.\n• A cholera outbreak introduced by UN peacekeepers killed around 10,000 people — a catastrophic secondary consequence of the response itself.\n• Reconstruction was extremely slow; hundreds of thousands remained in temporary camps years later, and much pledged aid was never disbursed.\n• Rebuilding largely reproduced pre-existing vulnerability rather than building back better, so the 2021 earthquake again caused heavy casualties.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — lives saved in the immediate phase, speed of recovery, capacity built, and reduction of future vulnerability.\n• The emergency phase saved lives but was hampered by logistics; the recovery phase largely failed against every criterion.\n• The deeper point: the response could not compensate for the absence of pre-event mitigation, so the decisive failures preceded the earthquake rather than following it.\n• Contrast with Chile 2010, a far larger magnitude event the same year with around 500 deaths, isolates preparedness and enforcement as the variable.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate place-specific evidence including figures, engages with why the response failed and with the pre-event context, and reaches a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate case detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the event and response with some evaluative comment; detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of a disaster with little specific detail.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.2': {
    name: 'Ecosystems',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Define the terms ecosystem, biome and habitat.', marks: 3, command: 'Define', markScheme: `• An ecosystem is a community of living organisms interacting with each other and with the non-living components of their environment, functioning as a system of energy and nutrient flows (1)\n• A biome is a global-scale ecosystem type defined principally by climate and characteristic vegetation — tropical rainforest, savanna, tundra (1)\n• A habitat is the specific place in which a particular organism lives, providing the conditions it requires (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between biotic and abiotic components of an ecosystem, giving examples.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Biotic components are the living elements — producers, consumers and decomposers, including plants, animals, fungi and bacteria (1)\n• Abiotic components are the non-living physical and chemical elements — climate, light, soil, water, nutrients, relief and geology (1)\n• The two interact continuously: abiotic conditions determine which organisms can survive, while organisms modify those conditions, as when vegetation builds soil and alters the microclimate (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the flow of energy through an ecosystem and why the number of trophic levels is limited.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Energy enters as solar radiation and is fixed by producers through photosynthesis, forming the base of the food chain (1)\n• It passes to primary consumers (herbivores), then to secondary and tertiary consumers (carnivores) (1)\n• Only around 10% of energy is transferred between successive trophic levels; the remainder is lost as heat through respiration, in movement, and in material not eaten or not digested (1)\n• Because energy declines so sharply at each step, there is insufficient to support more than four or five trophic levels, which is why top predators are always few in number (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between gross and net primary productivity.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Gross primary productivity is the total amount of energy fixed by producers through photosynthesis in a given area and time (1)\n• Net primary productivity is GPP minus the energy producers use in their own respiration (1)\n• NPP is the energy actually available to consumers and accumulating as biomass, so it is the measure that determines how much life an ecosystem can support; it is highest in tropical rainforest and lowest in desert and tundra (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what the Gersmehl model shows about nutrient cycling.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The Gersmehl model represents nutrient cycling using three stores — biomass, litter and soil — drawn as circles proportional in size to the nutrients each holds (1)\n• Transfers between them are shown as arrows whose width is proportional to the rate of flow: fallout from biomass to litter, decay from litter to soil, and uptake from soil to biomass (1)\n• Inputs come from precipitation to the soil and from weathering of parent rock; outputs are runoff and leaching from the soil (1)\n• The relative sizes reveal how an ecosystem functions: rainforest holds most nutrients in biomass with rapid transfers, whereas taiga holds most in litter because cold slows decomposition — which explains why clearing rainforest removes most of the nutrient capital (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by succession and the difference between primary and secondary succession.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Succession is the progressive change in the species composition of a community over time at a given site (1)\n• Primary succession begins on a surface that has never supported vegetation and has no soil — bare rock, a lava flow, a new sand dune (1)\n• Secondary succession occurs where vegetation has been removed but soil and a seed bank remain, as after fire, felling or abandonment of farmland (1)\n• Secondary succession is much faster because the soil, nutrients and propagules that primary succession must create from nothing are already present (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the stages of a lithosere.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Pioneer stage — bare rock colonised by lichens and algae, which tolerate extreme conditions and begin chemical weathering, releasing minerals (1)\n• Mosses colonise the thin accumulating debris, trapping more material and adding organic matter as they die (1)\n• A rudimentary soil forms, allowing herbs and grasses with shallow roots, then shrubs with deeper roots that weather rock further (1)\n• Climax woodland is reached as trees shade out earlier species; through the sere, soil depth, organic matter, moisture retention and species diversity all increase (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a climatic climax community and by a plagioclimax.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A climatic climax community is the stable, self-perpetuating end point of succession, determined principally by the regional climate (1)\n• It is in dynamic equilibrium, with species composition remaining broadly constant unless conditions change (1)\n• A plagioclimax is a community whose succession has been arrested or deflected by human activity, so it does not reach the climatic climax (1)\n• Examples: British heather moorland maintained by burning and grazing, and grassland maintained by mowing; remove the management and succession resumes toward woodland (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by an arresting factor in succession, giving two examples.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• An arresting factor is any influence that halts or deflects succession before the climatic climax is reached (1)\n• Grazing — sheep and deer remove tree seedlings, preventing regeneration and maintaining grassland or moorland (1)\n• Burning, mowing, trampling, drainage, or a natural factor such as exposure, waterlogging or repeated fire; the resulting community is a subclimax or plagioclimax (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain why tropical rainforest has high biodiversity.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Consistently high temperatures and rainfall all year permit continuous growth, giving very high net primary productivity and therefore more energy to support more organisms (1)\n• Stratification into emergent, canopy, understorey, shrub and ground layers creates many distinct niches within a small area (1)\n• Long-term climatic stability — the biome has persisted through glacial cycles — has allowed millions of years of uninterrupted evolution and specialisation (1)\n• Intense competition promotes narrow niche specialisation and co-evolution, so species occupy very specific roles; rainforest covers around 6% of the land surface but holds over half of terrestrial species (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain why the soils of tropical rainforests are often infertile despite the luxuriant vegetation.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Almost all the nutrient capital is held in the biomass rather than in the soil (1)\n• Decomposition is extremely rapid in the hot, humid conditions, and nutrients released are taken up immediately by dense shallow root mats and mycorrhizal fungi (1)\n• Heavy rainfall causes intense leaching, removing soluble nutrients from the upper soil, and produces oxisols rich in iron and aluminium oxides but poor in bases (1)\n• Consequently, when the forest is cleared and burnt, the ash gives two or three years of fertility before the nutrient store is exhausted — which explains why shifting cultivation is sustainable at low density and permanent agriculture is not (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics of savanna grassland and the role of fire within it.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Savanna has a marked wet and dry season, with 500–1,500 mm of rainfall concentrated in a few months (1)\n• Vegetation is dominated by grasses with scattered drought- and fire-tolerant trees such as acacia and baobab, with adaptations including deep roots, thick bark and water storage (1)\n• Fire occurs regularly in the dry season, killing tree seedlings while grasses regenerate from underground rhizomes and root reserves (1)\n• Fire is therefore a maintaining factor rather than a disturbance: without regular burning and grazing much savanna would succeed to woodland, so it is best understood as a fire-maintained plagioclimax (1)` },

      { q: 'An area of rainforest covers 5.4 million hectares and is cleared at 1.8% per year. Calculate the area lost annually and the area remaining after 10 years at that rate.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Annual loss in year 1 = 0.018 × 5,400,000 = 97,200 hectares (1)\n• After 10 years at a compound rate, remaining = 5,400,000 × (0.982)^10 (1)\n• (0.982)^10 = 0.834 (1)\n• Remaining = 5,400,000 × 0.834 = 4,503,600 hectares, so about 4.5 million (1)\n• A simple linear calculation would give 5,400,000 − 972,000 = 4,428,000, understating the remainder by around 76,000 ha, because a constant percentage applies to a shrinking area — credit recognition of the compound/linear distinction (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires the compound calculation and the distinction made explicit.` },

      { q: 'Net primary productivity is 2,200 g/m²/yr in rainforest and 90 g/m²/yr in desert. Calculate the ratio and the annual production of a 1 km² area of each in tonnes.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Ratio = 2,200 ÷ 90 = 24.4 : 1 (1)\n• 1 km² = 1,000,000 m² (1)\n• Rainforest: 2,200 × 1,000,000 = 2,200,000,000 g = 2,200 tonnes (1)\n• Desert: 90 × 1,000,000 = 90,000,000 g = 90 tonnes (1)\n• The difference is driven by water availability and temperature, the two limiting factors on photosynthesis; NPP determines how much biomass an ecosystem can support and therefore its capacity to store carbon (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct unit conversion and an explanation of the difference.` },

      { q: 'A food chain has 12,000 kJ/m²/yr fixed by producers. Assuming 10% transfer efficiency, calculate the energy available at the third trophic level and the total lost between levels one and three.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Level 2 (primary consumers) = 10% of 12,000 = 1,200 kJ/m²/yr (1)\n• Level 3 (secondary consumers) = 10% of 1,200 (1)\n• = 120 kJ/m²/yr (1)\n• Total lost = 12,000 − 120 = 11,880 kJ/m²/yr, or 99% of the original (1)\n• This is why food chains are short, why top predators require very large territories, and why eating at a lower trophic level feeds far more people from the same land area (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires correct calculation and a developed implication.` },

      { q: 'A quadrat survey of 20 quadrats records heather in 14, bilberry in 6 and grass in 18. Calculate the percentage frequency of each and state one limitation of frequency as a measure.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Heather = 14 ÷ 20 × 100 = 70% (1)\n• Bilberry = 6 ÷ 20 × 100 = 30% (1)\n• Grass = 18 ÷ 20 × 100 = 90% (1)\n• Limitation: frequency records only presence or absence, so a species present as a single plant counts the same as one covering the whole quadrat (1)\n• Percentage cover or density would distinguish these; frequency also depends on quadrat size, so results from different surveys are not directly comparable (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires all three calculations and a developed limitation.` },

      { q: 'Soil pH along a psammosere reads: embryo dune 8.4; yellow dune 7.6; grey dune 6.5; dune slack 6.1; woodland 5.3. Analyse the trend and explain its cause.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• pH falls consistently from 8.4 to 5.3, a decrease of 3.1 units across the sere (1)\n• Since pH is logarithmic, this represents roughly a thousand-fold increase in hydrogen ion concentration (1)\n• The high initial pH reflects calcium carbonate from shell fragments in the fresh sand (1)\n• Leaching by rainfall progressively removes the carbonate, while accumulating organic matter releases humic acids as it decomposes (1)\n• The falling pH is both a consequence of succession and a driver of it, since changing soil chemistry favours different species — an example of vegetation modifying its own habitat (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires the trend quantified, the logarithmic point, and a causal explanation.` },

      { q: 'Mangrove cover fell from 18.8 million hectares in 1980 to 14.8 million in 2020. Each hectare stores around 1,000 tonnes of carbon. Calculate the percentage loss and the carbon potentially released.', marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Loss = 18.8 − 14.8 = 4.0 million hectares (1)\n• Percentage loss = 4.0 ÷ 18.8 × 100 = 21.3% (1)\n• Carbon = 4,000,000 × 1,000 tonnes (1)\n• = 4,000,000,000 tonnes, or 4 gigatonnes of carbon (1)\n• Roughly a fifth of global mangrove cover lost in forty years; the carbon figure assumes complete release, which overstates the flux since some remains in sediment — but mangrove soils are among the most carbon-dense on Earth, so the loss of "blue carbon" is disproportionate to the area (1)\n\nBanded, 5 marks AO3 (bands 4–5 / 2–3 / 1). Band 3 requires both calculations and a qualified interpretation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics and adaptations of vegetation in the tropical rainforest biome.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — conditions:\n• Constant high temperatures around 26–28°C, rainfall over 2,000 mm distributed through the year, high humidity and intense competition for light.\n\nIndicative content — structure and adaptations:\n• Stratification into emergent, canopy, understorey, shrub and ground layers, partitioning light.\n• Buttress roots supporting tall trees in thin soils where deep anchorage is impossible.\n• Drip tips and waxy cuticles shedding water rapidly, preventing fungal and algal growth on leaves.\n• Lianas and epiphytes — climbing or growing on other plants to reach light without investing in a trunk; bromeliads collecting water in leaf rosettes.\n• Shallow, wide root mats with mycorrhizal associations capturing nutrients immediately on release.\n• Evergreen habit with continuous growth and leaf fall, since there is no dormant season.\n• Ground-layer species adapted to very low light; many understorey plants have large, dark leaves.\n• Specialised pollination and seed dispersal relationships with animals, reflecting the scattered distribution of individual species.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2; 0 = not creditworthy).\n• Band 3 (6–8): several adaptations explained in terms of the conditions they respond to, with accurate terminology.\n• Band 2 (3–5): adaptations listed with partial explanation of purpose.\n• Band 1 (1–2): describes the forest as hot, wet and green.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes of tropical deforestation.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — direct causes:\n• Commercial agriculture is the largest driver — cattle ranching and soya in Amazonia, oil palm in Southeast Asia.\n• Subsistence and smallholder clearance, including shifting cultivation intensified beyond sustainable rotation.\n• Logging, both legal and illegal; selective logging opens access roads that enable further clearance.\n• Mining and quarrying, and the infrastructure serving them.\n• Infrastructure — roads such as the Trans-Amazonian Highway, dams and settlement schemes; roads are a strong predictor of subsequent clearance.\n• Fuelwood and charcoal in some regions.\n\nIndicative content — underlying causes:\n• Global demand for beef, soya, palm oil and timber, so consumption in distant countries drives clearance.\n• Debt and the need for export earnings; foreign investment in extractive sectors.\n• Insecure land tenure, so clearance is a means of establishing a claim.\n• Weak governance, corruption and limited enforcement capacity over vast remote areas.\n• Poverty and population pressure limiting alternatives; policy incentives such as subsidies and tax breaks for conversion.\n• Political choices — enforcement in Brazil weakened sharply between 2019 and 2022 and deforestation rose, then fell again with renewed enforcement, showing the strength of the policy variable.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): distinguishes direct from underlying causes with examples and recognises the role of external demand and governance.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several causes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that trees are cut down for farming and wood.` },

      { q: 'Explain the impacts of deforestation on ecosystems and on people.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — ecological:\n• Biodiversity loss and species extinction, particularly for endemic species with restricted ranges; habitat fragmentation isolates populations and reduces genetic diversity.\n• Carbon release — deforestation contributes around 10–15% of global emissions, and removes future sequestration capacity.\n• Soil erosion once root binding and canopy protection are removed; rapid nutrient loss through leaching since most nutrients were held in biomass.\n• Hydrological change — reduced interception and transpiration, flashier hydrographs, increased flood risk downstream, and reduced recycled precipitation, which can dry the wider region.\n• Microclimate change — hotter, drier conditions at the forest edge, making remaining forest more fire-prone.\n\nIndicative content — human:\n• Displacement of indigenous peoples and loss of livelihoods, knowledge and culture; violent conflict over land in Amazonia.\n• Loss of forest products — food, medicine, materials — relied on by forest-dependent communities.\n• Short-term economic gain from timber, cattle and crops, and employment, which is why clearance occurs.\n• Health effects from smoke during burning seasons.\n• Global costs from emissions and lost potential pharmaceutical resources.\n• Key point: the benefits are short-term, local and concentrated while the costs are long-term, global and diffuse — which is precisely why the problem persists.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): ecological and human impacts both covered with examples and the distribution of costs and benefits recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): impacts listed with limited development or one dimension only.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that animals lose their homes.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of desertification.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Climatic — reduced and increasingly variable rainfall, prolonged drought, and rising temperatures increasing evapotranspiration; Sahel rainfall declined markedly from the 1960s.\n• Overgrazing — livestock numbers exceeding carrying capacity remove vegetation cover and compact soil.\n• Overcultivation — shortened fallow periods exhausting nutrients as population pressure rises.\n• Deforestation for fuelwood removing the root systems binding soil.\n• Poor irrigation practice causing salinisation; borehole provision concentrating livestock and degrading the surrounding area.\n• Underlying drivers: population growth, poverty forcing short-term exploitation, insecure tenure, and conflict displacing people onto marginal land.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Loss of soil fertility and structure; wind and water erosion removing topsoil, with a positive feedback as bare soil raises albedo and reduces local rainfall.\n• Falling crop yields and livestock productivity, causing food insecurity and malnutrition.\n• Migration and displacement, usually internal, and competition between pastoralists and cultivators that has contributed to conflict.\n• Biodiversity loss and dust storms affecting health, sometimes over long distances.\n• Around 12 million hectares are estimated to be lost annually; the UNCCD estimates a large share of the world’s drylands are affected.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): distinguishes climatic and human causes, covers consequences with examples and identifies a feedback.\n• Band 2 (3–5): causes or consequences covered with the other thin.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that land turns into desert.` },

      { q: 'Explain the ecosystem services that ecosystems provide.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the four categories:\n• Provisioning — the tangible goods obtained: food, fresh water, timber, fibre, fuel, genetic resources and medicines; a substantial proportion of pharmaceuticals derive from natural compounds.\n• Regulating — climate regulation through carbon storage; flood and erosion control by forests, wetlands and mangroves; water purification; pollination, on which around three-quarters of food crops partly depend; disease and pest regulation.\n• Supporting — the underlying processes enabling the others: soil formation, nutrient cycling, primary production, habitat provision.\n• Cultural — recreation, tourism, aesthetic and spiritual value, education and sense of place.\n\nIndicative content — significance:\n• Attempts to value these services globally reach figures exceeding world GDP, indicating their scale.\n• Most are unpriced, so they carry no weight in market decisions and are systematically undervalued until lost — mangrove clearance for shrimp farms prices the shrimp but not the coastal protection.\n• Payment for Ecosystem Services schemes attempt to correct this by paying landowners to maintain services such as watershed protection.\n• Valuation is contested: assigning monetary value to cultural and existence value is methodologically and ethically problematic.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): all four categories with examples, plus the point about pricing and its limitations.\n• Band 2 (3–5): several services identified across some categories.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that ecosystems give us food and oxygen.` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics and adaptations of vegetation in a tundra ecosystem.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — conditions:\n• Mean temperatures below 10°C even in the warmest month, a growing season of only 50–60 days, low precipitation (under 250 mm, much as snow), permafrost beneath a shallow active layer, strong winds and low-angle sunlight.\n\nIndicative content — adaptations:\n• Low, cushion and mat-forming growth avoiding wind chill and abrasion, and exploiting warmer air near the ground.\n• Shallow, spreading root systems because permafrost prevents deep rooting, and waterlogging in the active layer limits oxygen.\n• Perennial habit with slow growth and long lifespans; plants store nutrients between short growing seasons rather than starting afresh.\n• Small, waxy or hairy leaves reducing transpiration, since water is frozen and unavailable for much of the year — physiological drought.\n• Dark pigmentation absorbing heat; some species track the sun to warm their reproductive parts.\n• Rapid flowering and seed set to complete reproduction within the short season; many reproduce vegetatively instead.\n• Mosses and lichens dominate where conditions are most severe.\n\nIndicative content — system properties:\n• Very low NPP and biodiversity, simple food webs and slow decomposition, so nutrients are held in litter and soil rather than biomass — which makes the system extremely slow to recover from disturbance.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): adaptations explained in terms of specific conditions, with the system’s fragility recognised.\n• Band 2 (3–5): adaptations listed with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that plants are small because it is cold.` },

      { q: 'Explain how ecosystems can be managed sustainably.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — protection:\n• Protected areas — national parks, biosphere reserves, marine protected areas — with zoning to separate strict conservation from buffer and transition zones.\n• International designations: World Heritage sites, Ramsar wetlands, CITES restricting trade in endangered species.\n\nIndicative content — sustainable use:\n• Selective logging and reduced-impact logging rather than clear-felling; replanting and rotation.\n• Agroforestry combining trees with crops, maintaining cover while producing food.\n• Ecotourism generating income that depends on the ecosystem remaining intact, so conservation becomes economically rational.\n• Sustainable harvesting of non-timber forest products — rubber, nuts, medicinal plants.\n• Certification schemes such as FSC using consumer demand to reward good practice.\n\nIndicative content — governance and finance:\n• Community-based management and recognition of indigenous land rights; indigenous-managed lands consistently show lower deforestation than comparable unprotected areas.\n• Payment for Ecosystem Services and REDD+ compensating countries for avoided deforestation.\n• Monitoring by satellite enabling enforcement over vast areas.\n• Restoration — reforestation, rewilding, peatland and mangrove restoration.\n\nIndicative content — conditions for success:\n• Local people must gain more from conservation than from conversion, or enforcement alone fails; secure tenure and alternative livelihoods are therefore central.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): strategies across protection, use and governance, with examples and the conditions for success.\n• Band 2 (3–5): strategies listed with limited explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that areas should be protected.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of ecosystems in the carbon cycle.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Terrestrial ecosystems absorb roughly a quarter of anthropogenic CO₂ emissions through photosynthesis, making them a major sink.\n• Carbon is stored in living biomass above and below ground, in litter, and in soil organic matter; soils hold around three times as much as vegetation.\n• Rates and stores vary by biome: tropical rainforest has the highest productivity and holds most carbon in biomass; peatlands and permafrost hold enormous stores in soil because cold or waterlogged conditions inhibit decomposition.\n• Coastal "blue carbon" ecosystems — mangroves, salt marsh, seagrass — store carbon at rates per hectare far exceeding terrestrial forest, and bury it in sediment for centuries.\n• Ecosystems become sources when degraded: clearance, burning, peat drainage and permafrost thaw release stored carbon rapidly.\n• Feedbacks: warming increases respiration and decomposition relative to photosynthesis, and drought and fire can convert a forest sink into a source — parts of Amazonia have been measured as net sources in recent years.\n• Management implication: protecting intact ecosystems prevents emissions immediately, whereas restoration delivers sequestration only over decades, so the two are not interchangeable.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): treats ecosystems as both store and flux, differentiates biomes, and develops the source/sink switch and a management implication.\n• Band 2 (3–5): explains carbon absorption with limited differentiation.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that plants absorb carbon dioxide.` },

      { q: 'Explain the causes and consequences of biodiversity loss.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Habitat loss and fragmentation — the largest single driver, principally agricultural expansion.\n• Overexploitation — hunting, fishing and the wildlife trade.\n• Invasive species outcompeting or preying on natives, particularly damaging on islands.\n• Pollution — nutrients, pesticides, plastics and light; neonicotinoids implicated in pollinator decline.\n• Climate change shifting ranges faster than species can move or adapt, and decoupling seasonal relationships.\n• Underlying drivers: population growth, consumption, and the failure of markets to price ecosystem services.\n\nIndicative content — scale:\n• The Living Planet Index records a roughly 70% decline in monitored vertebrate populations since 1970; extinction rates are estimated at tens to hundreds of times the background rate.\n\nIndicative content — consequences:\n• Reduced ecosystem resilience — diverse systems recover better from disturbance, so loss increases vulnerability to further shocks.\n• Loss of ecosystem services — pollination, pest control, water purification, with direct economic cost.\n• Food security — narrowing of the genetic base of crops reduces capacity to breed for disease and climate resilience.\n• Loss of potential medicines and of scientific knowledge.\n• Cultural and existence value; disproportionate impact on indigenous and rural communities dependent on wild resources.\n• Trophic cascades — loss of a keystone species restructures an entire community.\n• Irreversibility — extinction is permanent, which distinguishes biodiversity loss from most other environmental problems.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): several causes and consequences with quantification and the irreversibility point made.\n• Band 2 (4–6): causes or consequences covered with the other thinner.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that animals are becoming extinct.` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics of one ecosystem you have studied and the ways it is threatened.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named ecosystem. Amazonia is used below; credit any appropriate example (Borneo rainforest, the Sahel, the Great Barrier Reef, UK peatland) at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — characteristics:\n• The largest tropical rainforest, covering around 5.5 million km² across nine countries, some 60% in Brazil.\n• Equatorial climate: 26–28°C year-round, over 2,000 mm rainfall, high humidity.\n• Stratified structure; extraordinary biodiversity with an estimated 10% of all known species, including some 16,000 tree species.\n• Nutrients held overwhelmingly in biomass with rapid cycling; infertile leached oxisols.\n• Holds an estimated 150–200 GtC; generates recycled precipitation through transpiration, sustaining rainfall across South America — the "flying rivers".\n\nIndicative content — threats:\n• Cattle ranching, the largest single cause of clearance, and soya cultivation.\n• Illegal logging and gold mining, the latter contaminating rivers with mercury.\n• Road building, notably the Trans-Amazonian Highway, opening previously inaccessible areas.\n• Fire, both for clearance and, increasingly, escaping into drought-stressed forest.\n• Hydroelectric dams flooding large areas.\n• Climate change lengthening dry seasons; parts of the southeastern Amazon have been measured as net carbon sources.\n• Weak enforcement; deforestation rose sharply between 2019 and 2022 before falling again, showing policy sensitivity.\n• Threats to indigenous peoples, whose territories nonetheless show markedly lower deforestation rates.\n• Tipping point concern — estimates suggest that beyond roughly 20–25% clearance, reduced transpiration could shift large areas irreversibly to savanna.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): accurate place-specific characteristics and threats with figures and named processes.\n• Band 2 (4–6): sound outline with some specific detail.\n• Band 1 (1–3): generic account of rainforest with little specific detail.` },

      { q: 'Explain how human activity has altered ecosystems at a global scale.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — land conversion:\n• Around half of habitable land is now used for agriculture; most temperate grassland and deciduous forest has been converted.\n• Urbanisation and infrastructure fragmenting remaining habitat.\n\nIndicative content — alteration of cycles:\n• Carbon — deforestation and peat drainage converting sinks into sources.\n• Nitrogen and phosphorus — industrial fertiliser has more than doubled reactive nitrogen in circulation, causing eutrophication and marine dead zones.\n• Water — damming, abstraction and drainage altering flows and drying wetlands.\n\nIndicative content — biotic change:\n• Species introductions dissolving biogeographical barriers, homogenising communities globally.\n• Selective removal of large-bodied species through hunting and fishing, restructuring food webs.\n• Domesticated animals now vastly outweigh wild mammals in total biomass.\n\nIndicative content — climatic and chemical:\n• Warming shifting species ranges and phenology worldwide; ocean acidification.\n• Novel pollutants — plastics, pesticides, persistent organic compounds — present in ecosystems from the deep ocean to the Arctic.\n\nIndicative content — synthesis:\n• The scale and simultaneity of these changes underpin the argument for an Anthropocene epoch.\n• Changes interact: a fragmented, warming, nutrient-enriched ecosystem is far more vulnerable than one facing any single pressure.\n• Counterpoint worth credit: restoration, protected area expansion and rewilding are increasing, and some populations have recovered.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): changes covered across several domains with quantification and recognition of interaction.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several changes identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that humans damage the environment.` },

      { q: 'Explain the value of ecosystems to people, and the difficulties of measuring that value.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the value:\n• Provisioning — food, water, timber, fibre, fuel and medicines; a significant proportion of pharmaceuticals derive from natural compounds.\n• Regulating — carbon storage, flood and erosion control, water purification, pollination underpinning around three-quarters of food crops.\n• Supporting — soil formation, nutrient cycling, primary production.\n• Cultural — recreation, tourism, spiritual and aesthetic value, identity and sense of place.\n• Direct economic contribution: tourism, fisheries and forestry support hundreds of millions of livelihoods.\n\nIndicative content — difficulties of measurement:\n• Most services are unpriced public goods, so markets give no signal of their value until they are lost.\n• Substitution is often impossible — there is no market price for a stable climate or for pollination at global scale.\n• Valuation methods are contested: contingent valuation asks hypothetical willingness to pay; replacement cost assumes a substitute exists; hedonic pricing infers value from property prices — each has serious limitations.\n• Non-linearity and thresholds: value does not decline smoothly, since ecosystems can collapse abruptly past a tipping point.\n• Intergenerational issues — discounting future benefits systematically undervalues long-lived services.\n• Ethical objection: assigning monetary value to species or cultural significance may be category error, and may legitimise trading them away.\n• Consequence: because value is hard to measure it is usually treated as zero in decisions, which is a principal reason ecosystems are degraded — making imperfect valuation arguably better than none.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): value covered across categories and several substantive measurement difficulties including the ethical objection.\n• Band 2 (4–6): value described with limited attention to measurement.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that ecosystems are important to people.` },

      { q: 'Explain the concept of a tipping point and its relevance to ecosystems.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — the concept:\n• A tipping point is a threshold beyond which a system shifts rapidly and often irreversibly to a different state, rather than changing smoothly in proportion to the pressure applied.\n• It arises from positive feedbacks that, once triggered, sustain the change without further external forcing.\n• Hysteresis — reversing the original pressure does not restore the original state, because the new state is itself self-sustaining.\n\nIndicative content — examples:\n• Amazonian dieback — the forest generates much of its own rainfall through transpiration; beyond an estimated 20–25% clearance, reduced recycling could shift large areas irreversibly to savanna.\n• Coral reefs shifting to algal-dominated states after bleaching and loss of herbivorous fish; the algal state resists recolonisation by coral.\n• Permafrost thaw releasing carbon that drives further warming.\n• Eutrophic lakes flipping from clear to turbid, with the turbid state persisting even after nutrient inputs are cut.\n• Fishery collapse — Newfoundland cod has not recovered thirty years after closure, suggesting an alternative stable state.\n\nIndicative content — relevance:\n• It undermines management based on gradual response: monitoring may show little change until the threshold is crossed, so warning is limited.\n• Thresholds are poorly known and identifiable with confidence only in retrospect, so the precautionary principle becomes the rational approach.\n• It shifts the cost-benefit calculation decisively: the potential loss is not proportional to the marginal hectare cleared but potentially catastrophic and permanent.\n• Interaction between pressures can lower thresholds, so a system under multiple stresses tips sooner.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): concept explained accurately including feedback and hysteresis, with several examples and the management implication.\n• Band 2 (4–6): concept understood with limited examples or implications.\n• Band 1 (1–3): states that ecosystems can collapse.` },

      { q: 'Explain the role of different players in ecosystem management.', marks: 10, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• National governments — designate protected areas, set and enforce environmental law, and determine land use and agricultural policy; the strongest lever, and the most politically constrained.\n• Local and regional authorities — planning decisions, management of parks and reserves.\n• Indigenous peoples and local communities — hold or manage a substantial share of the world’s remaining intact ecosystems; indigenous-managed lands show consistently lower deforestation, so secure tenure is itself a conservation measure.\n• NGOs — WWF, Greenpeace, RSPB — campaigning, land purchase, research and monitoring, and holding governments and firms to account.\n• International organisations — the Convention on Biological Diversity and its 30x30 target, CITES, Ramsar, UNESCO biosphere reserves, REDD+ finance.\n• Transnational corporations — agricultural, timber and mining companies as principal drivers of conversion, but also as the point at which supply chain commitments can be applied.\n• Consumers — demand for beef, soya, palm oil and timber; certification schemes attempt to translate consumer preference into producer behaviour.\n• Scientists — monitoring, evidence and identification of thresholds.\n• Financial institutions — lending and investment decisions increasingly incorporating deforestation risk.\n\nIndicative content — analysis:\n• Interests conflict, and power is unequal: those who benefit from conversion are usually better resourced than those bearing the costs.\n• Effective management generally requires alignment across scales — international finance, national enforcement and local incentive — which is why single-scale interventions often fail.\n\nBanded, 10 marks AO1 (bands 7–10 / 4–6 / 1–3).\n• Band 3 (7–10): a range of players with roles, conflicts and examples, and the multi-scale point developed.\n• Band 2 (4–6): several players identified with partial explanation.\n• Band 1 (1–3): lists organisations with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a keystone species and give one example of the effect of its removal.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A keystone species has an effect on its ecosystem disproportionately large relative to its abundance (1)\n• Its removal triggers a trophic cascade in which the structure of the whole community changes (1)\n• Example: removal of sea otters allows sea urchin populations to expand and destroy kelp forests; wolf reintroduction to Yellowstone reduced elk browsing, allowing willow and aspen regeneration and altering river behaviour (1)` },

      { q: 'Explain the characteristics of a hydrosere and the conditions that produce it.', marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content — conditions:\n• A hydrosere is a succession beginning in freshwater — a pond, lake margin or slow-flowing water — where sediment accumulates and water depth decreases over time.\n\nIndicative content — stages:\n• Open water with phytoplankton and submerged aquatic plants; organic and inorganic sediment accumulates on the bed.\n• Floating-leaved plants such as water lilies root in the shallowing sediment, adding further organic matter when they die.\n• Reed swamp — emergent species such as reeds and bulrushes colonise as depth falls below about a metre; their stems trap sediment, accelerating infilling.\n• Marsh/fen — the surface reaches water level; sedges and rushes dominate, and waterlogged anaerobic conditions slow decomposition, so peat may accumulate.\n• Carr — alder and willow colonise as the ground dries, their transpiration lowering the water table further.\n• Climax woodland — oak or other deciduous species once drainage is sufficient.\n\nIndicative content — system properties:\n• Each stage modifies conditions in ways that make the site less suitable for itself and more suitable for the next — autogenic succession.\n• Trends through the sere: increasing soil depth and organic matter, decreasing water content, increasing species diversity to the mid-sere.\n• Arresting factors such as grazing, cutting or maintained water levels can hold the sere at reed swamp or fen — many UK wetlands are managed plagioclimaxes.\n\nBanded, 8 marks AO1 (bands 6–8 / 3–5 / 1–2).\n• Band 3 (6–8): accurate sequence with named species and the autogenic mechanism explained.\n• Band 2 (3–5): sequence described with limited explanation of the mechanism.\n• Band 1 (1–2): states that ponds fill in over time.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of protected areas in conserving ecosystems.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — effective:\n• Protected areas now cover around 17% of land and 8% of ocean, a substantial expansion over recent decades.\n• Well-managed reserves demonstrably reduce habitat loss relative to comparable unprotected land, and species populations recover within them.\n• Marine no-take zones show large increases in biomass, with spillover benefiting adjacent fisheries.\n• They provide a legal basis for enforcement and attract tourism revenue and research funding.\n• Biosphere reserve zoning reconciles conservation with human use rather than excluding it.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• "Paper parks" — designation without funding, staff or enforcement achieves little; a large share of protected areas have inadequate management.\n• Siting bias: reserves are disproportionately located on land of low economic value — high, steep, remote — rather than on the most biodiverse land, so coverage statistics overstate protection.\n• Boundaries do not exclude climate change, pollution or invasive species, so protection is inherently partial.\n• Fragmentation — isolated reserves may be too small to sustain viable populations or to permit range shifts under warming; corridors are frequently absent.\n• Exclusion of local and indigenous people can create hostility and undermine compliance, and raises justice concerns — "fortress conservation".\n• Leakage — protection in one area can displace clearance to another.\n• Only around 3% of ocean is fully protected against an 8% designated figure.\n\nJudgement: effective where fully protected, adequately resourced, well sited and supported by local people; ineffective as designation alone. A defensible conclusion argues that the 30x30 target will succeed or fail on management quality rather than on area, and that protected areas are necessary but insufficient without action on climate and on the drivers of conversion.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with explicit criteria, accurate evidence including figures, and a supported judgement identifying the conditions for effectiveness.\n• Band 2 (5–8): strengths and weaknesses with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): describes protected areas with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that economic development and ecosystem conservation are incompatible.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting incompatibility:\n• Historically, development has proceeded through land conversion and resource extraction; almost all temperate forest and grassland in developed countries was cleared during their industrialisation.\n• Immediate returns from conversion — cattle, soya, palm oil, timber — exceed the unpriced value of conservation, so the economics favour clearance.\n• Poverty forces short-term exploitation; conservation can appear a luxury.\n• Demanding conservation of developing countries while developed countries converted their own ecosystems raises a fairness objection that makes agreement harder.\n\nIndicative content — challenging incompatibility:\n• Ecosystem services underpin the economy — pollination, water supply, soil fertility, fisheries — so degradation undermines the development it was meant to deliver.\n• Ecotourism, sustainable forestry and non-timber products generate income that depends on the ecosystem remaining intact; Costa Rica reversed deforestation while growing its economy, partly through PES and ecotourism.\n• Environmental Kuznets curve — some pressures decline after a certain income level, though the evidence is mixed and may reflect displacement of impacts abroad rather than genuine improvement.\n• REDD+, PES and carbon markets attempt to make conservation financially competitive with conversion.\n• Degraded land restoration can raise agricultural productivity without further clearance, so yield improvement substitutes for area expansion.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• The conflict is real in the short term and at the margin, but not inevitable in the long term — it depends on whether ecosystem value is priced and on who bears the costs.\n• Scale matters: local livelihoods may genuinely require conversion, while national and global interests favour conservation, so the incompatibility is often between groups rather than between the objectives.\n• The defensible conclusion is that they are compatible in principle and frequently incompatible in practice, because the institutions that would reconcile them are weak.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment with contrasting evidence and examples, distinguishing scales and timescales, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that development harms the environment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the relative importance of the threats facing tropical rainforests.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — the threats:\n• Commercial agriculture — cattle ranching and soya in Amazonia, oil palm in Southeast Asia; the largest driver by area, and directly responsive to global commodity demand.\n• Logging — damaging in itself, and significant because access roads enable subsequent clearance, so its impact exceeds the area it directly affects.\n• Mining and infrastructure — smaller in area but with disproportionate effects through access, pollution and settlement.\n• Fire — increasingly escaping into drought-stressed forest, so a management tool becomes an independent threat.\n• Climate change — lengthening dry seasons and raising fire risk; potentially the mechanism of a tipping point.\n• Weak governance and insecure tenure — an enabling condition rather than a direct threat.\n\nIndicative content — criteria:\n• Area affected — agriculture dominates.\n• Reversibility — selective logging allows recovery; conversion to pasture with compacted degraded soil does not.\n• Rate of change — clearance is immediate; climate effects are slower but compounding.\n• Causal position — governance and demand underlie the direct threats, so addressing them has greater leverage than tackling any single activity.\n• Interaction — fragmentation dries forest edges, making fire more likely, which enables further clearance; the threats reinforce one another rather than acting independently.\n\nJudgement: agricultural conversion is most important by area and immediacy, but climate change may be most important by irreversibility and by its potential to trigger dieback across areas never directly cleared. The strongest position notes that governance is the most tractable lever, since Brazilian deforestation rose and fell sharply with enforcement while the underlying demand was unchanged.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): compares threats against explicit criteria with accurate evidence, recognises interaction and identifies leverage, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): threats described with some comparison; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): lists threats with little assessment.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that local communities should have primary responsibility for managing ecosystems.', marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting:\n• Local people have detailed knowledge of the ecosystem accumulated over generations, often exceeding that available to external managers.\n• They bear the costs of conservation, so excluding them is both unjust and practically counterproductive — fortress conservation generates hostility and non-compliance.\n• Indigenous-managed lands show consistently lower deforestation rates than comparable unprotected areas, which is strong empirical evidence.\n• Ostrom demonstrated that user communities sustainably manage common resources where rules are locally devised, monitored and enforced.\n• Local management is cheaper and more responsive than remote enforcement over vast areas.\n\nIndicative content — challenging:\n• Local interests may conflict with wider ones: a community may rationally prefer conversion, and the global value of carbon storage and biodiversity is not theirs to capture.\n• Capacity and resources may be lacking for monitoring, legal defence and technical management.\n• Communities are not homogeneous — elite capture within them can disadvantage the poorest and women.\n• Threats operating at larger scales — climate change, invasive species, international demand, armed incursion by illegal miners — cannot be managed locally at all.\n• Migration and population change may weaken the traditional institutions the argument relies on.\n\nJudgement: local communities should have primary responsibility for day-to-day management and secure rights over land, because this is where knowledge, legitimacy and cost fall; but this requires national legal backing and international finance to compensate for benefits that accrue globally. A defensible conclusion argues for nested governance rather than for any single level holding responsibility alone.\n\nBanded, 12 marks AO2 (bands 9–12 / 5–8 / 1–4).\n• Band 3 (9–12): sustained assessment weighing local knowledge and legitimacy against scale limitations, with evidence, reaching a supported judgement.\n• Band 2 (5–8): both sides with some evaluation; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–4): states that local people know their area best.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that ecosystems are more threatened by climate change than by any other human activity. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content — supporting the view:\n• Climate change is global and pervasive: no ecosystem is outside its reach, including protected areas and remote deep ocean and polar systems.\n• It is largely irreversible on human timescales — acidification requires tens of thousands of years to reverse, and lost species cannot return.\n• It operates faster than many species can migrate or adapt, particularly where habitat fragmentation blocks range shifts.\n• It can trigger tipping points that transform ecosystems never directly exploited — Amazonian dieback, coral reefs shifting to algal states, permafrost thaw.\n• It compounds every other pressure: warmer, drier forest burns more readily; warmer water makes polluted water more hypoxic.\n• Coral reefs are arguably already committed to severe loss on current warming trajectories regardless of local protection.\n\nIndicative content — challenging the view:\n• Habitat loss remains the largest current driver of biodiversity decline, principally through agricultural conversion; around half of habitable land is already farmed.\n• Overexploitation has collapsed fisheries and removed large-bodied species from most ecosystems, independently of climate.\n• Invasive species have transformed island ecosystems irreversibly, with no climatic involvement.\n• Nutrient pollution has created marine dead zones and eutrophied freshwater systems.\n• Direct destruction is immediate and certain, whereas some climate impacts remain prospective and uncertain in magnitude.\n• Local pressures are tractable now: protection, restoration and regulation demonstrably work, whereas climate requires global action no ecosystem manager controls.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Timescale is decisive: habitat loss dominates the record to date, climate change dominates plausible futures.\n• Criterion matters — by current extinction attribution, land use wins; by scale, irreversibility and long-run risk, climate wins.\n• The threats are not independent: fragmentation prevents the range shifts that would allow adaptation to warming, so land use and climate act multiplicatively.\n• Practical implication: reducing local pressures buys resilience against the climate pressure that cannot be managed locally, so treating them as competing priorities is itself a mistake.\n• A strong answer commits to a position while resolving the apparent conflict through timescale or criteria rather than concluding that all threats matter.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5). Indicative content is not prescriptive.\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained, coherent reasoning engaging directly with the comparison; wide range of accurate evidence and named examples; resolves by timescale or criteria and addresses interaction; convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): well-organised evaluation of both sides with accurate support and a clear judgement, less fully resolved.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes threats with limited comparison; examples generalised; judgement asserted.\n• Band 1 (1–5): largely descriptive account of threats to ecosystems with little evaluation.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the success of management of one ecosystem you have studied. (20 marks)', marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `This question requires a specific named ecosystem. Costa Rica’s forests are used below; credit Amazonia, the Great Barrier Reef, UK peatlands or the Serengeti at the same standard.\n\nIndicative content — context and strategy:\n• Costa Rica lost around half its forest cover between 1950 and 1987, with deforestation rates among the highest in the world, driven by cattle ranching and subsidised land clearance.\n• Response from the late 1980s: cattle subsidies removed; a ban on land-use change from forest introduced in 1996.\n• Payment for Ecosystem Services scheme (1997) pays landowners for carbon storage, watershed protection, biodiversity and scenic beauty, funded partly by a fuel tax — making conservation financially competitive with clearance.\n• National park and reserve network expanded to around a quarter of national territory.\n• Ecotourism developed deliberately as an economic pillar.\n\nIndicative content — evidence of success:\n• Forest cover recovered from around 21% in 1987 to over 50% today — one of very few national reversals of deforestation.\n• Achieved alongside economic growth and rising GDP per capita, undermining the assumption that the two conflict.\n• Ecotourism now a major foreign exchange earner, supporting employment that depends on forest remaining intact.\n• Electricity generation is almost entirely renewable; the country has become an international reference case.\n• Biodiversity protected across a wide network, with wildlife corridors linking reserves.\n\nIndicative content — limitations:\n• Secondary regrowth is not equivalent to primary forest in biodiversity or carbon storage, so cover figures overstate ecological recovery.\n• PES payments have tended to favour larger landowners with secure title, and some payments went to land that would not have been cleared anyway — weak additionality.\n• Pressure displaced to other sectors: pineapple and banana plantations expanded, with pesticide and water pollution.\n• Possible leakage — reduced domestic production may have shifted beef and timber demand to neighbouring countries.\n• Costa Rica is small, politically stable, without a military budget, and has strong institutions, so transferability to larger or weaker states is limited.\n• Tourism itself creates pressure through development, water use and emissions from long-haul flights.\n\nIndicative content — evaluation:\n• Judge against explicit criteria — forest cover, ecological quality, economic sustainability, equity of benefit distribution, and transferability.\n• Outstanding on cover and on demonstrating compatibility with growth; more qualified on ecological quality, equity and leakage.\n• Timescale matters: sustained over three decades, which distinguishes it from schemes that reverse when governments change.\n\nBanded, 20 marks AO2 (bands 16–20 / 11–15 / 6–10 / 1–5).\n• Band 4 (16–20): sustained evaluation against explicit criteria with detailed accurate place-specific evidence including figures and dates, genuine treatment of limitations including additionality and leakage, and a convincing supported conclusion.\n• Band 3 (11–15): sound evaluation with accurate detail and a clear judgement; criteria partly implicit.\n• Band 2 (6–10): describes the management with some evaluative comment; detail generalised.\n• Band 1 (1–5): general account of conservation with little locational detail.` },
    ]
  },

};

if (typeof module !== 'undefined') { module.exports = GEOGRAPHY_EDUQAS_ALEVEL_PRACTICE; }
